WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:05.438
I've debated doing this

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:08.241
when I was pinned down by
a sniper in Afghanistan.

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:09.309
But there's no way that

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:11.111
enemy sniper would've hit that, though.

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:12.345
My name's Nicholas Irving.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:15.749
I'm a former US Army Ranger
special-operations sniper.

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:18.351
I'm Command Sgt. Maj.,
retired, Lamont Christian.

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:19.452
I'm Nicholas Moran.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:20.720
My name is Dakata Brodie.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.422
Hey, everyone, Jay Ly here.

00:00:22.422 --> 00:00:23.757
Hi, I'm John Spencer.

00:00:23.757 --> 00:00:24.924
Gareth Timmins here.

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:26.626
I'm a former Royal Marines commando.

00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:29.496
Today I'm back again to look
at some more sniper scenes

00:00:29.496 --> 00:00:30.630
from TV and film, 

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:31.598
rate tank battles,

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:32.432
urban warfare,

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:33.867
desert-warfare scenes,

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:34.934
boot-camp scenes,

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:36.169
helicopter-rescue scenes,

00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:37.504
bomb-disposal scenes

00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:40.907
in movies and TV and
judge how real they are.

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:43.977
Forrest: And always answer
every question with,

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:45.745
"Yes, drill sergeant."

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:47.147
Drill sergeant: Is that clear?!

00:00:47.147 --> 00:00:49.015
Yes, drill sergeant!

00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:52.585
The standard for responding
to a drill sergeant

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:55.221
is, "Yes, drill sergeant,"
"No, drill sergeant,"

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:58.758
and if it has to have
an affirmative response,

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:00.760
then the whole platoon
will sound just like that.

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:05.832
Done, drill sergeant!

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:07.600
Jesus H. Christ!

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.102
This is a new company record.

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:11.805
The drill sergeant was so
impressed because, you know,

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:13.573
in the early part of training,

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:14.874
it's a challenge for somebody

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:18.011
who perhaps has not
handled a weapon before,

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:20.580
what we would call weapons training,

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.783
to be able to take that weapon
apart, put it back together,

00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:25.785
and do what you saw Forrest do there,

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:27.353
which was that functions check.

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:30.390
What your hope is as
a DI or drill sergeant

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.760
is that everybody in there
can do it like Forrest.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.330
Shrimp and potatoes, shrimp burger.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:38.932
What's funny is when
they're in there with them

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:41.301
with toothbrushes, that's
something of the past.

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:43.536
There was times where, you know,

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:46.673
you'd give a recruit scissors
and say, "Go cut the grass."

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:48.208
They're fostering this environment

00:01:48.208 --> 00:01:50.243
of working together as a team,

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:51.911
which you saw with the weapons training,

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:54.247
which is what I call mindless repetition.

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:56.082
Just do it over and over and over again

00:01:56.082 --> 00:01:57.250
until you get it right.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.854
That has the same training
effect that this used to have.

00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:03.923
The approach that the
drill sergeant had in here

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:06.326
was very, very common
for that time period,

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:07.660
during the Vietnam War.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:08.995
So that's about a nine.

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:10.663
Pick it up! Move it! Move it!

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:12.499
I want to see some fire here!

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:18.104
When you look at obstacle courses,

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:20.707
usually in boot camp, you'll
go through them twice.

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:22.642
Obstacle courses are really designed

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:25.378
to find your upper-body strength

00:02:25.378 --> 00:02:27.580
and also determine your endurance,

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:30.884
and so it may even test
your fear of heights,

00:02:30.884 --> 00:02:34.120
fear of darkness, and then
you get to actually experience

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.522
the teamwork, helping each other.

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:37.657
You saw that a couple of times in there.

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:39.659
And definitely that screaming and yelling.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:43.163
Often we call it simulating
battlefield conditions.

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:44.531
Drill sergeant: Create a loop.

00:02:44.531 --> 00:02:46.299
This is the rabbit hole.

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:48.301
The rabbit comes out of his hole.

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:52.372
What is interesting is
ropes and knot tying

00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:54.240
is no longer taught in basic training.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.142
It is in Ranger School, though.

00:02:56.142 --> 00:02:58.845
You're actually tested
in the mountain phase.

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:02.615
As things change over time,
there's certain requirements

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:04.918
that the leaders think that people need

00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:06.553
upon finishing basic training.

00:03:06.553 --> 00:03:11.391
This is to be your lover,
your mistress, your concubine,

00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:14.761
perhaps the only thing in
life you'll ever truly love.

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:16.696
That's one of those things, again,

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:19.365
that you get from TV in Hollywood where

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:22.902
we place all of this level
of intimacy with our weapon.

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:25.371
Now, granted, it is an
intimate relationship

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:28.208
because you really want to be
confident with that weapon.

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:31.511
So taking it apart, putting it together

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:34.314
almost to the point where you
can do it blindfolded, right?

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:36.649
Talk about it in a way
that might be sexual,

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:38.851
that's kind of a Hollywood
thing that happens.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.286
Oh, I'm sorry, sergeant.

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:44.023
I can't touch a gun.

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:46.059
For that time period, being World War II,

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:47.694
being a conscientious objector

00:03:47.694 --> 00:03:49.362
was going to definitely be a challenge

00:03:49.362 --> 00:03:50.630
for the leadership here.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:54.067
This guy particularly is Desmond Doss.

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.135
He was actually a Medal
of Honor recipient,

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:58.171
and he didn't have to
carry a weapon to do it.

00:03:58.171 --> 00:04:01.774
I would definitely say
this was a nine on realism.

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:03.476
Drill instructor: Can't
think when I'm giving you

00:04:03.476 --> 00:04:04.944
a few love taps?

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:07.580
How the f--- are you
going to fire your rifle

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.250
when grenades are going off in your face?

00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:15.255
Unfortunately, what we wind
up running into a lot of times

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:17.757
is drill sergeants or DIs like this one

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:20.893
that will touch and grab by the throat.

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:24.030
That was familiar in other DI movies.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:28.701
Touching recruits in
training is unacceptable.

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:29.836
Always has been.

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:33.640
Sir, I got lost on the
way to college, sir!

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:36.276
That's exactly why, right there.

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:39.012
The open door that I could do even more,

00:04:39.012 --> 00:04:42.015
and it turns to him slamming
the kid's head into a wall,

00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:43.983
and that's why it's unacceptable.

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:45.652
Because where do you draw the line?

00:04:45.652 --> 00:04:47.854
Them paintball bullets, they hurt?

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:50.957
Oh, s---!

00:04:50.957 --> 00:04:52.592
Paintball is something,
as you notice there,

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:53.793
it was pretty painful.

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:56.663
That guy is going to
learn a valuable lesson

00:04:56.663 --> 00:04:59.932
not to put himself in a window
if he's going to engage.

00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:03.169
And so that's why paintball Simunitions,

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:04.304
is kind of what we call it,

00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:07.273
is very, very useful in
the training environment.

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:08.875
Drill sergeant: The sounds
that you are hearing

00:05:08.875 --> 00:05:10.343
are live rounds.

00:05:10.343 --> 00:05:12.111
You will get the fear out of you.

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:14.280
Keep moving! Keep your head down!

00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:18.451
Yeah, that's a trainer, a sergeant,

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:19.819
a leader's worst nightmare,

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:24.023
is to have somebody in
training lose their life.

00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:26.392
Shooting the rounds over their head,

00:05:26.392 --> 00:05:29.796
we actually do that, and
we also do it at night,

00:05:29.796 --> 00:05:32.198
but the conditions are totally different.

00:05:32.198 --> 00:05:35.468
The guns are raised up a whole lot higher,

00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:39.205
and it's what we call a safe surface zone.

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:41.974
So even if you stood up
or jumped up and down,

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:45.345
it's almost 20 feet above
the heads of the individuals.

00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:47.046
And, again, this tragedy right here,

00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:50.350
as unfortunate as it is, it does happen

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:52.785
when those things are not adhered to.

00:05:52.785 --> 00:05:57.357
For a boot-camp movie,
it's probably about a five.

00:06:00.126 --> 00:06:01.828
Rusty bayonet, Liebgott.

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:05.865
Now, thanks to these men
and their infractions,

00:06:05.865 --> 00:06:10.136
every man in the company who
had a weekend pass has lost it.

00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:13.206
This is pretty neat,
because I actually served

00:06:13.206 --> 00:06:16.376
as the command sergeant
major for this unit.

00:06:16.376 --> 00:06:20.046
For the 2nd Battalion, 506th
Parachute Infantry Regiment

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:21.647
out of the 101st Airborne.

00:06:21.647 --> 00:06:23.182
This right here is Easy Company

00:06:23.182 --> 00:06:25.818
and their preparation for World War II.

00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:28.154
During that time period in our military,

00:06:28.154 --> 00:06:30.289
not many people were married, right?

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.325
And everybody was always looking for

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:33.960
a two-day or a three-day pass.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.995
The stuff that they're being inspected on,

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:38.064
like this parade-level stuff,

00:06:38.064 --> 00:06:39.932
and they're out there in combat gear,

00:06:39.932 --> 00:06:41.768
I could see where he's going

00:06:41.768 --> 00:06:43.536
with trying to instill discipline.

00:06:43.536 --> 00:06:45.304
Just, I'm sure morale

00:06:45.304 --> 00:06:47.407
is not going to be too high much longer.

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:51.177
Platoon: Stand alone! Stand alone!

00:06:51.177 --> 00:06:52.779
Sobel: Do not help that man!

00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:54.180
Do not help that man!

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.482
We ran this mountain a couple of times.

00:06:56.482 --> 00:06:57.683
Currahee Mountain.

00:06:57.683 --> 00:06:59.719
Yeah, it's no joke.

00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:01.387
What I noticed here though, you know,

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:03.122
he was telling them to leave that guy.

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:06.159
Not leaving a fallen comrade,

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:08.060
that was part of the warrior ethos.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:09.061
And never quitting.

00:07:09.061 --> 00:07:13.132
So when you tie that together
in today's modern military,

00:07:13.132 --> 00:07:14.901
leaving somebody behind would be something

00:07:14.901 --> 00:07:16.335
that we wouldn't do.

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:18.304
That would be a 10 for
running up that hill.

00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:20.039
And for World War II,

00:07:20.039 --> 00:07:22.508
it'd probably be about a eight in realism.

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:23.910
Drill sergeant: "Yes,
drill sergeant, and "No,

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.245
drill sergeant." Do I make myself clear?

00:07:26.245 --> 00:07:28.080
Recruits: Yes, drill sergeant!

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.483
You get them in the barber
chair, spin them around,

00:07:30.483 --> 00:07:31.551
they had no hair.

00:07:31.551 --> 00:07:34.220
Like, that's even part of a
military cadence sometimes.

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:37.123
The males, anyway, are
getting their hair cut

00:07:37.123 --> 00:07:38.891
where they cut it all the way down.

00:07:38.891 --> 00:07:41.494
Female recruits are not required.

00:07:41.494 --> 00:07:42.528
And believe it or not,

00:07:42.528 --> 00:07:44.464
it's a health and hygiene issue, right?

00:07:44.464 --> 00:07:47.400
Because you get people that
come from all walks of life,

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.435
and so you don't know necessarily

00:07:49.435 --> 00:07:50.903
if they have, like, head lice

00:07:50.903 --> 00:07:54.841
or any other type of issues
underneath all the hair.

00:07:54.841 --> 00:07:57.343
Cherry: Next we got
about 100 f---ing shots.

00:07:58.644 --> 00:08:00.947
You just get shots until you
get all the way to the end

00:08:00.947 --> 00:08:02.815
and you're completely inoculated

00:08:02.815 --> 00:08:05.318
against every disease known to man, right?

00:08:07.153 --> 00:08:08.020
Execute.

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:10.490
Drill sergeant: Move, goddamn
it! Move! Let's go, move!

00:08:11.791 --> 00:08:14.494
The difference that I could
see so far in this scene is

00:08:14.494 --> 00:08:16.329
they have them all
taking their clothes off

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:18.464
in an open room or an open bay.

00:08:18.464 --> 00:08:20.867
It wouldn't be done that
way at what they call

00:08:20.867 --> 00:08:24.437
a military entrance processing
station, or the MEPS.

00:08:24.437 --> 00:08:26.906
All military members go through there,

00:08:26.906 --> 00:08:29.375
and there's no drill sergeants there.

00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:32.512
It's just a staff of military personnel,

00:08:32.512 --> 00:08:35.448
administrators, doctors, counselors,

00:08:35.448 --> 00:08:37.884
because what they're doing
is they're determining

00:08:37.884 --> 00:08:40.620
whether or not you're fit to train.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:42.154
Cherry: They had us strip
down to our underwear

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:44.257
and duckwalk the circuit of a big room.

00:08:46.092 --> 00:08:47.193
Oh, yeah, duckwalking.

00:08:47.193 --> 00:08:48.661
I remember the first time I did that.

00:08:48.661 --> 00:08:50.229
I was like, "What in the world?"

00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:52.865
They have you walk from one
side of the room to the other,

00:08:52.865 --> 00:08:56.969
and the doctor is looking
for your bone structure,

00:08:56.969 --> 00:09:00.006
your musculoskeletal development,

00:09:00.006 --> 00:09:03.776
if you're flat-footed, also
just your mobility and agility.

00:09:03.776 --> 00:09:06.112
But yeah, you definitely
wouldn't be in a room like that

00:09:06.112 --> 00:09:08.514
full of other people. Be hard to assess.

00:09:08.514 --> 00:09:10.850
I would have to say it's
about a four, right?

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:12.151
Because that's not necessarily

00:09:12.151 --> 00:09:14.720
how that whole indoctrination process

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:16.522
would happen with the drill sergeants.

00:09:16.522 --> 00:09:20.159
If you survive recruit
training, you will be a weapon.

00:09:20.159 --> 00:09:23.362
You will be a minister
of death praying for war.

00:09:23.362 --> 00:09:25.131
So far this is looking pretty good.

00:09:25.131 --> 00:09:27.366
Because in the DIs in the Marine Corps,

00:09:27.366 --> 00:09:28.467
they actually have a script.

00:09:28.467 --> 00:09:30.803
They say, "You'll sit up
straight and look at me!"

00:09:30.803 --> 00:09:33.272
So while the difference in the titles,

00:09:33.272 --> 00:09:36.776
the drill instructor was
tied into the Marine Corps.

00:09:36.776 --> 00:09:40.646
Drill sergeants came
into play for the Army.

00:09:40.646 --> 00:09:44.483
And the interaction in the
first part of boot camp

00:09:44.483 --> 00:09:47.019
is always going to be what
we call total control.

00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:49.589
Everywhere you look,
there's going to be some DI

00:09:49.589 --> 00:09:52.525
or drill sergeant
ensuring that the training

00:09:52.525 --> 00:09:55.828
that has been given is being reinforced.

00:09:55.828 --> 00:10:00.633
And so when you see R. Lee
Ermey walking around in the bay,

00:10:00.633 --> 00:10:02.802
he's doing exactly what a DI would do.

00:10:02.802 --> 00:10:05.605
He actually was a drill
sergeant, as a matter of fact.

00:10:05.605 --> 00:10:09.141
He served several years during
the Vietnam War as a DI.

00:10:09.141 --> 00:10:11.344
You maggots understand that?

00:10:11.344 --> 00:10:12.678
Sir, yes, sir!

00:10:12.678 --> 00:10:14.647
Bulls---! Can't hear you!

00:10:14.647 --> 00:10:16.082
Sir, yes, sir!

00:10:16.082 --> 00:10:18.551
If you notice, the DI
is constantly yelling,

00:10:18.551 --> 00:10:21.654
and it's not because they
like yelling at individuals,

00:10:21.654 --> 00:10:23.356
it's just so they don't have to repeat

00:10:23.356 --> 00:10:24.857
the same commands over and over.

00:10:24.857 --> 00:10:29.228
So if you have a recruit
on one end of the bay,

00:10:29.228 --> 00:10:31.731
he or she is going to
hear the instructions

00:10:31.731 --> 00:10:33.666
all the way on the other end of the bay.

00:10:33.666 --> 00:10:36.502
5-foot-9, I didn't know
they stacked s--- that high.

00:10:36.502 --> 00:10:38.904
You trying to squeeze an
inch in on me somewhere? Huh?

00:10:38.904 --> 00:10:41.140
Another thing that you
often see in these movies

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:44.043
and TV shows is the DI
or the drill sergeant

00:10:44.043 --> 00:10:46.012
doing a lot of cursing and swearing.

00:10:46.012 --> 00:10:49.949
Cursing and swearing is not
something that is acceptable,

00:10:49.949 --> 00:10:51.484
but it does happen.

00:10:51.484 --> 00:10:54.286
For that Vietnam era,
that's pretty realistic.

00:10:54.286 --> 00:10:59.025
Now you have people who are
deciding to serve their country,

00:10:59.025 --> 00:11:00.393
and it's a different view.

00:11:00.393 --> 00:11:03.229
I was part of the committee
that created the change,

00:11:03.229 --> 00:11:04.930
what we called shock and awe,

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.233
commonly called a shark attack,

00:11:07.233 --> 00:11:09.769
as opposed to this fallacy, which was

00:11:09.769 --> 00:11:12.505
we have to break them down
in order to build them up,

00:11:12.505 --> 00:11:14.407
and focus more on the teamwork.

00:11:14.407 --> 00:11:17.276
And so that's what The 100
Yards was designed to do.

00:11:17.276 --> 00:11:19.178
Day one, here's a challenge.

00:11:21.881 --> 00:11:23.249
Pop that blister!

00:11:23.249 --> 00:11:24.917
Lamont: Mm-hmm.

00:11:24.917 --> 00:11:29.255
Part of that indoctrination
when the DI comes in

00:11:29.255 --> 00:11:31.991
is the wellness of the
trainees or the recruits,

00:11:31.991 --> 00:11:34.260
and so they have to inspect the feet,

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:35.695
inspect the hands,

00:11:35.695 --> 00:11:37.663
and make sure that there's no open sores.

00:11:38.564 --> 00:11:41.333
What the f--- is that?

00:11:41.333 --> 00:11:43.135
What is that, Pvt. Pyle?

00:11:43.135 --> 00:11:44.537
Sir, a jelly doughnut, sir!

00:11:44.537 --> 00:11:46.439
We've gone away from footlockers

00:11:46.439 --> 00:11:50.342
and modularized most of
the equipment in the bay.

00:11:50.342 --> 00:11:53.345
So you wouldn't necessarily
have a footlocker in the bay,

00:11:53.345 --> 00:11:55.014
and he definitely wouldn't be standing

00:11:55.014 --> 00:11:56.248
on the equipment like that.

00:11:56.248 --> 00:11:59.485
They have this term in the
Army they call "toe the line,"

00:11:59.485 --> 00:12:01.554
and that's where they actually
stand next to the line

00:12:01.554 --> 00:12:03.489
so that they can do that inspection.

00:12:03.489 --> 00:12:07.593
From now on, whenever Pvt. Pyle f---s up,

00:12:07.593 --> 00:12:10.029
I will not punish him.

00:12:10.029 --> 00:12:13.032
I will punish all of you!

00:12:13.032 --> 00:12:15.034
Yep, there we go.

00:12:15.034 --> 00:12:18.704
Especially in the beginning
phases of boot camp,

00:12:18.704 --> 00:12:20.306
the DI and the drill sergeant

00:12:20.306 --> 00:12:23.042
is going to definitely dole out punishment

00:12:23.042 --> 00:12:26.579
as a mass punishment, even
though one person may mess up.

00:12:26.579 --> 00:12:29.615
This opportunity for
somebody to have looked

00:12:29.615 --> 00:12:33.753
and held Pyle accountable
for that doughnut was missed,

00:12:33.753 --> 00:12:35.454
and that's what the DI is reinforcing.

00:12:35.454 --> 00:12:36.922
We'll hold each other accountable.

00:12:36.922 --> 00:12:38.524
I would have to say it was probably

00:12:38.524 --> 00:12:40.960
about a nine for that era.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:42.628
If we're talking in today's terms,

00:12:42.628 --> 00:12:45.364
it'd probably be about a seven in realism.

00:12:45.364 --> 00:12:49.235
We are going to win this war
because we have the best men.

