00:24:41.179 --> 00:24:44.516
they immediately abandon the
attack and start falling back.

00:24:44.516 --> 00:24:46.318
This is as good as you're going to get

00:24:46.318 --> 00:24:47.819
on celluloid or on digits.

00:24:47.819 --> 00:24:49.454
If there is ever going to be a 10

00:24:49.454 --> 00:24:52.190
for "rate a tank battle,"
this is probably it.

00:24:59.998 --> 00:25:02.367
The tanks represent Saint-Chamond.

00:25:02.367 --> 00:25:04.636
There's only one that exists in the world.

00:25:04.636 --> 00:25:07.305
It's at the French national
tank museum in Saumur.

00:25:07.305 --> 00:25:09.407
Now, the cognoscenti will look
at the road wheels on that

00:25:09.407 --> 00:25:11.142
and realize that the vehicle is based off

00:25:11.142 --> 00:25:14.546
a BMP or an MT-LB, which are
Soviet Cold War vehicles.

00:25:18.149 --> 00:25:20.485
By this point in the war, we're talking

00:25:20.485 --> 00:25:23.722
towards the end of 1918, the
Germans are very familiar

00:25:23.722 --> 00:25:26.191
with tanks, but you'll still
want to shoot them anyway

00:25:26.191 --> 00:25:27.025
for a couple of reasons.

00:25:27.025 --> 00:25:29.995
One is if the tanks are
accompanying infantry,

00:25:29.995 --> 00:25:31.696
well, you're going to have
an effect on the infantry

00:25:31.696 --> 00:25:33.365
and maybe suppress them to the point

00:25:33.365 --> 00:25:35.267
that the tanks are going
to advance on their own.

00:25:35.267 --> 00:25:39.504
Secondly, the tanks were not
the most robust at the time.

00:25:39.504 --> 00:25:43.008
There was a problem known
as splash, especially when

00:25:43.008 --> 00:25:45.877
once the tankers would actually
wear a type of chain mail

00:25:45.877 --> 00:25:50.048
in front of their face with
goggles to stop the pieces

00:25:50.048 --> 00:25:52.918
of bullet coming in the gaps in the armor.

00:25:52.918 --> 00:25:54.553
Finally, it's psychological.

00:25:54.553 --> 00:25:57.889
It makes you feel a lot better
to actually do something,

00:25:57.889 --> 00:26:00.125
even if it's a gesture of futility.

00:26:04.229 --> 00:26:07.432
The early tank guns for
World War I would have

00:26:07.432 --> 00:26:10.201
generally either been
converted artillery pieces

00:26:10.201 --> 00:26:11.937
or, quite often, naval guns.

00:26:11.937 --> 00:26:14.706
The big problem you're going to have

00:26:14.706 --> 00:26:16.741
with getting them close
is called depression.

00:26:16.741 --> 00:26:20.478
So there is a stop as to how
far down the gun will depress,

00:26:20.478 --> 00:26:23.114
and that determines how close you can get

00:26:23.114 --> 00:26:27.085
to the enemy trench before you
can have to stop engaging it.

00:26:30.589 --> 00:26:34.259
To this date, trench-crossing
ability in terms of meters,

00:26:34.259 --> 00:26:36.294
how many meters of
trench you can cross, is

00:26:36.294 --> 00:26:38.129
still a criterion.

00:26:38.129 --> 00:26:41.600
The idea of can you cross
it without collapsing it,

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:44.235
well, that depends if you
want to collapse it or not.

00:26:44.235 --> 00:26:47.806
So even as late as the Iraq campaigns,

00:26:47.806 --> 00:26:50.275
a tank would go up to
fortified enemy position,

00:26:50.275 --> 00:26:54.245
turn on the spot, and
bury the people inside.

00:26:54.245 --> 00:26:57.782
And it's one of those unfortunate
realities of basically,

00:26:57.782 --> 00:26:59.918
tanks have many ways of killing you,

00:26:59.918 --> 00:27:01.620
and the tracks are one of them.

00:27:06.191 --> 00:27:08.693
You'd think today a hand grenade
isn't going to do very much.

00:27:08.693 --> 00:27:10.629
The tracks on the
Saint-Chamond were basically

00:27:10.629 --> 00:27:14.499
for my whole tractor, so
they weren't as battle-proof

00:27:14.499 --> 00:27:17.202
as a modern vehicle would be.

00:27:17.202 --> 00:27:18.837
In fact, the armor on
this is so thin that a lot

00:27:18.837 --> 00:27:22.607
of heavy bullets, they even
developed rifle bullets

00:27:22.607 --> 00:27:24.809
with an armor-piercing core could go

00:27:24.809 --> 00:27:26.444
through these early tanks.

00:27:26.444 --> 00:27:28.880
This is an eight and a half, maybe nine.

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:31.016
I could quibble a few things,

00:27:31.016 --> 00:27:33.485
but it generally gets the concept across.

00:27:40.191 --> 00:27:42.394
Oh, goody.

00:27:42.394 --> 00:27:47.399
So Rambo has gotten into his,
I'm going to call it a T-72.

00:27:47.532 --> 00:27:49.401
It's not actually,

00:27:49.401 --> 00:27:51.970
but the helicopter here represents a Hind.

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:54.973
I think it's actually
a Puma, but the Hind,

00:27:54.973 --> 00:27:59.678
the Mi-24, was a gunship
that still actually has

00:27:59.678 --> 00:28:02.714
a troop-carrying capacity,
as well as carrying a lot

00:28:02.714 --> 00:28:06.084
of rockets and missiles
and being heavily armored.

00:28:06.084 --> 00:28:09.521
It's basically a flying tank,
for lack of a better term.

00:28:16.094 --> 00:28:18.363
So hang on, is he
driving, or is he gunning?

00:28:18.363 --> 00:28:20.031
There's only one tank that I can think of

00:28:20.031 --> 00:28:23.401
that is fully combat-capable
with one crewman,

00:28:23.401 --> 00:28:25.904
and that is the Swedish Strv 103,

00:28:25.904 --> 00:28:27.739
commonly known as the S-tank.

00:28:27.739 --> 00:28:30.842
The driver functions
as the gunner as well,

00:28:30.842 --> 00:28:32.377
and this is why you're supposed to go

00:28:32.377 --> 00:28:35.513
with anti-aircraft vehicles
in company with you,

00:28:35.513 --> 00:28:39.250
because their job is to defend
you against air threats.

00:28:46.458 --> 00:28:49.561
So our enemy is in an attack
helicopter, a gunship,

00:28:49.561 --> 00:28:53.231
and it's got missiles on the
tips, AT-2 Swatters probably,

00:28:53.231 --> 00:28:55.600
that are effective from
several kilometers away.

00:28:55.600 --> 00:28:58.703
And instead, he's firing unguided rockets

00:28:58.703 --> 00:29:03.074
at the front of the Rambo tank.

00:29:03.074 --> 00:29:05.410
Why doesn't he just fly
around the back and shoot

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:08.279
the rockets into the engine
deck or the rear turret,

00:29:08.279 --> 00:29:10.949
or you know, some place
that'll actually cause damage?

00:29:18.289 --> 00:29:20.892
And so the enemy pilot dies the death

00:29:20.892 --> 00:29:23.161
that his stupidity deserved.

00:29:23.161 --> 00:29:26.965
Officially, if you must
charge towards an aircraft,

00:29:26.965 --> 00:29:28.600
do it at an angle.

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:32.237
He has to not only aim for lead,

00:29:32.237 --> 00:29:35.406
but also the change in
distance at the same time.

00:29:35.406 --> 00:29:37.242
Whereas if you're
charging straight at him,

00:29:37.242 --> 00:29:38.977
you're simplifying the problem

00:29:38.977 --> 00:29:40.445
for the aircraft that's coming at you.

00:29:40.445 --> 00:29:42.113
He doesn't need to worry about aiming.

00:29:42.113 --> 00:29:44.082
It has a tank, so I'll give it a one.

00:29:46.751 --> 00:29:47.585
Fire.

00:29:50.522 --> 00:29:52.190
The premise of an ambush

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:56.127
in an urban setting by
armor is not unheard of,

00:29:56.127 --> 00:29:59.364
but the idea of armor
operating in an urban setting

00:29:59.364 --> 00:30:02.467
without infantry support
is normally questionable.

00:30:02.467 --> 00:30:06.504
Panthers are not well suited
for close-range fighting.

00:30:06.504 --> 00:30:09.307
They really would excel
on the Eastern Front,

00:30:09.307 --> 00:30:11.276
well, the steppes of Ukraine, for example,

00:30:11.276 --> 00:30:13.144
nice long lines of fire.

00:30:13.144 --> 00:30:15.947
That said, they can get
away with it in this context

00:30:15.947 --> 00:30:19.484
because they know that
the enemy is a single tank

00:30:19.484 --> 00:30:20.652
with no infantry.

00:30:23.454 --> 00:30:24.689
Oh look, you're going
through a building again,

00:30:24.689 --> 00:30:26.157
except this time he's
done one thing right.

00:30:26.157 --> 00:30:28.493
He's got the turret
traversed over the rear.

00:30:28.493 --> 00:30:30.028
I'd mentioned before, you don't want to go

00:30:30.028 --> 00:30:31.629
through a building if you can avoid it.

00:30:31.629 --> 00:30:32.864
In this case, I guess they couldn't,

00:30:32.864 --> 00:30:34.165
because they're getting shot at.

00:30:34.165 --> 00:30:37.068
And if you are going to go
through, spin your turret over,

00:30:37.068 --> 00:30:39.304
and you'll keep yourself safe.

00:30:46.811 --> 00:30:47.645
And what you can see here,

00:30:47.645 --> 00:30:50.715
the T-34-85 has a very
neat traverse handle,

00:30:50.715 --> 00:30:51.983
so it performs two functions.

00:30:51.983 --> 00:30:54.752
In this case, you can see
he has it in manual mode,

00:30:54.752 --> 00:30:56.888
cranking around, as
opposed to electric mode,

00:30:56.888 --> 00:30:59.090
which, you simply flip
the handle 90 degrees.

00:30:59.090 --> 00:31:02.060
However, maybe the gunner
just doesn't know this,

00:31:02.060 --> 00:31:05.563
because in the movie,
this was a found tank.

00:31:05.563 --> 00:31:08.399
Even myself, when I got into
a T-34-85 for the first time,

00:31:08.399 --> 00:31:10.902
and trying to see where is
the second control handle,

00:31:10.902 --> 00:31:12.070
I can't find it.

00:31:21.379 --> 00:31:23.248
This is quite feasible.

00:31:23.248 --> 00:31:25.016
When faced with the Panther
in this close range,

00:31:25.016 --> 00:31:26.951
T-34 crew now has a choice.

00:31:26.951 --> 00:31:31.389
It can either run away, or
it can attempt to engage.

00:31:31.389 --> 00:31:33.358
Now, I would probably agree

00:31:33.358 --> 00:31:35.260
with the 34 crew on this for two reasons.

00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:37.695
One, the Panther was looking
the wrong way entirely.

00:31:37.695 --> 00:31:41.933
And the other thing is Panthers
are not the easiest thing

00:31:41.933 --> 00:31:42.767
in the world to kill,

00:31:42.767 --> 00:31:44.802
unless you can get around
the side or behind them.

00:31:44.802 --> 00:31:47.906
And in this case, they're
already behind them,

00:31:47.906 --> 00:31:49.140
so why not engage?

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:52.410
But the only thing I might have
done differently was back up

00:31:52.410 --> 00:31:54.312
two or three tank lengths

00:31:54.312 --> 00:31:57.415
and aim the gun into the engine deck.

00:31:57.415 --> 00:32:00.318
That would be a more guaranteed kill

00:32:00.318 --> 00:32:02.053
than trying to aim for the turret ring.

00:32:09.627 --> 00:32:11.629
So the explosion was
actually a little bit faster

00:32:11.629 --> 00:32:12.931
than what would really happen.

00:32:12.931 --> 00:32:15.934
Generally speaking, when a
tank is hit, it takes a couple

00:32:15.934 --> 00:32:19.137
of seconds, and those are
your couple of seconds

00:32:19.137 --> 00:32:21.272
that is the difference
between life and death

00:32:21.272 --> 00:32:22.640
when you're getting out of a vehicle.

00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:26.678
Although in reality, the
net-effect difference

00:32:26.678 --> 00:32:29.447
to the crew is academic.

00:32:29.447 --> 00:32:31.115
They're basically toast.

00:32:31.115 --> 00:32:34.152
In this context, I don't
know, maybe a seven.

00:32:39.857 --> 00:32:43.928
The movie is set about the
time of the Yemeni civil war.

00:32:43.928 --> 00:32:45.763
It's actually kind of still ongoing.

00:32:45.763 --> 00:32:48.099
The film was made in Morocco,

00:32:48.099 --> 00:32:52.303
hence, you have the M60s and
the T-72s. So the exact nature

00:32:52.303 --> 00:32:55.340
of the tanks that you get in
Yemen isn't entirely there,

00:32:55.340 --> 00:32:59.010
but hey, for movie purposes,
it gets the point across.

00:32:59.010 --> 00:33:04.682
Certainly there are Western
and Eastern tanks in the area.

00:33:04.682 --> 00:33:07.218
Tank Alpha, attack from the right!

00:33:08.019 --> 00:33:09.120
Hong Xu!

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:11.656
Turn 20 degrees left, hurry!

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:13.491
So what I have here is

00:33:13.491 --> 00:33:16.527
a VIC-1 CVC, combat
vehicle crewman, helmet.

00:33:16.527 --> 00:33:18.029
And the reason you would
want to wear a helmet

00:33:18.029 --> 00:33:20.398
even if you're inside
the tank is quite simply,

00:33:20.398 --> 00:33:21.332
you're being thrown around,

00:33:21.332 --> 00:33:24.869
and there's a lot of sharp,
pointy things sticking out at you

00:33:24.869 --> 00:33:25.970
that as you're being thrown around,

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:27.005
you don't want to hurt your head.

00:33:27.005 --> 00:33:30.008
And of course, you also
have the dust goggles,

00:33:31.209 --> 00:33:33.578
because the tanks are throwing
a lot of dust up at you.

00:33:33.578 --> 00:33:34.579
You don't have a windshield.

00:33:34.579 --> 00:33:35.880
If you're doing 40 miles an hour,

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:37.815
there's still enough
wind that you'll notice,

00:33:37.815 --> 00:33:39.751
so you wear a set of goggles, as well.

00:33:43.988 --> 00:33:45.890
It's good to see armor actually work.

00:33:45.890 --> 00:33:48.326
So what has happened there is a round hit

00:33:48.326 --> 00:33:51.262
explosive reactive armor. So
those bricks that you see,

00:33:51.262 --> 00:33:53.664
and what they generally consist of,

00:33:53.664 --> 00:33:56.067
though this is a gross oversimplification,

00:33:56.067 --> 00:33:58.269
is a couple of pieces of metal sandwiching

00:33:58.269 --> 00:34:01.072
a piece of explosive, and
that when a round hits it,

00:34:01.072 --> 00:34:04.842
it explodes and disrupt or destabilize it

00:34:04.842 --> 00:34:09.680
to the extent that the main
armor belt behind can stop it.

00:34:09.680 --> 00:34:11.049
Turn right!

00:34:12.917 --> 00:34:14.285
Nan Xia, get ready!

00:34:15.086 --> 00:34:16.454
Fire!

00:34:18.923 --> 00:34:20.091
You got a couple of problems.

00:34:20.091 --> 00:34:24.062
Firstly, if the entire
turret system is off line,

00:34:24.062 --> 00:34:25.897
then the tank is still
rocking a little bit.

00:34:25.897 --> 00:34:28.099
It may not look it much, but
it's going to be rocking enough

00:34:28.099 --> 00:34:32.737
that he cannot hip-shoot
a 125 on a point target

00:34:32.737 --> 00:34:34.238
like a tank like that.

00:34:34.238 --> 00:34:36.607
Secondly, he literally is hip-shooting,

00:34:36.607 --> 00:34:37.809
and he's eyeballing it.

00:34:37.809 --> 00:34:40.445
I don't see why he couldn't
still be using the gunsight.

00:34:40.445 --> 00:34:42.613
He can then pull the trigger himself.

00:34:51.089 --> 00:34:52.323
Sandstorms do happen.

00:34:52.323 --> 00:34:55.226
In the 2003 invasion
of Iraq, there was one

00:34:55.226 --> 00:34:57.428
that basically shut down the
war for a couple of days.

00:34:57.428 --> 00:35:00.398
Well, your big problem with a
sandstorm is the air filters.

00:35:00.398 --> 00:35:02.567
So the sand is going to
get into your air filters

00:35:02.567 --> 00:35:03.935
and clog them up very quickly.

00:35:03.935 --> 00:35:05.937
However, in this context,
I don't think they had

00:35:05.937 --> 00:35:07.605
a heck of a lot of choice,
because being chased

00:35:07.605 --> 00:35:09.340
by a platoon of M60s,

00:35:09.340 --> 00:35:11.109
they were probably going to die anyway,

00:35:11.109 --> 00:35:15.279
so why not make for the
sandstorm and hope for the best?

00:35:15.279 --> 00:35:17.682
So I think seven's probably fair.

00:35:22.787 --> 00:35:24.622
I'm not even sure where to start.

00:35:24.622 --> 00:35:29.627
I'm trying to think of any
reasonable military context

00:35:30.761 --> 00:35:32.630
that this vehicle could exist in.

00:35:32.630 --> 00:35:37.635
The US Army has a vehicle, the
M1117, which is a derivative

00:35:37.668 --> 00:35:39.704
of the old LAV-100s from Vietnam.

00:35:39.704 --> 00:35:42.039
It's basically a huge, armored 4-by-4

00:35:42.039 --> 00:35:46.110
with a gun turret on the
top for military police use.

00:35:46.110 --> 00:35:48.346
I'm sure they wouldn't
mind something like this.

00:35:52.383 --> 00:35:55.853
So generally speaking, convoys,
just because of the nature

00:35:55.853 --> 00:35:59.490
of roads, you tend to be in
column instead of line abreast

00:35:59.490 --> 00:36:00.691
like in the shot here.

00:36:00.691 --> 00:36:03.461
You have a bit of a choice,
depending on how many vehicles

00:36:03.461 --> 00:36:06.330
you have, is how far apart
you want the vehicles to be.

00:36:06.330 --> 00:36:09.200
Because the idea is that
your lead vehicle is going

00:36:09.200 --> 00:36:12.637
to be sufficiently far in
front of whatever it is

00:36:12.637 --> 00:36:14.672
that you're protecting the
main body of the convoy.

00:36:14.672 --> 00:36:18.209
So with only three vehicles,
you're kind of limited

00:36:18.209 --> 00:36:22.046
as to what you can do, but
your typical is going to be

00:36:22.046 --> 00:36:24.682
maybe two up front, then the main asset,

00:36:24.682 --> 00:36:26.250
and then one bringing up the rear.

00:36:32.223 --> 00:36:35.226
Cannons are not common,
but they are a thing.

00:36:35.226 --> 00:36:37.995
You can sometimes find
them mounted as experiments

00:36:37.995 --> 00:36:41.098
on tanks as well, but
they're basically a waste

00:36:41.098 --> 00:36:43.501
of space and weight, so you
don't see them on tanks.

00:36:47.572 --> 00:36:49.774
In terms of a missile launcher,

00:36:49.774 --> 00:36:53.110
yeah, missile launchers on
armored cars are a thing.

00:36:53.110 --> 00:36:56.514
This particular elevating
out of the 4-by-4 is

00:36:56.514 --> 00:37:01.018
probably closer to a
BRDM anti-tank system,

00:37:01.018 --> 00:37:03.454
but that's actually for
anti-armor purposes,

00:37:03.454 --> 00:37:05.456
not anti-aircraft.

00:37:05.456 --> 00:37:07.959
But hey, I guess when
you're dealing with Batman,

00:37:07.959 --> 00:37:09.293
anything goes.

00:37:09.293 --> 00:37:13.464
I got nothing. Zero. Sorry. No.

00:37:20.871 --> 00:37:23.274
The Germans appear to be
equipped with Pak 40s.

00:37:23.274 --> 00:37:27.612
It was a very good anti-tank
gun, 7.5 cm caliber,

00:37:27.612 --> 00:37:31.315
definitely a valid threat against an M4.

00:37:31.315 --> 00:37:33.284
Anti-tank! H.E.! Traverse left.

00:37:35.253 --> 00:37:37.455
Traverse left!

00:37:37.455 --> 00:37:40.157
That was a doctrinal fire command.

00:37:40.157 --> 00:37:42.059
Now, the reason I know this
is they actually sent me

00:37:42.059 --> 00:37:45.496
the script for filming
it. But that is basically

00:37:45.496 --> 00:37:49.834
alert, target, and command of execution

00:37:49.834 --> 00:37:52.236
in abbreviated fire command.

00:37:52.236 --> 00:37:54.238
The fire commands back in
World War II weren't quite

00:37:54.238 --> 00:37:55.673
as regimented as they are today,

00:37:55.673 --> 00:37:57.742
but the fundamentals are
actually still the same.

00:38:01.579 --> 00:38:03.581
You have an argument.
Do you want to advance

00:38:03.581 --> 00:38:05.683
over open ground as a whole platoon,

00:38:05.683 --> 00:38:07.318
or do you want to bound in sections?

00:38:07.318 --> 00:38:09.020
In this case, because of
the amount of infantry

00:38:09.020 --> 00:38:10.688
that they're trying to provide cover for,

00:38:10.688 --> 00:38:15.526
I can see perhaps why they
went as a full platoon on line.

00:38:15.526 --> 00:38:17.995
But also, it's a very,
very open, long field,

00:38:17.995 --> 00:38:20.464
so maybe it was necessary
for the entire platoon

00:38:20.464 --> 00:38:24.001
to advance on line simply so
that they could see the target.

00:38:26.804 --> 00:38:29.974
Another gun, who's got eyes on it?

00:38:29.974 --> 00:38:32.643
Somebody's going to ask,
how can a Pak 40 miss

00:38:32.643 --> 00:38:34.478
at such short range?

00:38:34.478 --> 00:38:38.182
You'll be surprised what
stress is going to do to you.

00:38:38.182 --> 00:38:41.986
So there have been
studies, and the reputation

00:38:41.986 --> 00:38:44.288
of the 88 is such that it doesn't miss.

00:38:44.288 --> 00:38:48.359
In reality, they're firing 20
rounds to get a single kill,

00:38:48.359 --> 00:38:53.364
because the stress of combat
is such that it is not

00:38:54.031 --> 00:38:56.367
calm and collected on a gunnery range

00:38:56.367 --> 00:38:57.668
when you're doing scientific testing.

00:38:57.668 --> 00:39:00.204
It's, "Oh God, I'm about to die."

00:39:00.204 --> 00:39:04.308
Let's light them up. Let's
go. Let's clean it up.

00:39:07.812 --> 00:39:10.648
Now, that scene is basically
why I became a tanker.

00:39:10.648 --> 00:39:13.517
A line of tanks, every machine gun going.

00:39:13.517 --> 00:39:15.920
A single tank is considered
to have firepower

00:39:15.920 --> 00:39:18.656
more or less equivalent
to an infantry platoon,

00:39:18.656 --> 00:39:20.424
because you have the
heavy machine gun up top,

00:39:20.424 --> 00:39:22.860
you got a co-ax machine gun,
you got a bow machine gun,

00:39:22.860 --> 00:39:25.329
and oh, by the way, you've got a cannon.

00:39:25.329 --> 00:39:27.698
That is a lot of firepower
from a single vehicle,

00:39:27.698 --> 00:39:29.900
and you can imagine this sheer suppression

00:39:29.900 --> 00:39:31.836
and psychological effect
that comes of this,

00:39:31.836 --> 00:39:33.437
let alone the lethal effects.

00:39:33.437 --> 00:39:34.672
I know there were folks that didn't like

00:39:34.672 --> 00:39:36.941
that I gave the last "Fury" a nine,

00:39:36.941 --> 00:39:38.943
but I'm giving this one a nine as well.

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:44.915
I can't get elevation and he knows it.

00:39:44.915 --> 00:39:48.119
So this was an actual
problem in Afghanistan,

00:39:48.119 --> 00:39:51.188
and it's a mountainous
country, is that their tanks,

00:39:51.188 --> 00:39:53.391
which were designed
really for open warfare

00:39:53.391 --> 00:39:56.527
on the plains of Germany,
can't elevate high enough.

00:39:56.527 --> 00:39:59.630
So what the Soviets ended
up doing was they developed

00:39:59.630 --> 00:40:03.300
a variant of the ZSU-23-4
anti-aircraft gun,

00:40:03.300 --> 00:40:06.270
the Shilka, called the Afghani.

00:40:06.270 --> 00:40:07.938
So they would use those in conjunction

00:40:07.938 --> 00:40:09.807
with the tanks to provide

00:40:09.807 --> 00:40:13.944
that close-range, high-elevation security

00:40:13.944 --> 00:40:17.448
that Daskal here is having a problem with.

00:40:22.920 --> 00:40:25.656
The actual effect of hitting
the gun tube is not going to be

00:40:25.656 --> 00:40:28.659
the petaling like you see
here. That's for visual effect.

00:40:28.659 --> 00:40:31.228
What you're actually going
to get is a very small hole

00:40:32.129 --> 00:40:33.597
that goes through the gun tube,

00:40:33.597 --> 00:40:34.799
but it's still plenty bad enough

00:40:34.799 --> 00:40:36.100
that you've disabled the gun.

00:40:43.340 --> 00:40:46.644
The grenade will not have
separated the tracks,

00:40:46.644 --> 00:40:49.113
so I must conclude it was the rocks,

00:40:49.113 --> 00:40:51.782
but it's not impossible for rocky terrain

00:40:51.782 --> 00:40:56.787
and maybe a less experienced
driver to angle his track

00:40:56.787 --> 00:41:00.658
in such a way when he's turning
that he gets rocks caught

00:41:00.658 --> 00:41:03.828
in between the wheels and the
tracks and snaps the track pin,

00:41:03.828 --> 00:41:05.796
and that throws your track and loses it.

00:41:05.796 --> 00:41:08.699
This scene is probably
a seven to an eight.

00:41:08.732 --> 00:41:09.967
My name's Nicholas Irving.

00:41:09.967 --> 00:41:13.771
I'm a former US Army Ranger
special-operations sniper.

00:41:13.771 --> 00:41:15.139
I'm also known as The Reaper

00:41:15.139 --> 00:41:18.742
for my 33 confirmed kills in Afghanistan.

00:41:23.113 --> 00:41:24.648
Eve: I may have a shot.

00:41:24.648 --> 00:41:25.516
Absolutely not.

00:41:25.516 --> 00:41:27.618
She's offhand. Like, there's no support.

00:41:27.618 --> 00:41:31.489
It's all muscling it in to make that shot.

00:41:31.489 --> 00:41:34.291
And that gun, well, yeah, that's, no.

00:41:34.291 --> 00:41:37.194
The Olympic Arms

00:41:37.194 --> 00:41:40.531
K23B is what it is.

00:41:40.531 --> 00:41:42.700
That weapon is literally accurate

00:41:42.700 --> 00:41:44.768
for up to, like, 50 meters.

00:41:44.768 --> 00:41:47.805
That distance looks like it's about,

00:41:47.805 --> 00:41:50.007
at most, a couple of hundred yards.

00:41:50.007 --> 00:41:52.843
Put it back in the case and go
back and get a different one.

00:41:54.478 --> 00:41:55.980
It's not clean.

00:41:55.980 --> 00:41:58.148
Repeat, I do not have a clean shot.

00:41:58.148 --> 00:42:00.317
Yeah I don't think she
had a clean shot at all.

00:42:00.317 --> 00:42:03.687
In the military a clean shot would be,

00:42:03.687 --> 00:42:06.323
you're not going to
wound or injure or kill

00:42:06.323 --> 00:42:07.858
something you don't intend to.

00:42:07.858 --> 00:42:10.127
If her target is one of those two men,

00:42:10.127 --> 00:42:12.663
that bullet, you know, it's a 5.56.

00:42:12.663 --> 00:42:14.331
It's going to zip right
through the bad guy

00:42:14.331 --> 00:42:17.067
and potentially wound or kill

00:42:17.067 --> 00:42:19.537
the guy she's trying to help out or save.

00:42:19.537 --> 00:42:20.838
M: I said take the shot.

00:42:20.838 --> 00:42:22.273
I can't. I may hit Bond.

00:42:22.273 --> 00:42:24.575
Take the bloody shot!

00:42:24.575 --> 00:42:26.944
If I was forced to take this
shot, I'll pull the trigger,

00:42:26.944 --> 00:42:29.046
but I'm not going to
guarantee you anything.

00:42:29.046 --> 00:42:33.150
Luckily the train does
kind of come towards her,

00:42:33.150 --> 00:42:34.852
as opposed to moving left or right.

00:42:34.852 --> 00:42:37.655
That takes out so much
more of the mathematics

00:42:37.655 --> 00:42:39.490
behind making a shot.

00:42:39.490 --> 00:42:42.126
When it's moving closer or away from you,

00:42:42.126 --> 00:42:45.029
it's all about just
aiming above the target

00:42:45.029 --> 00:42:46.497
or a little bit below the target,

00:42:46.497 --> 00:42:49.767
as opposed to, you know,
aiming in front of it

00:42:49.767 --> 00:42:53.170
and having that individual
essentially run into the bullet.

00:42:53.170 --> 00:42:54.438
I would rate it a one.

00:42:54.438 --> 00:42:57.174
Like, it's just not happening at all.

00:43:02.313 --> 00:43:06.250
That little good tactical
deep-breath pause

00:43:06.250 --> 00:43:08.385
before the shot was really good.

00:43:08.385 --> 00:43:12.356
To be more precise, you
essentially have to be calm,

00:43:12.356 --> 00:43:16.193
and one of the easiest
ways to calm your body down

00:43:16.193 --> 00:43:18.696
right before a shot is
to just take deep breaths

00:43:18.696 --> 00:43:20.431
and get the blood circulating.

00:43:23.968 --> 00:43:27.104
I would've got more
square behind the rifle,

00:43:27.104 --> 00:43:30.908
as opposed to having my legs
off to the side like that.

00:43:30.908 --> 00:43:34.645
Maybe got down in a squatting
stance or on my knees

00:43:34.645 --> 00:43:38.415
and kind of put my weight behind the gun.

00:43:38.415 --> 00:43:39.583
That way when it recoils

00:43:39.583 --> 00:43:41.719
it's kind of more of
a straight-back motion

00:43:41.719 --> 00:43:44.521
and not this motion.

00:43:44.521 --> 00:43:46.624
He's going to pretty much get that

00:43:46.624 --> 00:43:48.525
just because of his body alignment

00:43:48.525 --> 00:43:49.960
and the way he's holding the rifle.

00:43:51.161 --> 00:43:52.930
Mike: There you go.

00:43:55.799 --> 00:43:57.067
Yeah, I think it was smart for him

00:43:57.067 --> 00:43:59.536
to let the vehicle get closer to him.

00:43:59.536 --> 00:44:01.338
Everybody thinks being a sniper,

00:44:01.338 --> 00:44:03.273
we want the longest, furthest shots,

00:44:03.273 --> 00:44:05.542
but ideally, you want the closer ones

00:44:05.542 --> 00:44:08.045
just because it makes
your job that much easier

00:44:08.045 --> 00:44:11.348
and it's almost like 100%
guarantee you're going to hit.

00:44:11.348 --> 00:44:14.518
But with that, if I'm protecting someone

00:44:14.518 --> 00:44:18.922
and I shoot the vehicle
within a few yards from them,

00:44:18.922 --> 00:44:21.291
the guy is still going to get
hit by the moving vehicle.

00:44:22.993 --> 00:44:27.231
I don't like that the car
just did a triple axel,

00:44:27.231 --> 00:44:29.433
like, 20 feet from the guy.

00:44:29.433 --> 00:44:31.135
Like, that's not how that works at all.

00:44:31.135 --> 00:44:32.636
The only moving vehicles that I've shot,

00:44:32.636 --> 00:44:33.804
I was a machine gunner.

00:44:33.804 --> 00:44:36.540
And I've never once made a car flip over.

00:44:36.540 --> 00:44:39.476
Normally they just crash into a wall

00:44:39.476 --> 00:44:41.278
or crash into another parked car

00:44:41.278 --> 00:44:43.814
or just roll off the side
of the road into the desert.

00:44:43.814 --> 00:44:44.748
I'd give it a six.

00:44:44.748 --> 00:44:46.150
The only thing that's pulling me back

00:44:46.150 --> 00:44:50.654
or giving it a lesser number
is because of the sniper

00:44:50.654 --> 00:44:52.256
waiting for the car to get closer.

00:44:59.930 --> 00:45:04.468
I mean, as far as being a
highly accurate sniper rifle

00:45:04.468 --> 00:45:07.137
concealed as a flute, I don't think so.

00:45:07.137 --> 00:45:09.506
There's a lot of big
things on a sniper rifle

00:45:09.506 --> 00:45:13.043
that makes it accurate, but
the barrel is really important,

00:45:13.043 --> 00:45:17.214
and most barrels on sniper
rifles are fluted and floated.

00:45:17.214 --> 00:45:19.550
There's no interference between the barrel

00:45:19.550 --> 00:45:20.818
and the stock of the gun,

00:45:20.818 --> 00:45:23.887
as to not interfere with the
harmonics when it goes off.

00:45:23.887 --> 00:45:27.825
That flute looks like one
continuously long, long object.

00:45:27.825 --> 00:45:29.393
It probably is accurate,

00:45:29.393 --> 00:45:31.729
maybe at the distances
that he's shooting at,

00:45:31.729 --> 00:45:33.497
but I also don't know what
caliber he's shooting.

00:45:33.497 --> 00:45:36.333
But it's Tom Cruise, so it's
probably really accurate.

00:45:41.939 --> 00:45:45.676
I'd give the female
sniper the best position.

00:45:45.676 --> 00:45:46.510
Whatever she's in,

00:45:46.510 --> 00:45:49.413
that little room or the
designs on the walls,

00:45:49.413 --> 00:45:51.515
I think that really
gives her an advantage,

00:45:51.515 --> 00:45:53.917
and it almost is like
shooting out of a loophole.

00:45:53.917 --> 00:45:56.487
It's like a tactic snipers used overseas

00:45:56.487 --> 00:45:59.323
where you punch a small hole
through a wall or something

00:45:59.323 --> 00:46:01.058
and a sniper would back away from the wall

00:46:01.058 --> 00:46:04.361
and shoot through that hole
to hit an object outside.

00:46:04.361 --> 00:46:06.063
The guy with the shades on,

00:46:06.063 --> 00:46:09.867
well, he's not really
in a concealed position,

00:46:09.867 --> 00:46:12.569
but I guess he's using his attire

00:46:12.569 --> 00:46:15.773
to make him least stand out.

00:46:15.773 --> 00:46:19.042
Worst position, I'd
have to say Tom Cruise.

00:46:23.413 --> 00:46:25.649
Any sniper rifle can be suppressed,

00:46:25.649 --> 00:46:28.752
even up to, like,
.50-caliber sniper rifles.

00:46:28.752 --> 00:46:31.822
You're still going to hear a noise.

00:46:31.822 --> 00:46:34.658
It sounds like a clap, a loud clap,

00:46:34.658 --> 00:46:38.262
or if you took a ruler
and smacked it on a desk.

00:46:38.262 --> 00:46:40.264
Unless you use, like, subsonic rounds,

00:46:40.264 --> 00:46:43.267
which he may be using in this instance,

00:46:43.267 --> 00:46:46.236
where I could see nobody else
would really hear anything.

00:46:49.139 --> 00:46:52.409
Shooting lights are actually
extremely hard to do.

00:46:52.409 --> 00:46:54.711
Like, that was one of our
things overseas sometimes.

00:46:54.711 --> 00:46:56.580
When an assault team is
going up on a building,

00:46:56.580 --> 00:46:58.649
they want the lights shot out for them

00:46:58.649 --> 00:47:01.351
to make it dark, and that's,
it's actually pretty tough

00:47:01.351 --> 00:47:02.653
because it blurs out everything,

00:47:02.653 --> 00:47:04.588
and it's hard to find exactly,

00:47:04.588 --> 00:47:05.923
I guess, where the middle is at.

00:47:05.923 --> 00:47:07.291
I'll give it a five.

00:47:07.291 --> 00:47:09.927
Those shots are, you know, possible,

00:47:09.927 --> 00:47:11.728
but I don't think that this

00:47:11.728 --> 00:47:13.697
scenario, setup is that realistic.

00:47:16.466 --> 00:47:18.001
I grew up watching this movie.

00:47:18.001 --> 00:47:20.871
It's one of the reasons why
I wanted to become a sniper.

00:47:20.871 --> 00:47:22.806
Every sniper, you have your rifle

00:47:22.806 --> 00:47:24.208
and you have your ghillie suit.

00:47:24.208 --> 00:47:26.276
What it essentially does
is break up the outline

00:47:26.276 --> 00:47:29.746
of the human body and mimics
or makes you appear to be

00:47:29.746 --> 00:47:32.416
brush or foliage.

00:47:32.416 --> 00:47:33.917
But it's not just the ghillie suit

00:47:33.917 --> 00:47:35.886
that's what makes you camouflage.

00:47:35.886 --> 00:47:37.888
What you're going to really want to do

00:47:37.888 --> 00:47:41.692
is put a bunch of natural
vegetation into the ghillie suit

00:47:41.692 --> 00:47:43.760
so you look more like
the natural surrounding,

00:47:43.760 --> 00:47:46.930
as opposed to just this
big pile of ghillie suit.

00:47:46.930 --> 00:47:48.632
Sniper: Three to five on the legs.

00:47:51.268 --> 00:47:53.904
You have the spotter
and you have the sniper.

00:47:53.904 --> 00:47:55.906
The spotter's the one who's
not pulling the trigger,

00:47:55.906 --> 00:47:59.743
and he's holding up a, or
using a spotting scope.

00:47:59.743 --> 00:48:03.347
Through his, it looked
like a red-dot camera thing

00:48:03.347 --> 00:48:04.248
or something like that,

00:48:04.248 --> 00:48:05.983
and that's just not what it looks like.

00:48:05.983 --> 00:48:07.651
Either you're going to have a reticle

00:48:07.651 --> 00:48:10.721
that mimics or is similar
to what the sniper's using.

00:48:10.721 --> 00:48:14.725
These mil dots or radian
lines are going to

00:48:14.725 --> 00:48:16.326
give you your distance

00:48:16.326 --> 00:48:19.796
and be able to track
where the bullet impacts.

00:48:19.796 --> 00:48:20.797
He's moving.

00:48:23.133 --> 00:48:25.636
Sniper: 650. Range me.

00:48:27.337 --> 00:48:30.941
Being a sniper, like,
80% to 90% of your job

00:48:30.941 --> 00:48:33.944
is observation. It's actually really rare

00:48:33.944 --> 00:48:36.480
that a sniper does get a
chance to pull the trigger.

00:48:36.480 --> 00:48:39.016
Most of our stuff is just
relaying information,

00:48:39.016 --> 00:48:40.550
watching stuff, taking pictures,

00:48:40.550 --> 00:48:42.986
and telling the commanders
what's going on on the ground.

00:48:42.986 --> 00:48:45.656
So you can be out there for
as long as the job needs.

00:48:50.627 --> 00:48:52.996
I think everything he
did there was just fine.

00:48:52.996 --> 00:48:57.834
Looks like he's using an
M24 sniper rifle, could be,

00:48:57.834 --> 00:48:59.970
or something similar to that, a variant,

00:48:59.970 --> 00:49:03.440
but they're good out to, like, 800 meters.

00:49:03.440 --> 00:49:06.176
At 650 yards, like, that's not,

00:49:06.176 --> 00:49:08.912
that's far, but it's not
that far for a sniper.

00:49:08.912 --> 00:49:12.482
And a body shot, which is
what I'd normally aim for,

00:49:12.482 --> 00:49:14.151
was perfect.

00:49:14.151 --> 00:49:16.453
Like, that's all you're going
to go for at that distance.

00:49:16.453 --> 00:49:19.389
My only issue, would
probably have something soft

00:49:19.389 --> 00:49:22.559
underneath his rifle,
maybe put down a pack

00:49:22.559 --> 00:49:26.029
or his backpack or
something to rest it on.

00:49:26.029 --> 00:49:30.467
That helps with the harmonics
and having the rifle bounce

00:49:30.467 --> 00:49:31.768
and get all the way from you.

00:49:31.768 --> 00:49:33.804
I'll give it a seven. I was
going to give it an eight,

00:49:33.804 --> 00:49:35.572
but a seven because the sniper scope

00:49:35.572 --> 00:49:36.907
and the reticles and all that stuff

00:49:36.907 --> 00:49:38.241
just didn't make any sense.

00:49:44.648 --> 00:49:48.251
To stay still when you're
pinned down by a sniper,

00:49:48.251 --> 00:49:50.954
it's almost a death wish.

00:49:50.954 --> 00:49:53.256
It's just a matter of
time before he's going to

00:49:53.256 --> 00:49:55.859
figure something out to
make those bullets impact.

00:49:55.859 --> 00:49:57.561
Running zigzag patterns,

00:49:57.561 --> 00:50:00.030
getting out as fast as you
can is probably your best bet.

00:50:05.335 --> 00:50:07.404
To prevent an enemy coming in

00:50:07.404 --> 00:50:10.707
to do what just happened
here, sneak up behind you,

00:50:10.707 --> 00:50:13.043
in sniper school they teach you

00:50:13.043 --> 00:50:14.644
no more than, like, two shots.

00:50:14.644 --> 00:50:18.015
Two or three shots, your
sniper hide is burnt.

00:50:18.015 --> 00:50:21.251
You would want to move
ideally to a new location.

00:50:21.251 --> 00:50:22.953
Once you have a good sniper hide set up,

00:50:22.953 --> 00:50:26.156
the odds of you getting
out after that second shot,

00:50:26.156 --> 00:50:27.858
third shot, it's slim.

00:50:27.858 --> 00:50:31.595
But there are countermeasures
to deter people

00:50:31.595 --> 00:50:33.196
from entering that sniper hide.

00:50:33.196 --> 00:50:37.467
Like, for us, we might put up
a Claymore or some booby traps

00:50:37.467 --> 00:50:39.636
or something like that behind us,

00:50:39.636 --> 00:50:41.071
or even a guy with a machine gun

00:50:41.071 --> 00:50:43.673
is usually watching our back.

00:50:49.146 --> 00:50:50.914
That actually wasn't too bad,

00:50:50.914 --> 00:50:52.783
and it's at nighttime
with no night vision,

00:50:52.783 --> 00:50:54.985
so I'll give him that.

00:50:54.985 --> 00:50:57.354
We hit targets at nighttime,

00:50:57.354 --> 00:50:59.623
but the distances are extremely close.

00:50:59.623 --> 00:51:02.459
We had a sniper scope that was

00:51:02.459 --> 00:51:05.962
mounted with a PVS-14, I believe,

00:51:05.962 --> 00:51:08.098
which is a night-vision attachment

00:51:08.098 --> 00:51:09.299
that goes on the front of your scope.

00:51:13.970 --> 00:51:17.074
Bolt guns, bolt rifles,
they jam all the time,

00:51:17.074 --> 00:51:19.209
especially in dusty conditions.

00:51:19.209 --> 00:51:22.012
That bolt gun, when you pull it back,

00:51:22.012 --> 00:51:24.114
it's exposed to the elements.

00:51:24.114 --> 00:51:25.682
And it's such a slower process

00:51:25.682 --> 00:51:27.317
as opposed to, like, a semiautomatic,

00:51:27.317 --> 00:51:30.020
where the bolt is coming
back extremely fast.

00:51:30.020 --> 00:51:33.523
So it's more prone to collect dust

00:51:33.523 --> 00:51:36.993
and things of that nature
that would cause it to jam up.

00:51:36.993 --> 00:51:38.562
Man, this is getting tough.

00:51:38.562 --> 00:51:41.098
I don't know. I'd give it a six because,

00:51:41.098 --> 00:51:43.700
realistically, shooting
at nighttime like that,

00:51:43.700 --> 00:51:45.735
I could see you missing that much.

00:51:49.940 --> 00:51:51.408
I've debated doing this

00:51:51.408 --> 00:51:54.244
when I was pinned down by
a sniper in Afghanistan,

00:51:54.244 --> 00:51:55.245
taking my helmet off.

00:51:55.245 --> 00:51:57.380
If that's your last resort
and all you're trying to do

00:51:57.380 --> 00:51:59.382
is locate a sniper's position,

00:51:59.382 --> 00:52:01.952
I would've probably stuck my helmet up on

00:52:01.952 --> 00:52:05.889
the tip of my gun and dangled
it up, as opposed to throw it.

00:52:05.889 --> 00:52:07.324
I might need that for later.

00:52:07.324 --> 00:52:09.025
But there's no way that enemy sniper

00:52:09.025 --> 00:52:10.160
would've hit that, though.

00:52:15.065 --> 00:52:16.366
Like, back in World War II,

00:52:16.366 --> 00:52:19.469
snipers used to use the trees
to hide in and shoot from,

00:52:19.469 --> 00:52:21.805
and there really is no
benefit to hiding in a tree.

00:52:21.805 --> 00:52:24.808
Once they find you out,
all they have to do is

00:52:24.808 --> 00:52:28.745
center all their guns onto
that tree and chop it down.

00:52:28.745 --> 00:52:33.383
Hiding underneath the tree
or in the hole of the tree,

00:52:33.383 --> 00:52:37.287
where some of them get
hollowed out, I could see that.

00:52:38.255 --> 00:52:40.690
But I'm scared of bugs,
so that's not for me.

00:52:44.794 --> 00:52:47.164
As far as taking the shot when he took it,

00:52:47.164 --> 00:52:49.566
nine times out of 10, if
a sniper's being sent out,

00:52:49.566 --> 00:52:51.468
it's to overwatch his guys

00:52:51.468 --> 00:52:53.737
and make sure nothing happens to them.

00:52:53.737 --> 00:52:55.872
But if we're already engaged in targets

00:52:55.872 --> 00:52:57.674
and we're already being shot at,

00:52:57.674 --> 00:53:00.477
then anything that's
moving on that opposite end

00:53:00.477 --> 00:53:04.347
that is a threat or has a
weapon is going to get shot.

00:53:04.347 --> 00:53:05.782
So, yeah, he had every right

00:53:05.782 --> 00:53:08.385
to pop a few rounds off at this guy.

00:53:08.385 --> 00:53:09.486
I would have too.

00:53:09.486 --> 00:53:10.754
I'd give it a nine,

00:53:10.754 --> 00:53:14.424
just because I've been in
a situation where I debated

00:53:14.424 --> 00:53:16.927
taking my helmet off
and using it as a decoy.

00:53:24.868 --> 00:53:26.169
Isaac: I don't f---ing know!

00:53:26.169 --> 00:53:29.673
So, I was an on-set tech
advisor on this film

00:53:29.673 --> 00:53:32.342
and also played the dead guy

00:53:32.342 --> 00:53:35.045
that he got the radio from.

00:53:35.045 --> 00:53:36.546
Should've won an award for it.

00:53:36.546 --> 00:53:38.014
Best dead guy ever.

00:53:38.014 --> 00:53:40.317
But that sand was real.

00:53:40.317 --> 00:53:42.719
We were really in the
desert, and it was brutal.

00:53:42.719 --> 00:53:45.855
It's extremely hard to aim through,

00:53:45.855 --> 00:53:47.390
we call it a brownout,

00:53:47.390 --> 00:53:51.394
where it's just, like, a semi,
little, I guess dust storm

00:53:51.394 --> 00:53:53.296
that rose, so you can't see anything.

00:53:53.296 --> 00:53:57.500
So he's definitely not going
to be able to make a shot,

00:53:57.500 --> 00:53:59.669
especially at the distances
that he's going for

00:53:59.669 --> 00:54:03.373
with that much dust blowing
around, obscuring his vision.

00:54:03.373 --> 00:54:05.308
Isaac: Give me max plus four!

00:54:07.577 --> 00:54:09.179
Six mils right!

00:54:09.179 --> 00:54:10.914
I'm not going to say it's impossible

00:54:10.914 --> 00:54:12.515
to take out an enemy sniper

00:54:12.515 --> 00:54:14.417
who's hiding among stuff like that.

00:54:14.417 --> 00:54:16.586
The hardest thing the good sniper

00:54:16.586 --> 00:54:18.388
is going to have to do is locate him.

00:54:18.388 --> 00:54:19.322
From what I remember,

00:54:19.322 --> 00:54:23.426
I recall it being over 1,000 meters away,

00:54:23.426 --> 00:54:25.895
which is extremely, extremely far

00:54:25.895 --> 00:54:28.531
to be conducting a
countersniper operation.

00:54:35.171 --> 00:54:38.575
The sound of the rifle
that John Cena is firing,

00:54:38.575 --> 00:54:41.411
in the movie is not how
it sounds in real life.

00:54:41.411 --> 00:54:45.148
Like, this sounds like
a semiautomatic rifle

00:54:45.148 --> 00:54:47.584
with a suppressor on it
or something like that.

00:54:47.584 --> 00:54:50.887
In real life, that gun is
just a big thunderous boom,

00:54:50.887 --> 00:54:52.088
a big bang.

00:54:52.088 --> 00:54:53.456
The way he was shooting in the movie,

00:54:53.456 --> 00:54:56.159
it's kind of like it's a semiautomatic,

00:54:56.159 --> 00:54:59.296
as opposed to him cranking
the bolt back every time,

00:54:59.296 --> 00:55:00.297
and the sound is off.

00:55:00.297 --> 00:55:01.731
Did you get him?

00:55:04.501 --> 00:55:06.569
Please tell me you got him --

00:55:06.569 --> 00:55:08.271
Yeah, he was dead from the get-go.

00:55:08.271 --> 00:55:10.607
Like, shooting in an open area

00:55:10.607 --> 00:55:13.076
and a sniper has eyes on you,

00:55:13.076 --> 00:55:14.878
there's really nothing you can do about it

00:55:14.878 --> 00:55:16.713
unless you are going to get up and just

00:55:16.713 --> 00:55:18.481
take a chance and run for it.

00:55:18.481 --> 00:55:20.250
Just crawling away slowly wouldn't work.

00:55:20.250 --> 00:55:21.451
He's just going to watch you crawl

00:55:21.451 --> 00:55:23.586
and decide when to shoot anyways.

00:55:23.586 --> 00:55:25.588
So it's a lose-lose.

00:55:25.588 --> 00:55:29.592
This scene here, I don't
think I worked that day,

00:55:29.592 --> 00:55:31.161
so [laughs].

00:55:31.161 --> 00:55:32.896
No, I only have so much control,

00:55:32.896 --> 00:55:36.333
but this scene I would rate, like, a ...

00:55:36.333 --> 00:55:38.068
oh my, I'm doing this to myself.

00:55:38.068 --> 00:55:39.302
I'm going to give it a five.

00:55:45.075 --> 00:55:47.310
I don't know if I would be
comfortable with taking that shot

00:55:47.310 --> 00:55:50.547
through the windshield
portion of a helicopter.

00:55:50.547 --> 00:55:52.582
Bullets act funny when they hit objects,

00:55:52.582 --> 00:55:53.983
and the first object

00:55:53.983 --> 00:55:55.652
that bullet is going to hit is that glass,

00:55:55.652 --> 00:55:56.986
and because it's curved,

00:55:56.986 --> 00:55:59.823
it's going to deflect the bullet
up or down, left or right.

00:55:59.823 --> 00:56:02.926
There's really no determining factor

00:56:02.926 --> 00:56:04.761
as to where that bullet's
going to land at.

00:56:04.761 --> 00:56:08.698
So one shot, one kill for
that helicopter scene,

00:56:08.698 --> 00:56:09.666
not so much.

00:56:13.937 --> 00:56:14.938
From underneath the car,

00:56:14.938 --> 00:56:16.272
that's actually a good thing to do.

00:56:16.272 --> 00:56:19.109
I've shot from underneath various vehicles

00:56:19.109 --> 00:56:20.677
and structures and stuff like that.

00:56:20.677 --> 00:56:23.480
Especially in an urban environment,

00:56:23.480 --> 00:56:25.115
you're going to encounter so much debris

00:56:25.115 --> 00:56:28.118
and rubble on the battlefield
or where you're fighting from,

00:56:28.118 --> 00:56:30.353
so you have to make do with what you have.

00:56:30.353 --> 00:56:34.891
The only issue was her hand
placement on the rifle.

00:56:34.891 --> 00:56:36.726
Like, she already has the bipods.

00:56:36.726 --> 00:56:39.896
I don't see a need to have her hand

00:56:39.896 --> 00:56:41.798
underneath it supporting it as well.

00:56:41.798 --> 00:56:45.268
It's just extra weight
dangling for no reason.

00:56:45.268 --> 00:56:48.838
I would've probably had
my support hand closer

00:56:48.838 --> 00:56:52.108
or holding the buttstock
into my shoulder pocket

00:56:52.108 --> 00:56:53.176
as opposed to that.

00:56:57.981 --> 00:57:01.618
During sniper school,
we were always told to

00:57:01.618 --> 00:57:04.654
call in an airstrike when
up against another sniper,

00:57:04.654 --> 00:57:08.725
because it's really tough to
take out or fight against.

00:57:08.725 --> 00:57:11.428
I believe she's shooting,
like, a 7.62-caliber round,

00:57:11.428 --> 00:57:13.396
which is well within the capabilities

00:57:13.396 --> 00:57:15.265
of taking out a target at that distance.

00:57:15.265 --> 00:57:19.369
The odds of it being dead
on at whatever range he was

00:57:19.369 --> 00:57:21.805
would've meant that she would've had to

00:57:21.805 --> 00:57:23.773
know that distance ahead of time

00:57:23.773 --> 00:57:27.577
and kind of dial in her scope for that.

00:57:27.577 --> 00:57:29.078
Instead, she just stuck it on there

00:57:29.078 --> 00:57:30.847
and pulled the trigger on the head.

00:57:30.847 --> 00:57:32.115
It's not really like that.

00:57:32.115 --> 00:57:34.184
But other than that, I
think she did pretty good.

00:57:34.184 --> 00:57:35.218
Given the circumstances,

00:57:35.218 --> 00:57:36.886
I would've probably done the same thing.

00:57:36.886 --> 00:57:38.154
But I'd give it an eight.

00:57:38.154 --> 00:57:39.389
She looked really calm behind it.

00:57:39.389 --> 00:57:41.658
And some of the best
snipers I know are female.

00:57:50.066 --> 00:57:51.401
Ooh.

00:57:51.401 --> 00:57:54.971
Yeah, so this movie is based
off of an actual sniper,

00:57:54.971 --> 00:57:56.339
Ms. Pavlichenko.

00:57:56.339 --> 00:57:58.908
She was credited with over 309 kills,

00:57:58.908 --> 00:58:01.444
and as far as putting that

00:58:01.444 --> 00:58:04.180
bullet through the tank
like that, I don't know.

00:58:04.180 --> 00:58:07.016
I've only aimed at a tank one
time, and it was by accident,

00:58:07.016 --> 00:58:09.419
and I was quickly reminded,
don't do that again,

00:58:09.419 --> 00:58:11.454
when they pointed the big cannon my way.

00:58:17.827 --> 00:58:19.295
She went into her bubble.

00:58:19.295 --> 00:58:22.065
Carlos Hathcock, a Marine Corps sniper,

00:58:22.065 --> 00:58:24.601
called it getting into his bubble.

00:58:24.601 --> 00:58:27.804
That's what she's doing
when all these mortar shells

00:58:27.804 --> 00:58:30.206
and explosions are going off around her,

00:58:30.206 --> 00:58:31.474
is getting into that bubble.

00:58:31.474 --> 00:58:35.278
Being able to shut that
outside world off of the chaos

00:58:35.278 --> 00:58:37.847
and calming down and ignoring everything

00:58:37.847 --> 00:58:39.215
to pull off a shot.

00:58:39.215 --> 00:58:41.451
I can relate to that 110%.

00:58:45.288 --> 00:58:49.492
No. So, bullets don't travel
in a straight line like that.

00:58:49.492 --> 00:58:52.495
They travel at an arc, even at 100 yards.

00:58:52.495 --> 00:58:56.666
So to win that battle,
you have to aim higher,

00:58:56.666 --> 00:58:58.835
causing the bullet to go up

00:58:58.835 --> 00:59:02.171
and hit its apex point and fall back down.

00:59:02.171 --> 00:59:06.543
All snipers are just like
really good NFL quarterbacks.

00:59:06.543 --> 00:59:09.846
He's throwing it up and they're
falling into the target,

00:59:09.846 --> 00:59:11.814
and bullets work the same exact way.

00:59:11.814 --> 00:59:14.017
Because it was based on a true story,

00:59:14.017 --> 00:59:15.084
I'll give it a nine,

00:59:15.084 --> 00:59:18.388
not a 10 just because
the bullet trajectory.

00:59:18.388 --> 00:59:22.825
Females, women make the
best snipers out there.

00:59:22.825 --> 00:59:23.726
New shot.

00:59:23.726 --> 00:59:25.128
Team: Abandon selective targeting.

00:59:25.128 --> 00:59:26.462
All targets are now free.

00:59:26.462 --> 00:59:30.667
The distances he's shooting
from, that's very plausible.

00:59:30.667 --> 00:59:32.335
Here it looks like he's

00:59:32.335 --> 00:59:37.307
maybe at most 150, 100 yards away.

00:59:37.307 --> 00:59:39.242
Less than that, because
he's shooting down,

00:59:39.242 --> 00:59:43.212
so less than 50 yards possibly.

00:59:43.212 --> 00:59:45.148
The scope is not bad either.

00:59:45.148 --> 00:59:47.483
I'm used to mil-dot scopes

00:59:47.483 --> 00:59:49.752
and Horus reticles,

00:59:49.752 --> 00:59:52.889
and they look like busy Christmas trees

00:59:52.889 --> 00:59:55.124
without the lights on
inside of sniper scopes.

00:59:55.124 --> 00:59:56.426
It was a very simple reticle.

01:00:02.532 --> 01:00:05.668
Shooting in a crowded
environment is extremely tough.

01:00:05.668 --> 01:00:08.705
During the International Sniper
Competition that I attended,

01:00:08.705 --> 01:00:10.540
that was one of the challenges,

01:00:10.540 --> 01:00:14.377
was to shoot moving
targets amongst a crowd.

01:00:14.377 --> 01:00:16.279
It's extremely hard to do,

01:00:16.279 --> 01:00:18.548
but our distances were way further

01:00:18.548 --> 01:00:21.851
than what he appears to be
shooting, by, like, a lot.

01:00:21.851 --> 01:00:24.921
At the angles he was
given and the distances,

01:00:24.921 --> 01:00:27.023
it's not that complicated.

01:00:27.023 --> 01:00:29.792
Just worrying about where
the bullet's going to hit

01:00:29.792 --> 01:00:33.162
after you hit your target
would be my only concern.

01:00:33.162 --> 01:00:35.932
Because they're in so close
proximity to each other,

01:00:35.932 --> 01:00:37.934
he has a possibility of getting hit too.

01:00:37.934 --> 01:00:39.702
I would give it a solid eight.

01:00:39.702 --> 01:00:41.537
It's highly realistic.

01:00:47.777 --> 01:00:49.112
The scopes are always messed up.

01:00:49.112 --> 01:00:50.613
I don't understand why they

01:00:50.613 --> 01:00:53.216
just don't put a real
scope reticle in there.

01:00:53.216 --> 01:00:56.586
So, this is a actual spotting scope,

01:00:56.586 --> 01:00:58.721
similar to what the individual

01:00:58.721 --> 01:01:00.757
or the sniper's using in the movie.

01:01:00.757 --> 01:01:02.158
And he's using it pretty correctly,

01:01:02.158 --> 01:01:05.061
like, he's using it for target detection,

01:01:05.061 --> 01:01:07.463
to identify where a threat may be.

01:01:07.463 --> 01:01:12.068
He noticed a little bullet
on the ledge of that window

01:01:12.068 --> 01:01:13.436
and then, you know,

01:01:13.436 --> 01:01:14.937
finding things that were out of place,

01:01:14.937 --> 01:01:15.905
that didn't make sense,

01:01:15.905 --> 01:01:18.808
and eventually finding
where the sniper was.

01:01:18.808 --> 01:01:21.644
It's something you see rare in movies,

01:01:21.644 --> 01:01:23.546
the actual use of target detection

01:01:23.546 --> 01:01:25.748
and how important it is for a sniper

01:01:25.748 --> 01:01:27.183
to find the smallest details

01:01:27.183 --> 01:01:28.251
and things that are out of place

01:01:28.251 --> 01:01:30.687
to find who they're looking for.

01:01:35.425 --> 01:01:38.728
What you want to do is be
pushed back away from the hole

01:01:38.728 --> 01:01:41.564
and not put the barrel
protruding outside of the hole.

01:01:41.564 --> 01:01:44.000
Gives your position away and
alleviates that whole purpose

01:01:44.000 --> 01:01:46.436
of why you made that
hole in the first place.

01:01:46.436 --> 01:01:47.437
I'll give it a seven.

01:01:47.437 --> 01:01:49.405
I love the use of target detection,

01:01:49.405 --> 01:01:52.442
but he messed up putting
the barrel through the hole.

01:01:52.442 --> 01:01:54.377
Hey, everyone, Jay Ly here.

01:01:54.377 --> 01:01:58.047
13-year Navy explosive
ordnance disposal, or EOD, vet.

01:01:58.047 --> 01:01:59.749
Did four combat deployments,

01:01:59.749 --> 01:02:02.852
three to Afghanistan
and one to East Africa.

01:02:08.458 --> 01:02:10.860
That thing is apparently
the Cadillac of trolleys.

01:02:10.860 --> 01:02:14.764
Can I say 100% that this
wouldn't be entertained?

01:02:14.764 --> 01:02:17.433
No, because it might, but what I do know

01:02:17.433 --> 01:02:21.637
is there are probably a dozen other ways

01:02:21.637 --> 01:02:26.142
to attack this device from inside the bus.

01:02:26.142 --> 01:02:27.443
I mean, something rudimentary

01:02:27.443 --> 01:02:28.611
as getting a stick with some film,

01:02:28.611 --> 01:02:30.513
so we can take an X-ray of the device

01:02:30.513 --> 01:02:32.315
so we can actually see what's going on.

01:02:32.315 --> 01:02:35.084
Harry: Jack, I'm going to
need you to look at the wire.

01:02:36.719 --> 01:02:37.787
Harry, it's covered.

01:02:37.787 --> 01:02:40.389
He is talking on a radio and/or a phone

01:02:40.389 --> 01:02:41.691
right next to this thing.

01:02:41.691 --> 01:02:43.292
It's electronically initiated.

01:02:43.292 --> 01:02:44.393
They don't know if that energy

01:02:44.393 --> 01:02:47.964
will cause that device to fire or not.

01:02:47.964 --> 01:02:51.534
So that whole thing is
not realistic whatsoever.

01:02:55.271 --> 01:02:57.406
I can't bypass! It'll fire.

01:02:57.406 --> 01:02:59.509
S---, it's a collapsible circuit.

01:02:59.509 --> 01:03:01.978
Yeah, collapsing circuits are real.

01:03:01.978 --> 01:03:04.313
There's two or more detonators,

01:03:04.313 --> 01:03:06.949
and when you cut one,
the other one will fire.

01:03:06.949 --> 01:03:08.518
It's common for us to train to that.

01:03:08.518 --> 01:03:12.221
It's very unlikely to encounter that.

01:03:12.221 --> 01:03:14.223
In, well, at least in
Afghanistan, in my experience,

01:03:14.223 --> 01:03:16.826
we didn't encounter a lot
of collapsing circuits.

01:03:16.826 --> 01:03:18.094
It was mainly pressure
plates, stuff like that.

01:03:18.094 --> 01:03:19.962
Very simple circuits.

01:03:19.962 --> 01:03:23.466
Officer: Howard Payne,
Atlanta PD bomb squad.

01:03:23.466 --> 01:03:25.234
Jack, I want you to get
out of there and sit tight.

01:03:25.234 --> 01:03:26.469
We're going to go after the source.

01:03:26.469 --> 01:03:30.306
No, they should 100% do
something to this device

01:03:30.306 --> 01:03:33.910
and not just solely rely on
the bomber helping them out.

01:03:33.910 --> 01:03:35.178
There are tools we have

01:03:35.178 --> 01:03:38.114
to disrupt an IED and a component,

01:03:38.114 --> 01:03:39.982
so it'll disrupt the firing train.

01:03:39.982 --> 01:03:42.985
It'll disrupt all the circuitry

01:03:42.985 --> 01:03:44.954
and separate components remotely,

01:03:44.954 --> 01:03:47.089
and then we'll go down,
check the disruption,

01:03:47.089 --> 01:03:49.458
and then separate components
further and do our thing.

01:03:49.458 --> 01:03:51.828
Jack, I believe he is

01:03:51.828 --> 01:03:55.031
a bomb-disposal officer with the LAPD.

01:03:55.031 --> 01:03:57.733
He should know these
component-recognition things

01:03:57.733 --> 01:04:00.670
and be able to identify
how this device works,

01:04:00.670 --> 01:04:01.904
or at least have a good idea of it.

01:04:01.904 --> 01:04:05.341
This definitely gets a zero
because he knows better,

01:04:05.341 --> 01:04:06.475
and he should have taken an action.

01:04:06.475 --> 01:04:08.010
He should have brought a
tool down there with him.

01:04:08.010 --> 01:04:09.645
He could have taped it right next to it

01:04:09.645 --> 01:04:12.014
and disrupted that
thing right off the bus.

01:04:12.014 --> 01:04:15.117
We got under three minutes
left. What do I do first?

01:04:15.117 --> 01:04:18.454
Pressure sensors. I
activated it when I sat down.

01:04:18.454 --> 01:04:20.556
If I get up, my car explodes.

01:04:20.556 --> 01:04:24.160
This is already hokey because he sat down,

01:04:24.160 --> 01:04:27.630
and now it's armed. And there's a timer.

01:04:27.630 --> 01:04:32.134
In movies and TV, they often
incorporate these big timers

01:04:32.134 --> 01:04:33.836
where it's counting
down to the detonation.

01:04:33.836 --> 01:04:37.773
But in all reality, it's mainly

01:04:37.773 --> 01:04:40.309
a safe-and-arm-type mechanism.

01:04:40.309 --> 01:04:44.046
If I'm a bomb emplacer, I
need to be able emplace it

01:04:44.046 --> 01:04:45.514
and then safely get away.

01:04:45.514 --> 01:04:46.349
So there's a timer,

01:04:46.349 --> 01:04:48.484
but you're not going to
see this giant clock.

01:04:48.484 --> 01:04:51.053
It's like, "You have five
minutes before you die."

01:04:51.053 --> 01:04:52.922
Orange, yellow, green.

01:04:52.922 --> 01:04:55.324
Colors don't matter. What
I need you to do is tell me

01:04:55.324 --> 01:04:57.827
which one is hot to the touch.

01:04:59.795 --> 01:05:01.831
The warm wire versus cold wires,

01:05:01.831 --> 01:05:04.233
you can make a circuit
with the proper resistance,

01:05:04.233 --> 01:05:06.002
so none of the wires are warm.

01:05:06.002 --> 01:05:09.805
So, like, the movie
tropes of the warm wire

01:05:09.805 --> 01:05:13.743
or the colors of the
wires is a total farce.

01:05:13.743 --> 01:05:16.646
I can go to Home Depot, Lowe's,
whichever hardware store,

01:05:16.646 --> 01:05:19.115
your hardware store of choice,

01:05:19.115 --> 01:05:23.719
get any color wire I
want, and put it in here.

01:05:23.719 --> 01:05:27.156
Take the small box out of
the kit and turn it on.

01:05:28.724 --> 01:05:30.526
That kit probably looked like something

01:05:30.526 --> 01:05:32.028
I would pull tools from

01:05:32.028 --> 01:05:34.330
in a special-operations-forces
environment.

01:05:34.330 --> 01:05:36.399
From my experience in Afghanistan,

01:05:36.399 --> 01:05:38.935
it was all special operations forces

01:05:38.935 --> 01:05:40.536
or naval special warfare.

01:05:40.536 --> 01:05:43.839
We flew in helicopters somewhere, landed,

01:05:43.839 --> 01:05:45.541
and ran to a target, right,

01:05:45.541 --> 01:05:49.145
or walked a great distance
to get to a target.

01:05:49.145 --> 01:05:51.681
You really want to limit
what you have on your body.

01:05:51.681 --> 01:05:53.316
So, yeah, we're not going
to be carrying around

01:05:53.316 --> 01:05:55.584
briefcases and backpacks
just full of tools.

01:05:58.988 --> 01:06:00.556
Just cut it in the middle.

01:06:00.556 --> 01:06:03.092
The orange wire between the two clamps.

01:06:06.095 --> 01:06:10.399
I don't want to get into
that specifically in detail

01:06:10.399 --> 01:06:14.537
because that is how we
attack certain things.

01:06:14.537 --> 01:06:15.938
But yeah, those clamps,

01:06:15.938 --> 01:06:20.276
they're for a very specific
purpose for multiple detonators,

01:06:20.276 --> 01:06:23.980
and you would need another
item that he doesn't have there

01:06:23.980 --> 01:06:28.684
to do diagnostic work
on that type of device.

01:06:28.684 --> 01:06:30.753
You would need a little bit more time,

01:06:30.753 --> 01:06:32.755
I mean, a lot more training
than just him saying,

01:06:32.755 --> 01:06:34.190
"Oh, yeah, clamp it on the warm wire

01:06:34.190 --> 01:06:35.591
and then cut in between it."

01:06:35.591 --> 01:06:36.826
There's a lot of testing you have to do

01:06:36.826 --> 01:06:37.994
before you can cut a wire.

01:06:37.994 --> 01:06:40.529
But yeah, the whole setting,
him sitting down in it,

01:06:40.529 --> 01:06:43.232
arming it, and then
having this opportunity

01:06:43.232 --> 01:06:45.668
to render it safe, yeah, you wish.

01:06:45.668 --> 01:06:47.403
So yeah, we'll give him a
two. We'll give him a two.

01:06:54.043 --> 01:06:56.712
How it was set up and how it
was in place, totally legit.

01:06:56.712 --> 01:06:57.680
He had a Claymore.

01:06:57.680 --> 01:07:00.783
He put it on top of the Grand
Slam bomb, huge British bomb.

01:07:00.783 --> 01:07:03.486
Claymores are used all
over. They're already wired.

01:07:03.486 --> 01:07:06.489
They're premade to be
set up as booby traps.

01:07:06.489 --> 01:07:09.892
That's what they're for. So
he just had the trip wire,

01:07:09.892 --> 01:07:11.827
put something that he thought

01:07:11.827 --> 01:07:15.131
one of these people chasing
him, idiots, would grab.

01:07:20.636 --> 01:07:23.305
There are 100% bomb-detection dogs,

01:07:23.305 --> 01:07:25.841
and in this clip, those dogs, I believe,

01:07:25.841 --> 01:07:28.377
are going for human detection.

01:07:28.377 --> 01:07:30.479
They're going for sniffing out Rambo,

01:07:30.479 --> 01:07:33.783
not necessarily sniff out
explosive hazards in the woods.

01:07:33.783 --> 01:07:36.619
And even if they were, those
dogs are running full speed.

01:07:36.619 --> 01:07:39.055
That's like essentially
you trying to sniff

01:07:39.055 --> 01:07:41.857
a hot dog in the corner while
sprinting down the hallway,

01:07:41.857 --> 01:07:44.160
like hyperventilating essentially.

01:07:44.160 --> 01:07:45.461
It's never going to happen.

01:07:50.533 --> 01:07:52.234
Would the blast wave be that extended?

01:07:52.234 --> 01:07:55.638
I mean, it was drawn
out to have that effect.

01:07:55.638 --> 01:07:58.174
The explosive component
in there is torpex.

01:07:58.174 --> 01:08:02.978
They added aluminum into the
composition of that explosive

01:08:02.978 --> 01:08:04.680
to extend the blast.

01:08:04.680 --> 01:08:08.951
But with that being said,
that's like a nuclear blast wave

01:08:08.951 --> 01:08:11.287
that is just kind of continuing to go out.

01:08:11.287 --> 01:08:14.423
It would be really big and done.

01:08:20.529 --> 01:08:23.165
That blast wave would've
killed a lesser man.

01:08:23.165 --> 01:08:24.300
Not John Rambo.

01:08:24.300 --> 01:08:26.902
The explosive component
in there is torpex.

01:08:26.902 --> 01:08:30.539
It's about 50% stronger than
TNT. And that's only the blast.

01:08:30.539 --> 01:08:34.243
Frag goes exponentially
further than blast does.

01:08:34.243 --> 01:08:36.245
So yeah, he is definitely within that.

01:08:36.245 --> 01:08:39.381
This is probably the most realistic clip

01:08:39.381 --> 01:08:42.818
as far as how things function
and how people think.

01:08:42.818 --> 01:08:45.187
Man, I might give "Rambo" an eight.

01:08:45.187 --> 01:08:46.789
He's knocked two because of survival.

01:08:46.789 --> 01:08:49.391
That's hokey at best, even for John Rambo.

01:08:49.391 --> 01:08:50.893
I got it!

01:08:50.893 --> 01:08:54.997
It's a cylinder cipher! D-U-N-N.

01:08:54.997 --> 01:08:57.466
It's my last name. It knows who I am.

01:08:57.466 --> 01:08:58.901
If someone is just going to make

01:08:58.901 --> 01:09:01.470
an improvised nuclear
device like this cipher,

01:09:01.470 --> 01:09:02.605
anything is possible.

01:09:02.605 --> 01:09:07.309
There's many ways that
nuclear weapons are armed.

01:09:07.309 --> 01:09:09.011
Can't get into any specifics.

01:09:09.011 --> 01:09:10.045
There's state-sponsored,

01:09:10.045 --> 01:09:11.780
and then there's non-state-sponsored.

01:09:11.780 --> 01:09:12.681
State-sponsored are going to have

01:09:12.681 --> 01:09:14.250
all kinds of different protocols in place,

01:09:14.250 --> 01:09:16.418
safeties, what have you, arming sequences.

01:09:16.418 --> 01:09:18.888
Non-state-sponsored, if someone is just

01:09:18.888 --> 01:09:20.523
going to make an improvised nuclear device

01:09:20.523 --> 01:09:23.292
like this cipher, it can be
armed when it hears your voice,

01:09:23.292 --> 01:09:25.227
when my voice, whatever the case may be.

01:09:25.227 --> 01:09:27.029
Benji: I speak without a mouth.

01:09:27.029 --> 01:09:30.432
I fly through the air
without wings. What am I?

01:09:30.432 --> 01:09:31.267
An echo.

01:09:32.134 --> 01:09:33.002
That's it! That's it, that's it.

01:09:33.002 --> 01:09:36.038
Ugh. I can't. I can't even with this one.

01:09:36.038 --> 01:09:37.573
This is ... he's talking to it.

01:09:37.573 --> 01:09:40.276
How does he know if he
answers this question,

01:09:40.276 --> 01:09:41.477
it's not going to go off?

01:09:46.482 --> 01:09:47.316
It's empty.

01:09:47.316 --> 01:09:49.952
Apparently it was a mud suck, right?

01:09:49.952 --> 01:09:53.088
A hoax IED or a hoax device.

01:09:53.088 --> 01:09:56.992
In real life, hoaxes are actually
very common, very common,

01:09:56.992 --> 01:09:59.495
especially in Iraq and Afghanistan.

01:09:59.495 --> 01:10:01.263
They were utilized

01:10:01.263 --> 01:10:05.067
as intelligence-gathering
devices, essentially.

01:10:05.067 --> 01:10:07.002
They'd put it in the road. Totally fake.

01:10:07.002 --> 01:10:11.540
Just something that would
key up an EOD response,

01:10:11.540 --> 01:10:13.943
and then they would be watching

01:10:13.943 --> 01:10:17.146
and videotaping the whole procedure.

01:10:17.146 --> 01:10:20.349
Despite all of the craziness
that was in the scene,

01:10:20.349 --> 01:10:23.185
using a hoax to gather
intelligence on people

01:10:23.185 --> 01:10:25.721
and to learn more about
them, absolutely true.

01:10:25.721 --> 01:10:27.656
We'll give this one a eight,
up there with "Rambo."

01:10:33.329 --> 01:10:34.396
Oh, God.

01:10:34.396 --> 01:10:36.932
We would clown this movie
so much in training.

01:10:36.932 --> 01:10:38.601
Like, we would just
pretend we're this dude

01:10:38.601 --> 01:10:40.569
and just do the most outlandish s---

01:10:40.569 --> 01:10:42.371
because that's exactly what he did.

01:10:42.371 --> 01:10:43.939
He's attacking a VBIED,

01:10:43.939 --> 01:10:46.775
which is a vehicle-borne
improvised explosive device.

01:10:46.775 --> 01:10:49.812
Walking up to a VBIED without a tool,

01:10:49.812 --> 01:10:52.514
an explosive tool, an
energetic tool, or anything,

01:10:52.514 --> 01:10:57.152
not even a pole line or anything
to prosecute this vehicle,

01:10:57.152 --> 01:10:58.053
is just dumb.

01:10:58.053 --> 01:11:00.789
He went downrange in a
bomb suit with a crowbar.

01:11:00.789 --> 01:11:02.258
I mean, cool.

01:11:02.258 --> 01:11:03.559
They would probably send a robot down

01:11:03.559 --> 01:11:04.893
with a tool first, right?

01:11:04.893 --> 01:11:06.962
Especially because yeah,
that's a lot of HE.

01:11:06.962 --> 01:11:09.198
A VBIED is no joke.

01:11:09.198 --> 01:11:12.334
I mean, even the guys on the
wall, his security element,

01:11:12.334 --> 01:11:14.136
they'd probably die if
that thing went off.

01:11:16.372 --> 01:11:17.873
What the hell is he doing?

01:11:20.075 --> 01:11:21.210
Dead.

01:11:22.578 --> 01:11:23.612
Dead.

01:11:23.612 --> 01:11:27.049
He could have died a half
dozen to a dozen ways

01:11:27.049 --> 01:11:29.451
in this clip alone.

01:11:29.451 --> 01:11:30.686
Not to mention the whole movie.

01:11:30.686 --> 01:11:33.956
Kicking it, randomly
opening the trunk by hand,

01:11:33.956 --> 01:11:36.692
opening the hood by hand,
turning the windshield wipers on,

01:11:36.692 --> 01:11:38.661
yanking random wires underneath the hood.

01:11:38.661 --> 01:11:41.163
Like, any one of those could
have set that thing off.

01:11:41.163 --> 01:11:43.565
Yeah, I think he removed his headset.

01:11:43.565 --> 01:11:45.668
Please put your headset back on.

01:11:47.703 --> 01:11:50.906
The most realistic thing in
there was the radio thing.

01:11:50.906 --> 01:11:52.808
He actually took his headset
off so he could focus

01:11:52.808 --> 01:11:54.943
and get his thought process going.

01:11:54.943 --> 01:11:57.179
And then when they asked
him to put it back on,

01:11:57.179 --> 01:11:58.981
yeah, that's probably the
response that you'd get

01:11:58.981 --> 01:12:00.382
from an EOD tech.

01:12:06.755 --> 01:12:10.292
We definitely render safe IEDs by hand.

01:12:10.292 --> 01:12:13.495
I've done it,

01:12:13.495 --> 01:12:15.531
and it's not ideal.

01:12:15.531 --> 01:12:17.166
We want to go as remote as possible

01:12:17.166 --> 01:12:19.868
because of the risk of
something going off.

01:12:19.868 --> 01:12:23.372
But yeah, he could have done
a lot more with his hands,

01:12:23.372 --> 01:12:25.074
like cut that cord,

01:12:25.074 --> 01:12:26.709
detonating cord right out of the gate.

01:12:26.709 --> 01:12:28.944
I forgot how many 155s,

01:12:28.944 --> 01:12:30.813
but that's what was in
the trunk of that car

01:12:30.813 --> 01:12:32.848
once he saw the main charge,

01:12:32.848 --> 01:12:35.517
and that's what he should
be going after anyway.

01:12:35.517 --> 01:12:37.052
Just reckless abandon.

01:12:37.052 --> 01:12:39.288
Yeah, it's totally Hollywooded up.

01:12:39.288 --> 01:12:42.424
The only realistic thing was
the communication aspect of it.

01:12:42.424 --> 01:12:45.394
He gets a one. One out of 10.

01:12:50.799 --> 01:12:52.401
Three miles underwater,

01:12:52.401 --> 01:12:56.004
the water's crystal clear,
so I mean, that's ...

01:12:57.039 --> 01:12:58.574
no, there's no way.

01:12:58.574 --> 01:13:01.844
My fourth team, I was on a
mine-countermeasure team,

01:13:01.844 --> 01:13:03.579
but it was underwater sea mines

01:13:03.579 --> 01:13:06.382
and underwater IEDs and stuff like that.

01:13:06.382 --> 01:13:08.617
This is supposed to be a nuke

01:13:08.617 --> 01:13:11.186
that has fallen into the abyss.

01:13:11.186 --> 01:13:14.823
It's a big deal when we go
to 200 feet of seawater.

01:13:14.823 --> 01:13:17.359
We're definitely not going to
do free ascent to the bottom.

01:13:17.359 --> 01:13:18.694
There's always descent lines.

01:13:18.694 --> 01:13:20.696
We establish a safe area.

01:13:20.696 --> 01:13:22.564
Yeah, he doesn't have any
search tools whatsoever.

01:13:22.564 --> 01:13:26.969
He just has his eyeballs and
a flare, which is insane.

01:13:26.969 --> 01:13:28.170
And that's just the diving aspect,

01:13:28.170 --> 01:13:29.938
so we haven't even gotten
to the EOD part of it.

01:13:31.874 --> 01:13:32.708
Hiram: He made it.

01:13:32.708 --> 01:13:33.809
Oh, my God.

01:13:33.809 --> 01:13:35.644
We'll go step by step.

01:13:35.644 --> 01:13:37.479
The communication is totally wazoo.

01:13:37.479 --> 01:13:39.314
It's three miles underwater.

01:13:39.314 --> 01:13:42.551
What RF signal can penetrate

01:13:42.551 --> 01:13:44.820
three miles' worth of water?

01:13:44.820 --> 01:13:48.123
We do have very rudimentary
ways of communicating,

01:13:48.123 --> 01:13:50.192
i.e., a float on the top of the water

01:13:50.192 --> 01:13:52.361
that's connected to us via a
line, and we'll just pull it,

01:13:52.361 --> 01:13:55.297
and the certain number of
pulls means a certain thing.

01:13:55.297 --> 01:13:57.099
So we communicate like that

01:13:57.099 --> 01:13:59.768
in a bottom-line-centric environment

01:13:59.768 --> 01:14:02.838
where we're taking seismic
and magnetic precautions.

01:14:02.838 --> 01:14:06.241
But yeah, nuke, that is a
totally different ballgame.

01:14:06.241 --> 01:14:09.411
You have to cut the ground
wire, not the lead wire.

01:14:11.513 --> 01:14:13.982
Without getting into any super specifics

01:14:13.982 --> 01:14:16.285
on nuclear-weapons design,

01:14:16.285 --> 01:14:18.854
we would've researched this ordnance item:

01:14:18.854 --> 01:14:20.589
this is exactly how you render it safe,

01:14:20.589 --> 01:14:22.090
if we want to separate components,

01:14:22.090 --> 01:14:23.158
separate the fuse from it.

01:14:23.158 --> 01:14:24.993
Sometimes we do that explosively,

01:14:24.993 --> 01:14:26.595
sometimes we do it mechanically,

01:14:26.595 --> 01:14:29.498
and then we'd just raise it
up and bring it to the beach

01:14:29.498 --> 01:14:30.866
and exploit it on the surface.

01:14:30.866 --> 01:14:34.536
I'd have to give that a big
fat doughnut for realism,

01:14:34.536 --> 01:14:37.506
a zero, because that's not at all

01:14:37.506 --> 01:14:39.341
how nuclear weapons function.

01:14:41.176 --> 01:14:44.279
Here! McIntyre, can you hear me?

01:14:44.279 --> 01:14:46.348
I love this show. It's so funny.

01:14:46.348 --> 01:14:49.117
Unexploded ordnance, UXO.

01:14:49.117 --> 01:14:52.988
Those are very common because
sometimes fuses just dud out,

01:14:52.988 --> 01:14:54.022
and it's still a threat

01:14:54.022 --> 01:14:58.093
because we just don't
know the condition of it.

01:14:58.093 --> 01:14:59.928
Why did it not go off?

01:14:59.928 --> 01:15:02.097
Is it a time? Is it a proximity thing?

01:15:02.097 --> 01:15:05.701
Is it a delay that just
hasn't gone all the way yet?

01:15:05.701 --> 01:15:08.103
So, very much a concern,

01:15:08.103 --> 01:15:09.538
less so for everyone
else that's behind cover,

01:15:09.538 --> 01:15:12.040
but mainly for the guys
that are right on top of it.

01:15:12.040 --> 01:15:14.076
Heartbeat's regular.

01:15:14.076 --> 01:15:16.578
[hissing]

01:15:16.578 --> 01:15:18.213
You spring a leak?

01:15:18.213 --> 01:15:19.314
It stopped ticking.

01:15:19.314 --> 01:15:22.384
It is definitely plausible
to use a stethoscope

01:15:22.384 --> 01:15:26.021
to examine a suspected clockwork fuse.

01:15:26.021 --> 01:15:29.491
Seems hokey, but yeah,
that is old-school legit.

01:15:29.491 --> 01:15:30.325
It was a thing.

01:15:30.325 --> 01:15:31.593
For old bombs like that,

01:15:31.593 --> 01:15:33.962
they had clockwork mechanisms in the fuse

01:15:33.962 --> 01:15:36.298
that you could hear ticking down.

01:15:36.298 --> 01:15:37.599
We don't see that a lot anymore.

01:15:37.599 --> 01:15:40.469
That was a lot of training in school,

01:15:40.469 --> 01:15:41.670
just to get the exposure to it.

01:15:46.341 --> 01:15:48.410
It's a propaganda bomb!

01:15:48.410 --> 01:15:51.747
Leaflets are definitely employed.

01:15:51.747 --> 01:15:56.618
They were dropped in all over Europe

01:15:56.618 --> 01:15:58.921
during World War II.

01:15:58.921 --> 01:16:03.592
They were employed in, I
mean, North Korea, Vietnam.

01:16:03.592 --> 01:16:07.663
Leaflets are definitely a
type of psychological warfare

01:16:07.663 --> 01:16:12.167
or even just a means to
communicate with a population

01:16:12.167 --> 01:16:15.971
that just is confined to an area.

01:16:15.971 --> 01:16:18.273
But yeah, I'd give it a
solid seven for realism.

01:16:18.273 --> 01:16:22.277
Granted, two random people
would not be working this bomb,

01:16:22.277 --> 01:16:24.212
but yeah, so we've gotta
dock it a little bit.

01:16:24.212 --> 01:16:25.213
I just don't know why they didn't have any

01:16:25.213 --> 01:16:26.682
bomb-squad people.

01:16:26.682 --> 01:16:28.083
So, on the side of the timer,

01:16:28.083 --> 01:16:29.618
there should be a serial number.

01:16:29.618 --> 01:16:31.420
That'll tell me which
wire y'all need to cut.

01:16:31.420 --> 01:16:34.690
"Archer" here is showing
an inexperienced person

01:16:34.690 --> 01:16:36.825
being coached through this whole ordeal,

01:16:36.825 --> 01:16:38.794
like many other movies and TV shows.

01:16:38.794 --> 01:16:42.364
It would be almost impossible
unless it was set up

01:16:42.364 --> 01:16:45.000
exactly how the manufacturer
wanted it to be set up.

01:16:45.000 --> 01:16:47.135
If that's the case, totally doable, right?

01:16:47.135 --> 01:16:49.204
I can walk you through
anything. It's like, OK, cool,

01:16:49.204 --> 01:16:51.106
how many wires are there,
what colors are they?

01:16:51.106 --> 01:16:52.007
You ready?

01:16:52.007 --> 01:16:53.008
Lana: God dammit, Archer!

01:16:53.008 --> 01:16:54.042
First letter is B.

01:16:54.042 --> 01:16:54.876
Bravo.

01:16:54.876 --> 01:16:56.211
Thanks. Second letter is --

01:16:56.211 --> 01:16:57.913
M as in Mike?

01:16:57.913 --> 01:17:02.884
Oh, my God, that is so spot
on as far as communications,

01:17:02.884 --> 01:17:05.487
especially when s---'s hitting the fan.

01:17:05.487 --> 01:17:07.155
Oh, I can relate.

01:17:07.155 --> 01:17:09.625
You have radio frequencies,
you have interference,

01:17:09.625 --> 01:17:12.227
you have distance, you have
other things in the way,

01:17:12.227 --> 01:17:14.229
so your signal can be degraded.

01:17:14.229 --> 01:17:16.531
Everyone is getting
ramped up in this episode,

01:17:16.531 --> 01:17:20.902
but in real life, when stuff is going on

01:17:20.902 --> 01:17:25.073
and comms are an issue,
you just really stay calm

01:17:25.073 --> 01:17:27.209
and cool and collected on the radio.

01:17:27.209 --> 01:17:30.846
That way, even if it is a weak signal,

01:17:30.846 --> 01:17:32.714
you can still hear what's being said.

01:17:32.714 --> 01:17:34.650
Archer: I'm doing it, God!

01:17:34.650 --> 01:17:36.418
Lana: Oh, my God. Ray!
Ray: What's going on?

01:17:36.418 --> 01:17:37.886
The timer sped up!

01:17:37.886 --> 01:17:40.922
The trope here with the
green wires being cut

01:17:40.922 --> 01:17:44.893
and speeding up the
clock is just erroneous

01:17:44.893 --> 01:17:47.496
because it makes no sense to
have that feature in there

01:17:47.496 --> 01:17:50.732
unless you're trying to
create some type of anxiety,

01:17:50.732 --> 01:17:52.000
and in real life, trust me,

01:17:52.000 --> 01:17:54.336
if you're faced with a live explosive

01:17:54.336 --> 01:17:56.405
that can detonate at any action,

01:17:56.405 --> 01:18:00.175
keeping that at bay is already
going to be difficult enough.

01:18:00.175 --> 01:18:01.043
So yeah, at the end of the day,

01:18:01.043 --> 01:18:02.711
people want their things to go off.

01:18:02.711 --> 01:18:04.446
So that's how they make them.

01:18:04.446 --> 01:18:05.614
I'll give them a four.

01:18:05.614 --> 01:18:09.851
The communication piece, that
was hilariously accurate.

01:18:09.851 --> 01:18:11.253
Bombs aren't on the same fire line.

01:18:11.253 --> 01:18:12.954
I have to defuse them individually.

01:18:12.954 --> 01:18:13.922
How long is that going to take?

01:18:13.922 --> 01:18:17.859
I gotta find them and then
dismantle them. 30 minutes.

01:18:17.859 --> 01:18:19.261
There's just, like, a chunk

01:18:19.261 --> 01:18:22.097
of high explosive taped to a pipe.

01:18:22.097 --> 01:18:23.965
EOD would see that and,
boom, and make a call,

01:18:23.965 --> 01:18:25.634
and everyone would get out of there

01:18:25.634 --> 01:18:26.968
except for one other person,

01:18:26.968 --> 01:18:28.770
him and one other security person,

01:18:28.770 --> 01:18:31.039
and then I'm going to take
immediate action on that device.

01:18:31.039 --> 01:18:33.308
And immediate is immediate.
It's not 30 minutes.

01:18:38.914 --> 01:18:41.116
He was using a mirror for whatever reason.

01:18:41.116 --> 01:18:44.352
I'm not going to use an inspection mirror

01:18:44.352 --> 01:18:46.988
when I have my eyeballs and I
can see all the way around it.

01:18:46.988 --> 01:18:48.423
So yeah, that's kind of hokey.

01:18:48.423 --> 01:18:51.660
He's got NODs on his helmet,
so that's cumbersome,

01:18:51.660 --> 01:18:52.961
especially when he's working on that one

01:18:52.961 --> 01:18:54.896
that was at head level.
His NODs could hit it,

01:18:54.896 --> 01:18:57.432
and if there's an anti-move
thing on there, boom.

01:18:57.432 --> 01:18:59.167
He just functioned it, as designed.

01:18:59.167 --> 01:19:02.104
So I would take my helmet
off, so there's nothing,

01:19:02.104 --> 01:19:04.706
no obstructions that could
potentially hit this device.

01:19:11.980 --> 01:19:13.348
If he's the only one there,

01:19:13.348 --> 01:19:14.282
yeah, that's what he's got to do.

01:19:14.282 --> 01:19:15.884
It's because it's threat assessment

01:19:15.884 --> 01:19:17.419
of what's going to kill me right now.

01:19:17.419 --> 01:19:19.321
That's an enemy combatant.

01:19:19.321 --> 01:19:21.423
He's probably going to
kill me if he sees me.

01:19:21.423 --> 01:19:23.325
So that needs to be taken care of first.

01:19:23.325 --> 01:19:26.495
But yeah, he would have

01:19:26.495 --> 01:19:29.998
at least one shooter with
him a little bit away,

01:19:29.998 --> 01:19:34.302
so when I'm working, I
can be focused on this.

01:19:34.302 --> 01:19:37.339
They could've classed it up
a little bit, but all good.

01:19:39.441 --> 01:19:40.475
Bingo.

01:19:40.475 --> 01:19:41.943
He did use ceramic scissors at the end.

01:19:41.943 --> 01:19:43.178
I thought that was a nice touch. Granted,

01:19:43.178 --> 01:19:45.714
he used ceramic scissors
instead of diagonal cutters,

01:19:45.714 --> 01:19:47.849
and typically when wires
are coiled up like that,

01:19:47.849 --> 01:19:49.684
there's two of them, and he cut them both.

01:19:49.684 --> 01:19:51.419
He could have smooshed the two wires

01:19:51.419 --> 01:19:54.956
that are conductive
together and set it off.

01:19:54.956 --> 01:19:56.458
Yeah, there's a lot of hokey stuff.

01:19:56.458 --> 01:19:58.627
So yeah, it's gotta be low. Low three.

01:20:00.295 --> 01:20:02.397
Towards the general
direction of the mountain.

01:20:02.397 --> 01:20:05.100
The robot rarely finds
what you're looking for.

01:20:05.100 --> 01:20:08.436
They're in Helmand, which,
I was also in Helmand.

01:20:08.436 --> 01:20:09.838
Helmand Province in Afghanistan.

01:20:09.838 --> 01:20:11.807
And yeah, robots are awesome.

01:20:11.807 --> 01:20:13.608
I didn't see the robot
go down with a tool.

01:20:13.608 --> 01:20:15.210
Tool in, tool out, that's what we do.

01:20:15.210 --> 01:20:17.479
That's what's preached
from day one in EOD school.

01:20:17.479 --> 01:20:19.748
The robot has cutters
on it, it has grippers,

01:20:19.748 --> 01:20:21.850
it can separate components,
whatever the case may be.

01:20:21.850 --> 01:20:25.487
And so if it did find something,
it could countercharge it

01:20:25.487 --> 01:20:27.189
or they could set it down.

01:20:30.625 --> 01:20:31.459
No!

01:20:32.294 --> 01:20:34.629
At the end of the day, that
is what a robot is for.

01:20:34.629 --> 01:20:36.464
They're expendable devices.

01:20:36.464 --> 01:20:38.099
If worst comes to worst,

01:20:38.099 --> 01:20:39.868
they find something by functioning it,

01:20:39.868 --> 01:20:41.469
it blows up, not a human being.

01:20:41.469 --> 01:20:45.407
The entire team was standing
out in front of the vehicles.

01:20:45.407 --> 01:20:48.109
That's totally not what would happen.

01:20:48.109 --> 01:20:51.313
They would be inside their
vehicle driving this robot.

01:20:51.313 --> 01:20:54.082
That way, you have mitigation

01:20:54.082 --> 01:20:56.718
from fragmentation coming at you.

01:20:56.718 --> 01:21:00.155
At least be behind the
vehicle, for God's sake.

01:21:00.155 --> 01:21:04.726
I need you to keep your head still.

01:21:05.727 --> 01:21:06.561
Don't nod.

01:21:08.730 --> 01:21:10.365
This is a clothespin switch.

01:21:10.365 --> 01:21:14.803
Very old and very rudimentary,
very easy to defeat.

01:21:14.803 --> 01:21:17.105
Nick, the EOD operator,
should have seen that.

01:21:17.105 --> 01:21:20.642
He identified that it's
a pull. Cool. Cut it.

01:21:20.642 --> 01:21:22.377
Just cut it and be done with it.

01:21:22.377 --> 01:21:24.846
But he did the thing of holding his head.

01:21:24.846 --> 01:21:27.115
You tell someone that
they're connected to a line

01:21:27.115 --> 01:21:28.950
with a bunch of explosives,
they're going to freak out.

01:21:28.950 --> 01:21:30.452
That's one of the rare times

01:21:30.452 --> 01:21:32.587
where you can actually attack a switch.

01:21:32.587 --> 01:21:33.722
It's a very clear cut.

01:21:41.963 --> 01:21:43.765
That could have been
handled way differently,

01:21:43.765 --> 01:21:45.734
and then everyone would've survived.

01:21:45.734 --> 01:21:49.437
It's tough. A lot of EOD
procedures and tactics

01:21:49.437 --> 01:21:51.339
are written in blood, just like this.

01:21:51.339 --> 01:21:52.173
The survivors are like,

01:21:52.173 --> 01:21:53.642
OK, this is exactly what they encountered.

01:21:53.642 --> 01:21:55.277
Over the radio, this is what was said.

01:21:55.277 --> 01:21:57.712
Now we have our new set of TTPs

01:21:57.712 --> 01:21:59.080
so it doesn't happen again.

01:21:59.080 --> 01:22:01.349
I'll give this one of
five. It's got some stuff,

01:22:01.349 --> 01:22:05.954
some super-hokey stuff,
but all in all, not bad.

01:22:05.954 --> 01:22:06.721
John: What are you doing?

01:22:06.721 --> 01:22:08.423
Batman: I can get it out over the bay.

01:22:13.695 --> 01:22:16.464
The plausibility of having a bomb

01:22:16.464 --> 01:22:20.302
that is unable to be rendered safe,

01:22:20.302 --> 01:22:22.804
it's all a matter of time, really.

01:22:22.804 --> 01:22:26.808
If time is not an object,

01:22:26.808 --> 01:22:30.478
then yeah, you can spend
the time and the diligence

01:22:30.478 --> 01:22:34.449
and really get it disassembled
to where it's demil'd.

01:22:34.449 --> 01:22:36.918
So now you have to weigh,

01:22:36.918 --> 01:22:41.389
can I render this safe in a
minute and 30 seconds or not?

01:22:41.389 --> 01:22:43.558
And the answer's probably no, right?

01:22:43.558 --> 01:22:45.093
Because especially with a device

01:22:45.093 --> 01:22:47.262
as sophisticated as
that was supposed to be,

01:22:47.262 --> 01:22:49.264
your best case is you have a minute and 30

01:22:49.264 --> 01:22:51.366
to get this as far away
from everyone as possible.

01:22:58.306 --> 01:23:01.543
Yeah, water has a great tamping effect

01:23:01.543 --> 01:23:03.278
because it cannot compress.

01:23:03.278 --> 01:23:06.815
It really depends on what
the actual explosive,

01:23:06.815 --> 01:23:08.850
the nuclear yield of this bomb was

01:23:08.850 --> 01:23:10.785
and how deep it was able to go.

01:23:10.785 --> 01:23:13.922
The mission of EOD is protection
of personnel and property.

01:23:13.922 --> 01:23:18.093
So yeah, getting that device
out in a way, moving it,

01:23:18.093 --> 01:23:21.529
relocating it, even if
it's to your own demise,

01:23:21.529 --> 01:23:25.033
is what we would do

01:23:25.033 --> 01:23:28.203
for the sake of protecting
personnel and property.

01:23:28.203 --> 01:23:30.739
Is it a nine? Can Batman be a nine?

01:23:30.739 --> 01:23:31.873
I'll give it a nine.

01:23:31.873 --> 01:23:35.110
I like to think his actions were genuine

01:23:35.110 --> 01:23:36.845
of Navy EOD.

01:23:36.845 --> 01:23:38.480
Hey, my name is Dakata Brodie.

01:23:38.480 --> 01:23:40.348
I'm a 21-year military veteran.

01:23:40.348 --> 01:23:42.784
I served 13 years in the US Coast Guard

01:23:42.784 --> 01:23:44.352
as a search-and-rescue-helicopter pilot

01:23:44.352 --> 01:23:45.954
and eight years in the Army

01:23:45.954 --> 01:23:48.356
as a UH-60 Black Hawk helicopter pilot.

01:23:49.724 --> 01:23:52.127
All right. Tuck them in, boys.

01:23:52.127 --> 01:23:53.762
For the Coast Guard, primarily,

01:23:53.762 --> 01:23:56.331
we face offshore and going off shores

01:23:56.331 --> 01:23:58.033
to effect most of our rescues.

01:23:58.033 --> 01:23:59.834
However, the Coast Guard does train

01:23:59.834 --> 01:24:02.170
for vertical-surface rescues,

01:24:02.170 --> 01:24:03.571
which is what you're seeing right here.

01:24:03.571 --> 01:24:05.874
Cliffside, cliff rescues,
and everything else.

01:24:05.874 --> 01:24:08.009
Just stay perfectly still. I
have to stabilize the car, OK?

01:24:08.009 --> 01:24:09.110
Hurry!

01:24:09.110 --> 01:24:10.311
Rescuer: Car secured.

01:24:14.749 --> 01:24:17.719
All right. If that is the hoist hook,

01:24:17.719 --> 01:24:20.555
you are in what you call
a committed situation.

01:24:20.555 --> 01:24:24.259
Every helo pilot would be
shaken at this point right now

01:24:24.259 --> 01:24:26.995
knowing that the hoist hook
is attached to a vehicle

01:24:26.995 --> 01:24:27.896
that could fall off.

01:24:27.896 --> 01:24:30.498
Not happening. We're not
connecting up to any vehicle.

01:24:30.498 --> 01:24:33.001
Joby, I'm coming down.

01:24:33.001 --> 01:24:34.202
Harrison, you're flying.

01:24:36.538 --> 01:24:38.940
So, a pilot going to the back seat?

01:24:38.940 --> 01:24:41.109
First of all, you know how
much stuff you can knock off?

01:24:41.109 --> 01:24:43.244
You could literally turn
off both of your engines.

01:24:43.244 --> 01:24:46.081
The fact that your vest
and everything could snag

01:24:46.081 --> 01:24:47.382
and you could flip things off.

01:24:47.382 --> 01:24:50.418
We actually had a person
turn with their NVGs

01:24:50.418 --> 01:24:51.686
sitting on top of their head

01:24:51.686 --> 01:24:53.788
and actually hit one of the engines

01:24:53.788 --> 01:24:55.657
and knock an engine offline with that.

01:24:55.657 --> 01:24:57.592
So, yeah, it's very possible

01:24:57.592 --> 01:24:59.727
to actually turn off
something inadvertently

01:24:59.727 --> 01:25:02.931
just climbing and trying to
get to the back of the cabin.

01:25:02.931 --> 01:25:04.065
Pilot: Out the door.

01:25:06.101 --> 01:25:08.336
Natalie, don't you worry. I'm
going to get you out of here!

01:25:08.336 --> 01:25:10.905
If I am outside the helicopter,

01:25:10.905 --> 01:25:12.507
we have a real bad problem.

01:25:12.507 --> 01:25:15.009
As a pilot, I am never outside.

01:25:16.111 --> 01:25:17.712
I got you!

01:25:17.712 --> 01:25:18.680
Cut it!

01:25:18.680 --> 01:25:19.881
Joby, swing!

01:25:23.485 --> 01:25:24.786
They were trying to show, like,

01:25:24.786 --> 01:25:27.088
the hoist was holding the truck up, right?

01:25:27.088 --> 01:25:29.290
The hoist that you have
on there about 600 pounds.

01:25:29.290 --> 01:25:31.593
So it's not your primary
means of holding up anything.

01:25:31.593 --> 01:25:35.096
At that point that it was
smoking, it was straining so hard.

01:25:35.096 --> 01:25:37.565
Now, there is a squib that
you could flip the switch

01:25:37.565 --> 01:25:40.802
to actually cut and
shear that hoist cable,

01:25:40.802 --> 01:25:42.937
which is what they were
doing at that point.

01:25:42.937 --> 01:25:46.341
So somehow they sheared the one cable,

01:25:46.341 --> 01:25:50.245
and The Rock and the other
person was on a different cable.

01:25:50.245 --> 01:25:51.513
I don't know what that is.

01:25:51.513 --> 01:25:54.115
I'll say that our helicopter has one cable

01:25:54.115 --> 01:25:55.150
that we're going to operate with.

01:25:55.150 --> 01:25:56.317
Once that cable gets sheared,

01:25:56.317 --> 01:25:57.852
everything is going down with it.

01:25:57.852 --> 01:25:59.821
It's not going to happen
on so many different levels

01:25:59.821 --> 01:26:00.688
on this clip.

01:26:00.688 --> 01:26:02.423
This right here is a zero.

01:26:02.423 --> 01:26:03.558
Pilot: Where are they?

01:26:03.558 --> 01:26:05.059
Tanker's on the way.

01:26:05.059 --> 01:26:07.295
Air Force rescue 231.

01:26:07.295 --> 01:26:09.664
I don't know why nobody wears NVGs.

01:26:09.664 --> 01:26:12.700
Everybody has NVGs on,
night-vision goggles on,

01:26:12.700 --> 01:26:14.836
in the helicopters. Nobody wears them.

01:26:14.836 --> 01:26:17.138
If you have the option of having them on,

01:26:17.138 --> 01:26:18.373
yeah, you're going to have them on.

01:26:23.845 --> 01:26:25.680
The fuel probe that you just saw,

01:26:25.680 --> 01:26:28.650
that's not a standard piece
of equipment on helicopters.

01:26:28.650 --> 01:26:30.151
There's just certain helicopters

01:26:30.151 --> 01:26:33.021
that are either doing
some long-range rescues.

01:26:33.021 --> 01:26:34.856
Most helicopters do not have that.

01:26:41.429 --> 01:26:42.330
Pilot: No!

01:26:42.330 --> 01:26:46.034
Your biggest thing at
that point is if you miss,

01:26:46.034 --> 01:26:48.736
where's that tube that's
going that's flowing fuel,

01:26:48.736 --> 01:26:50.605
if you're going to cut that
thing with the main rotor,

01:26:50.605 --> 01:26:53.074
you're talking about damage
to the main rotor system.

01:26:53.074 --> 01:26:55.210
You'd be in a bad spot at that point.

01:27:01.216 --> 01:27:03.551
Looking right now at a lightning strike.

01:27:03.551 --> 01:27:06.387
Knock on wood and thank God
I've never had that happen.

01:27:06.387 --> 01:27:08.756
You risk frying a lot of electronics,

01:27:08.756 --> 01:27:10.658
engine-control systems that are on there.

01:27:10.658 --> 01:27:13.027
You're hoping that your
engines are staying online.

01:27:13.027 --> 01:27:15.496
You would probably lose
a lot of electronic

01:27:15.496 --> 01:27:18.166
AC and DC systems inside the helicopter,

01:27:18.166 --> 01:27:20.034
but you should be able to keep flying

01:27:20.034 --> 01:27:23.338
unless you got hit in the
tail rotor or something.

01:27:28.543 --> 01:27:30.511
Every year, the Coast Guard has to do

01:27:30.511 --> 01:27:31.913
what's called dunker training.

01:27:31.913 --> 01:27:34.782
So we get flipped over
in a chair in a pool,

01:27:34.782 --> 01:27:36.584
which simulates having to ditch.

01:27:36.584 --> 01:27:37.485
You're rolled upside down.

01:27:37.485 --> 01:27:40.021
You have to do multiple different egresses

01:27:40.021 --> 01:27:41.756
to show that you have the proficiency

01:27:41.756 --> 01:27:43.992
to be able to get out of
a submerged helicopter.

01:27:43.992 --> 01:27:46.160
Every single year, and
then every six years,

01:27:46.160 --> 01:27:47.629
unless they've changed
it, but every six years

01:27:47.629 --> 01:27:49.130
you would go to actual school,

01:27:49.130 --> 01:27:51.132
where you go through the
whole mock-up simulator

01:27:51.132 --> 01:27:53.101
of a device going in and rolling over.

01:27:53.101 --> 01:27:54.669
So this is a part of the business.

01:28:00.742 --> 01:28:02.977
He's got SEAs bottle or HEEDs bottle,

01:28:02.977 --> 01:28:07.782
which gives you about two to
three minutes at normal breath.

01:28:07.782 --> 01:28:11.252
In that mode right there, you
have about 10 to 15 seconds

01:28:11.252 --> 01:28:12.687
if they're breathing the
way I think they are.

01:28:12.687 --> 01:28:14.956
But the pure shock of going in

01:28:14.956 --> 01:28:16.424
and the water temperature itself

01:28:16.424 --> 01:28:17.925
is going to take the breath out of you.

01:28:17.925 --> 01:28:19.894
But I do like the realistic aspect

01:28:19.894 --> 01:28:22.130
of seeing somebody grab that SEAs bottle

01:28:22.130 --> 01:28:23.431
and put that thing in their mouth.

01:28:29.504 --> 01:28:31.005
Now, for everybody that flies,

01:28:31.005 --> 01:28:32.573
we have emergency jettison handles.

01:28:32.573 --> 01:28:34.442
So the normal exits are no longer

01:28:34.442 --> 01:28:35.877
going to get you out of the door.

01:28:35.877 --> 01:28:38.579
It's too much pressure that's
on that door at that point.

01:28:38.579 --> 01:28:40.748
So you're literally hitting
your emergency jettison

01:28:40.748 --> 01:28:43.184
like it's blowing cartridges,
and the door pops off.

01:28:43.184 --> 01:28:45.920
It just is lifting up the
actual door-hinge pins

01:28:45.920 --> 01:28:47.955
out of the way so you can create

01:28:47.955 --> 01:28:49.857
that full opening at that point.

01:28:49.857 --> 01:28:53.094
Anybody's going for that,
yeah, you're going to die.

01:28:53.094 --> 01:28:54.962
I'd give this one probably about a six.

01:28:54.962 --> 01:28:56.164
You can lower me down.

01:28:56.164 --> 01:28:57.098
I'll get the two men.

01:28:57.098 --> 01:28:59.634
You return and pick us up on my signal.

01:29:00.635 --> 01:29:03.471
The theatrics on there is amazing.

01:29:03.471 --> 01:29:04.939
They had no business being that close

01:29:04.939 --> 01:29:06.207
to a rig that's exploding.

01:29:06.207 --> 01:29:09.310
So, we've done multiple
rescues in the Gulf of Mexico.

01:29:09.310 --> 01:29:10.511
I was stationed at before.

01:29:10.511 --> 01:29:12.146
So, flying out to rigs,

01:29:12.146 --> 01:29:14.282
rigs typically have burn-offs happening,

01:29:14.282 --> 01:29:15.683
but there's always either gases

01:29:15.683 --> 01:29:16.984
or stuff like that that's coming off.

01:29:16.984 --> 01:29:20.888
So the rigs all have more of
escape-type pods and things

01:29:20.888 --> 01:29:23.324
for the rig workers to be able to get out

01:29:23.324 --> 01:29:25.059
and be able to get off of the rigs,

01:29:25.059 --> 01:29:27.562
and then we would effect the
rescue from the open water.

01:29:27.562 --> 01:29:30.865
To try to go into there again,
we're no good to anybody,

01:29:30.865 --> 01:29:33.401
and it's so much risk
of that flying debris,

01:29:33.401 --> 01:29:36.237
shrapnel going through
the rotor-blade system.

01:29:36.237 --> 01:29:37.672
You're just now a victim.

01:29:37.672 --> 01:29:38.573
You're not going to put yourself

01:29:38.573 --> 01:29:41.008
or the crew in that situation.

01:29:41.008 --> 01:29:42.343
Rescuer: Count five.

01:29:47.248 --> 01:29:48.583
Five.

01:29:48.583 --> 01:29:51.319
Any standard deployment
of the rescue swimmer,

01:29:51.319 --> 01:29:52.754
they're just going down.

01:29:52.754 --> 01:29:54.756
At that point, you just
call it a direct deployment

01:29:54.756 --> 01:29:55.656
of the rescue swimmer.

01:29:55.656 --> 01:29:57.658
Especially when you're deploying somebody

01:29:57.658 --> 01:30:00.061
either to a boat that's underway,

01:30:00.061 --> 01:30:01.095
you'll have them down,

01:30:01.095 --> 01:30:03.264
you'll have them just off
the water or just above.

01:30:03.264 --> 01:30:05.433
But to say that you were
about to send somebody

01:30:05.433 --> 01:30:09.470
through a flame, a blowtorch in essence,

01:30:09.470 --> 01:30:10.371
not gonna happen.

01:30:11.572 --> 01:30:12.774
Rescuer: One!

01:30:18.880 --> 01:30:20.581
When somebody is on the cable,

01:30:20.581 --> 01:30:23.818
the hook is actually locked
just to prevent any mishap

01:30:23.818 --> 01:30:25.853
to where it could inadvertently come open.

01:30:25.853 --> 01:30:28.289
For somebody to pirouette

01:30:28.289 --> 01:30:30.958
and unhook themself off of the cable

01:30:30.958 --> 01:30:33.027
at the same time, that's pretty amazing.

01:30:33.027 --> 01:30:34.796
Nobody would be that crazy

01:30:34.796 --> 01:30:37.999
to even attempt to put
somebody in that situation.

01:30:37.999 --> 01:30:39.300
The rescue swimmer would come up

01:30:39.300 --> 01:30:41.202
and slap everybody in the helicopter

01:30:41.202 --> 01:30:43.070
for even putting them down
in something like that.

01:30:43.070 --> 01:30:44.939
So absolutely not.

01:30:44.939 --> 01:30:47.742
It's completely
unrealistic. That's a zero.

01:30:51.879 --> 01:30:53.181
Pilot: Swimmers away!

01:30:57.051 --> 01:30:58.553
When he jumps from the helicopter,

01:30:58.553 --> 01:31:01.689
that's a free-fall deployment
of the rescue swimmer.

01:31:01.689 --> 01:31:03.391
That is reserved for daytime only,

01:31:03.391 --> 01:31:06.694
so we would've only hoisted
the rescue swimmer down

01:31:06.694 --> 01:31:09.730
at that point, not let him
jump from the helicopter.

01:31:09.730 --> 01:31:13.367
They're seeing, like, 20-foot
swells or something like that.

01:31:13.367 --> 01:31:15.169
That means the helicopter is hovering

01:31:15.169 --> 01:31:17.004
at least 30 to 40 feet.

01:31:17.004 --> 01:31:20.942
You misjudge that swell, that's
a four- or five-story drop.

01:31:20.942 --> 01:31:23.444
That water turns into a solid real quick,

01:31:23.444 --> 01:31:25.379
especially if that was cold temperatures.

01:31:26.681 --> 01:31:28.483
I have the ready-for-pickup signal.

01:31:28.483 --> 01:31:30.818
Basket is going out of the cabin door.

01:31:31.752 --> 01:31:33.321
Basket is in the water.

01:31:33.321 --> 01:31:35.022
It's a standard rescue basket.

01:31:35.022 --> 01:31:37.592
He's calling for the basket,
has the ready-for-pickup sign.

01:31:37.592 --> 01:31:38.826
That's exactly what you would hear,

01:31:38.826 --> 01:31:40.328
because we're standing by,

01:31:40.328 --> 01:31:42.430
keeping our light in the
direction of the swimmer,

01:31:42.430 --> 01:31:44.432
kind of making sure we
always have sight on them.

01:31:44.432 --> 01:31:45.533
And as they're getting ready,

01:31:45.533 --> 01:31:46.901
you're going to try to place the device

01:31:46.901 --> 01:31:48.970
really within about
five to 10 feet of them

01:31:48.970 --> 01:31:50.438
once they're calling for it.

01:31:50.438 --> 01:31:52.573
So very, very real.

01:31:52.573 --> 01:31:53.875
Pilot: How we looking on fuel there?

01:31:53.875 --> 01:31:55.710
Crew: Bingo, we're at bingo. Gotta go.

01:31:55.710 --> 01:31:57.645
We're out of time. We gotta go, Carl.

01:31:57.645 --> 01:32:00.648
That "bingo" fuel, that's a real term.

01:32:00.648 --> 01:32:03.150
It just means if you leave now,

01:32:03.150 --> 01:32:05.019
you will land with a fuel reserve

01:32:05.019 --> 01:32:06.454
when you get back at your station.

01:32:06.454 --> 01:32:08.256
Anything that's less than there,

01:32:08.256 --> 01:32:10.591
you can be at risk of either not making it

01:32:10.591 --> 01:32:13.227
to your destination or
airport or flaming out.

01:32:13.227 --> 01:32:16.097
It's always a race against
how much fuel you have.

01:32:18.266 --> 01:32:21.002
Go, go! Behind you!

01:32:21.002 --> 01:32:22.470
Hurry!

01:32:22.470 --> 01:32:24.071
When you're seeing the winds,

01:32:24.071 --> 01:32:26.140
that rotor wash, it's beating down on you,

01:32:26.140 --> 01:32:27.742
it's a mini hurricane underneath it.

01:32:27.742 --> 01:32:30.611
There is no communicating
with the swimmer in the water

01:32:30.611 --> 01:32:32.813
at that point in time.
All hand-arm signals.

01:32:32.813 --> 01:32:34.348
That would be insane to think

01:32:34.348 --> 01:32:35.850
that you're going to hear a person,

01:32:35.850 --> 01:32:38.819
a swimmer in the water
underneath a spinning helicopter.

01:32:38.819 --> 01:32:40.087
Not gonna happen on there.

01:32:40.087 --> 01:32:41.589
Holy s---!

01:32:43.658 --> 01:32:45.526
We lost our tail rotor!

01:32:48.696 --> 01:32:51.566
The waves out there, that's
in the Arctic, kind of,

01:32:51.566 --> 01:32:53.668
where I believe that
scene was taking place.

01:32:53.668 --> 01:32:56.771
20-, 30-foot seas, and even
having rogue waves can happen,

01:32:56.771 --> 01:32:58.706
but you try to be above that.

01:32:58.706 --> 01:33:00.908
The explosion, that's the Hollywood part.

01:33:00.908 --> 01:33:04.612
I've seen helicopters crash on
tarmacs and different things,

01:33:04.612 --> 01:33:06.047
and I haven't seen one explode.

01:33:06.047 --> 01:33:08.816
Even in the Army, I saw one take a SA-7

01:33:08.816 --> 01:33:09.917
and hit the engine out.

01:33:09.917 --> 01:33:11.886
That was the only one
I saw that was on fire.

01:33:11.886 --> 01:33:13.254
I would give it a seven.

01:33:13.254 --> 01:33:14.956
It's the Coast Guard.

01:33:14.956 --> 01:33:16.691
They must have picked
up the distress call.

01:33:16.691 --> 01:33:18.893
Pilot: Swimmers ready. Deploy swimmers.

01:33:22.463 --> 01:33:24.732
So far right now, you're
seeing pretty much

01:33:24.732 --> 01:33:26.801
a legitimate helicopter rescue.

01:33:26.801 --> 01:33:30.304
It's daytime. You can free-fall
the swimmer in daytime.

01:33:30.304 --> 01:33:31.772
Their altitude was good,

01:33:31.772 --> 01:33:33.841
where the swimmer could safely go out.

01:33:33.841 --> 01:33:35.910
The swimmer's going to have
their goggles, everything down.

01:33:35.910 --> 01:33:38.179
They're not going to
free-fall with their gear up.

01:33:38.179 --> 01:33:40.081
They go in the water, they're
going to lose all their gear.

01:33:40.081 --> 01:33:40.948
So they're going down.

01:33:40.948 --> 01:33:42.617
They're going to cover
their mask and everything

01:33:42.617 --> 01:33:43.484
as they're going in.

01:33:46.187 --> 01:33:48.189
The device going down and being placed

01:33:48.189 --> 01:33:50.725
about five feet from them in the water,

01:33:50.725 --> 01:33:53.494
that's just textbook Coast Guard

01:33:53.494 --> 01:33:55.262
rescue-swimmer operations on there.

01:34:01.002 --> 01:34:02.370
When you have baywatch,

01:34:02.370 --> 01:34:04.772
that's really the LA
County's fire department.

01:34:04.772 --> 01:34:08.509
They're aka baywatch here
in the Los Angeles area.

01:34:08.509 --> 01:34:09.844
We hoist with them routinely.

01:34:09.844 --> 01:34:10.978
The rescue swimmer could easily

01:34:10.978 --> 01:34:12.446
just bring them over to the vessel.

01:34:12.446 --> 01:34:14.782
The fire department, they're
trained paramedics as well.

01:34:14.782 --> 01:34:17.284
Primarily, you're going
to try to put people in

01:34:17.284 --> 01:34:20.821
with the least-risky rescue method,

01:34:20.821 --> 01:34:22.723
which would be to put them on the boat,

01:34:22.723 --> 01:34:24.825
unless they had sustained something else

01:34:24.825 --> 01:34:26.694
that they needed immediate transport,

01:34:26.694 --> 01:34:27.795
bring them in a helicopter

01:34:27.795 --> 01:34:29.664
and transport them to
the nearest hospital.

01:34:29.664 --> 01:34:32.566
But the rescue in itself,
that's pretty textbook

01:34:32.566 --> 01:34:34.068
for what you could see

01:34:34.068 --> 01:34:36.570
if you're going to free-fall
the rescue swimmer, yes.

01:34:36.570 --> 01:34:39.707
It's a nine, just because
of how the rescue swimmer

01:34:39.707 --> 01:34:42.543
had his goggles and
stuff on top of his head.

01:34:48.249 --> 01:34:49.483
I love the precision flying of that.

01:34:49.483 --> 01:34:53.187
This is the MH-68 or AW109, the Stingray.

01:34:53.187 --> 01:34:55.022
Really what you'll call for HITRON,

01:34:55.022 --> 01:34:57.692
which is our helicopters
tactics interdiction.

01:34:57.692 --> 01:35:00.094
It's a specialized unit that we have that

01:35:00.094 --> 01:35:03.631
primarily their focus is
interdicting either drug smugglers

01:35:03.631 --> 01:35:05.933
or anything else like that
in international waters.

01:35:05.933 --> 01:35:08.269
Mostly dealing with drug smuggling,

01:35:08.269 --> 01:35:09.570
what you'll call a go-fast.

01:35:09.570 --> 01:35:11.038
It'll typically outrun a boat.

01:35:11.038 --> 01:35:12.506
Your normal cruise is somewhere around

01:35:12.506 --> 01:35:15.009
the 120 to 130, 130 knots,

01:35:15.009 --> 01:35:17.845
if you really got a good
tailwind and stuff like that.

01:35:20.214 --> 01:35:21.615
Watch out, he's got a weapon on board.

01:35:21.615 --> 01:35:22.683
Bail out! Bail out!

01:35:25.453 --> 01:35:26.754
I've never been at HITRON.

01:35:26.754 --> 01:35:30.591
I did fly airborne use of
force with the Coast Guard.

01:35:30.591 --> 01:35:32.093
A lot of times what you're trying to do is

01:35:32.093 --> 01:35:33.694
we're going to try to stay outside

01:35:33.694 --> 01:35:36.230
of what they have on board of there

01:35:36.230 --> 01:35:38.432
and make sure we can at least maximize

01:35:38.432 --> 01:35:40.234
the use of our weapons systems.

01:35:40.234 --> 01:35:42.036
Coast Guard, take them down.

01:35:46.507 --> 01:35:48.342
They went for the kill shots right there.

01:35:48.342 --> 01:35:49.910
So, normally they're going to probably

01:35:49.910 --> 01:35:52.012
put some stitches in the water.

01:35:52.012 --> 01:35:53.848
They say "stitches in the
water" because when you see it,

01:35:53.848 --> 01:35:56.851
it looks like stitches from
the machine gun going out.

01:35:56.851 --> 01:35:59.453
That's kind of just to escalate the level

01:35:59.453 --> 01:36:01.689
of the use of force at that point

01:36:01.689 --> 01:36:03.691
before they try to shoot the engines out

01:36:03.691 --> 01:36:08.195
and really are more so trying
to just disable the vessel.

01:36:08.195 --> 01:36:09.730
That's primarily what they're doing, yeah,

01:36:09.730 --> 01:36:11.198
is disabling the vessel.

01:36:11.198 --> 01:36:13.100
So I'd give them about a seven.

01:36:17.838 --> 01:36:20.474
At any point in time you're committed,

01:36:20.474 --> 01:36:22.243
you have something that's attached

01:36:22.243 --> 01:36:24.111
to something that can pull you down,

01:36:24.111 --> 01:36:26.614
that's just the worst situation
for a helicopter, right?

01:36:26.614 --> 01:36:30.584
So you're just at the mercy
of being on that pivot point

01:36:30.584 --> 01:36:32.353
and just completely helpless.

01:36:39.293 --> 01:36:41.562
Wet, slippery hand conditions,

01:36:41.562 --> 01:36:43.898
trying to hold on to a
ladder with adrenaline

01:36:43.898 --> 01:36:45.232
and the amount of rotor wash.

01:36:45.232 --> 01:36:46.367
It's a mini hurricane down there.

01:36:46.367 --> 01:36:48.536
So you're holding on
with quite a bit of grip.

01:36:48.536 --> 01:36:50.371
So I'm pretty sure you're almost probably

01:36:50.371 --> 01:36:52.606
bear-hugging the ladder at any point

01:36:52.606 --> 01:36:54.208
just to try to maintain your grip.

01:36:54.208 --> 01:36:55.843
We're going to stall!

01:36:55.843 --> 01:36:57.111
Cut it now!

01:36:57.111 --> 01:36:57.945
Mercenary: We have a job!

01:36:57.945 --> 01:37:00.848
Pilot: We're going to
die here! I said cut it!

01:37:00.848 --> 01:37:03.317
They were actually doing
well talking as a crew

01:37:03.317 --> 01:37:04.752
for saying get rid of it.

01:37:04.752 --> 01:37:05.820
That's a tough call.

01:37:05.820 --> 01:37:08.389
It sounds easy to watch
the movie and say that,

01:37:08.389 --> 01:37:11.625
but that is a really
hard call to say shear.

01:37:11.625 --> 01:37:13.294
Even when we're getting ready to start

01:37:13.294 --> 01:37:15.830
any hoist and rescues,
like, "Hey, if this happens,

01:37:15.830 --> 01:37:18.599
you'll say 'shear' three
times, safety power system

01:37:18.599 --> 01:37:20.901
or the flight mech, you
race for that shear button."

01:37:20.901 --> 01:37:23.737
It was an eight for how dynamic
that environment would be.

01:37:30.611 --> 01:37:32.513
The stretcher. We call it a litter.

01:37:32.513 --> 01:37:33.781
Anytime we're going to use a litter,

01:37:33.781 --> 01:37:36.584
we're going to use a trail
line to help stabilize,

01:37:36.584 --> 01:37:40.187
because that flat plane
surface that's underneath

01:37:40.187 --> 01:37:42.456
with all the rotor wash that's down below

01:37:42.456 --> 01:37:43.791
will really have a good propensity

01:37:43.791 --> 01:37:45.826
to have somebody start
spinning really quick.

01:37:51.632 --> 01:37:56.103
I'm not sure what was causing
the pilot to struggle so much.

01:37:56.103 --> 01:37:59.473
He's got the whole ship to
look at as far as in reference.

01:37:59.473 --> 01:38:01.041
Power really wasn't an issue.

01:38:01.041 --> 01:38:03.477
It almost looked like they
were making the hoist cable

01:38:03.477 --> 01:38:07.014
feel like it was somehow taking
power away from the engines.

01:38:07.014 --> 01:38:07.848
I don't know.

01:38:14.088 --> 01:38:16.023
Having a fouled cable or watching

01:38:16.023 --> 01:38:19.560
a cable actually birdcage,
that part is real.

01:38:19.560 --> 01:38:22.196
That can happen, so it could
go in there and not be.

01:38:22.196 --> 01:38:24.398
You can't let out some
more cable at that point.

01:38:34.408 --> 01:38:37.344
I don't understand why the
pilot was struggling so bad.

01:38:37.344 --> 01:38:38.279
I'm not sure.

01:38:38.279 --> 01:38:40.381
At this point, what's got a hold of them

01:38:40.381 --> 01:38:42.383
is some good bait that's
clearly under there

01:38:42.383 --> 01:38:43.951
for whatever is eating on them.

01:38:43.951 --> 01:38:46.987
So there's no need to take
the whole helicopter down.

01:38:46.987 --> 01:38:48.255
Shear, shear, shear.

01:38:48.255 --> 01:38:50.190
We'll have to preserve the
lives of the rest of the crew.

01:38:50.190 --> 01:38:51.525
There's nothing else you could do.

01:38:51.525 --> 01:38:53.527
This is more of a three.

01:39:00.834 --> 01:39:03.304
Please don't shoot the
flare at the helicopter.

01:39:03.304 --> 01:39:06.106
We see the flare in
the distance. Trust us.

01:39:06.106 --> 01:39:08.375
If the flare gets on the raft,
it'll start eating through.

01:39:08.375 --> 01:39:10.044
It's, like, a phosphor material,

01:39:10.044 --> 01:39:12.379
so you're shooting hot phosphorus

01:39:12.379 --> 01:39:14.081
at the thing that's trying to rescue you.

01:39:14.081 --> 01:39:15.582
You're trying to take us down with you

01:39:15.582 --> 01:39:16.717
at that point in time.

01:39:16.717 --> 01:39:19.520
The flare is meant to be
seen, not to be caught.

01:39:19.520 --> 01:39:21.355
We just want to see the flare go up

01:39:21.355 --> 01:39:22.957
and then find the point of origin

01:39:22.957 --> 01:39:24.591
so we can come and effect the rescue.

01:39:30.030 --> 01:39:32.366
A lot of times, if we're going out,

01:39:32.366 --> 01:39:34.301
it's more likely just one helicopter.

01:39:34.301 --> 01:39:35.469
I'm not saying you can't have two.

01:39:35.469 --> 01:39:37.504
If it's a longshore rescue as well,

01:39:37.504 --> 01:39:39.840
there's a fixed wing asset overhead

01:39:39.840 --> 01:39:41.108
that will probably go out and do

01:39:41.108 --> 01:39:43.544
really good radar sweeps
inside of the area

01:39:43.544 --> 01:39:45.346
and try to help us. And
they'll also be able

01:39:45.346 --> 01:39:48.849
to drop and deliver any
type of rescue materials.

01:39:48.849 --> 01:39:50.684
Certain helicopters do have radar systems,

01:39:50.684 --> 01:39:52.553
so we're trying to either
see a blip on the radar,

01:39:52.553 --> 01:39:55.289
or we're trying to map the
shoreline when we're out there

01:39:55.289 --> 01:39:56.991
in really poor weather conditions.

01:40:01.762 --> 01:40:03.263
Helicopters have a few different lights.

01:40:03.263 --> 01:40:05.165
So, each pilot have their own searchlight,

01:40:05.165 --> 01:40:06.934
and then a lot of times you'll operate

01:40:06.934 --> 01:40:08.268
with what's called a Nightsun.

01:40:08.268 --> 01:40:11.071
So it's just a really powerful searchlight

01:40:11.071 --> 01:40:12.973
towards the middle or
the back of the cabin.

01:40:12.973 --> 01:40:17.011
This right here is probably a 9.5.

01:40:17.011 --> 01:40:18.312
Hi, I'm John Spencer.

01:40:18.312 --> 01:40:20.047
I'm the chair of urban warfare studies

01:40:20.047 --> 01:40:21.382
at the Modern War Institute.

01:40:21.382 --> 01:40:23.650
I spent over 25 years in the US Army,

01:40:23.650 --> 01:40:26.020
but I've been studying urban
warfare for over a decade.

01:40:30.324 --> 01:40:32.493
It's very accurate to be behind a wall

01:40:32.493 --> 01:40:35.362
and you pop out for a second
to throw a grenade like that.

01:40:35.362 --> 01:40:37.431
Where he's throwing the
grenade is a little off.

01:40:37.431 --> 01:40:39.199
Grenades have a timer in them,

01:40:39.199 --> 01:40:41.135
basically, so they don't
blow up in your hand.

01:40:41.135 --> 01:40:43.871
Once it hits the target.
It's not throw, explode.

01:40:48.809 --> 01:40:52.746
100% air support is that
vulnerable in urban operations,

01:40:52.746 --> 01:40:54.681
and why usually you don't bring it in.

01:40:54.681 --> 01:40:56.917
It's low-flying, it's slow,

01:40:56.917 --> 01:40:58.352
it doesn't have much protection,

01:40:58.352 --> 01:41:02.423
and usually you drop soldiers
offset of the urban battle.

01:41:02.423 --> 01:41:04.391
In this situation, they didn't expect

01:41:04.391 --> 01:41:06.860
that level of resistance.

01:41:08.262 --> 01:41:09.096
Go.

01:41:14.301 --> 01:41:16.103
Crossing the street can
actually be one of the most

01:41:16.103 --> 01:41:17.704
vulnerable things of urban warfare

01:41:17.704 --> 01:41:19.373
because there's so many angles,

01:41:19.373 --> 01:41:21.375
so many windows, so many doorways

01:41:21.375 --> 01:41:22.376
which you can get shot.

01:41:22.376 --> 01:41:25.212
So we teach things like
throwing smoke out there

01:41:25.212 --> 01:41:28.582
so that people can't see
you and going one at a time

01:41:28.582 --> 01:41:31.151
and mixing up the speed
at which you're going.

01:41:31.151 --> 01:41:33.287
If I didn't know this battle,
the battle of Mogadishu,

01:41:33.287 --> 01:41:34.922
I would say that this is not realistic

01:41:34.922 --> 01:41:38.459
where you have hundreds of
people shooting at a small unit,

01:41:38.459 --> 01:41:40.127
but that's really what happened.

01:41:40.127 --> 01:41:43.130
And they got the soldiers pretty accurate,

01:41:43.130 --> 01:41:45.299
but there's been a lot of
criticism about this movie

01:41:45.299 --> 01:41:48.235
and the depiction of the
Somalis and kind of the context

01:41:48.235 --> 01:41:52.139
of what's going on in
Mogadishu at this time.

01:41:52.139 --> 01:41:54.608
Hey, Grimes, stay away from the wall.

01:41:58.378 --> 01:41:59.713
You OK?

01:41:59.713 --> 01:42:00.547
Yeah!

01:42:00.547 --> 01:42:02.416
That's actually an iconic
scene, when he says,

01:42:02.416 --> 01:42:03.517
"Stay off the wall."

01:42:03.517 --> 01:42:06.019
That was real advice
because once the bullets

01:42:06.019 --> 01:42:07.955
are hitting the walls, then
they travel down the wall

01:42:07.955 --> 01:42:09.456
kind of like a bowling alley,

01:42:09.456 --> 01:42:11.692
and it can hit if you're standing on that.

01:42:11.692 --> 01:42:13.227
I wouldn't say it happens in all,

01:42:13.227 --> 01:42:14.828
because every city is different.

01:42:14.828 --> 01:42:18.432
You have mud, cement, glass,
metal is a little different,

01:42:18.432 --> 01:42:19.766
but that's real.

01:42:24.471 --> 01:42:25.606
Let's go, Maddox.

01:42:27.374 --> 01:42:29.743
You see the bullet holes in the glass

01:42:29.743 --> 01:42:32.346
because back then we weren't as prepared

01:42:32.346 --> 01:42:33.814
for urban warfare as we are today,

01:42:33.814 --> 01:42:36.450
so those aren't bulletproof vehicles.

01:42:36.450 --> 01:42:39.019
A lot of the weapons
there aren't really ready

01:42:39.019 --> 01:42:40.721
for that environment
that they're moving into,

01:42:40.721 --> 01:42:43.490
but they also were surprised
by what they encountered.

01:42:43.490 --> 01:42:47.928
So I'd give it, like, a nine
from realism in urban warfare,

01:42:47.928 --> 01:42:51.565
not realism in the
portrayal of the population

01:42:51.565 --> 01:42:52.599
and things that are going on.

01:42:58.438 --> 01:43:01.675
Chris Hemsworth is more
like Jason Bourne there

01:43:01.675 --> 01:43:02.976
with his hand-to-hand.

01:43:02.976 --> 01:43:06.480
Close-quarters fighting is
very common in urban warfare.

01:43:06.480 --> 01:43:08.582
We actually tie the weapons to ourselves,

01:43:08.582 --> 01:43:10.617
so if we get caught in that situation

01:43:10.617 --> 01:43:12.386
and turn a corner or
somebody grabs our weapon

01:43:12.386 --> 01:43:13.754
they can't take it away from us,

01:43:13.754 --> 01:43:16.456
but not the tactics that
you're seeing in that scene.

01:43:16.456 --> 01:43:19.426
He's surrounded by
people with bigger guns,

01:43:19.426 --> 01:43:22.129
so he's trying to disarm one
while another one's there.

01:43:22.129 --> 01:43:24.998
Personally, I would have
tried to disarm one of them

01:43:24.998 --> 01:43:26.166
with one of those rifles

01:43:26.166 --> 01:43:28.035
and then get your back against something

01:43:28.035 --> 01:43:31.972
so you're not expecting
a bad guy 360 degrees

01:43:31.972 --> 01:43:34.107
and then incapacitate them in some way.

01:43:41.048 --> 01:43:43.550
I can't say I've found
myself in a similar situation

01:43:43.550 --> 01:43:45.719
or we teach that, but it could work.

01:43:45.719 --> 01:43:48.555
Absolutely slow the person
down, buy yourself some time.

01:43:48.555 --> 01:43:50.824
I'd say you'd want to turn
corners as much as possible,

01:43:50.824 --> 01:43:52.492
and especially in a building like that,

01:43:52.492 --> 01:43:54.595
just to confuse them
on which way you went.

01:43:57.798 --> 01:43:58.865
Out of everything out of that scene,

01:43:58.865 --> 01:44:00.367
that's probably the most realistic.

01:44:00.367 --> 01:44:02.169
The fact that he's hiding behind a column,

01:44:02.169 --> 01:44:03.737
that column is a supporting column,

01:44:03.737 --> 01:44:06.840
so it has steel-reinforced concrete.

01:44:06.840 --> 01:44:09.743
Where some walls, it can be
paper-thin, it can be plaster.

01:44:09.743 --> 01:44:11.111
If you don't have that, then you do get

01:44:11.111 --> 01:44:13.513
behind things like
furniture and other things,

01:44:13.513 --> 01:44:15.983
anything you can to try to get you cover.

01:44:15.983 --> 01:44:17.718
Yeah, it was a lot of fun to watch,

01:44:17.718 --> 01:44:21.288
but I'd give it about a
one or a two in realism.

01:44:28.362 --> 01:44:29.363
I actually love this clip,

01:44:29.363 --> 01:44:31.431
and I wish I could show
it to more soldiers

01:44:31.431 --> 01:44:33.567
to think about buildings differently.

01:44:33.567 --> 01:44:35.135
Especially if you're defending a house,

01:44:35.135 --> 01:44:37.204
you want to take the stairways away,

01:44:37.204 --> 01:44:38.872
you want to take the doors away,

01:44:38.872 --> 01:44:40.641
and absolutely throw furniture down.

01:44:40.641 --> 01:44:42.943
But the scene is really great
too because it shows you that

01:44:42.943 --> 01:44:44.911
that's not necessarily
what you stay behind

01:44:44.911 --> 01:44:45.979
to protect you from bullets,

01:44:45.979 --> 01:44:47.547
because bullets pass through wood,

01:44:47.547 --> 01:44:48.749
through cloth and everything.

01:44:50.951 --> 01:44:52.953
That's a great lesson
that clip teaches you

01:44:52.953 --> 01:44:55.889
that we teach our soldiers,
is to stay away from windows.

01:44:55.889 --> 01:44:58.859
You crouch down to go underneath them,

01:44:58.859 --> 01:45:01.428
kind of slowly walk your way around them.

01:45:01.428 --> 01:45:04.531
Bullets incoming probably
wouldn't be that dramatic,

01:45:04.531 --> 01:45:06.333
but like that, absolutely we tell you

01:45:06.333 --> 01:45:07.501
to stay away from windows.

01:45:14.441 --> 01:45:16.343
Yeah, that clip is really important

01:45:16.343 --> 01:45:17.944
to what people don't even think about

01:45:17.944 --> 01:45:20.180
even in the military, called mouse holing,

01:45:20.180 --> 01:45:23.917
putting holes in walls, in floors

01:45:23.917 --> 01:45:26.119
to move throughout without being seen.

01:45:26.119 --> 01:45:29.423
It's very common in major urban battles.

01:45:29.423 --> 01:45:31.858
Actually, I would love for
soldiers to carry an axe

01:45:31.858 --> 01:45:34.895
or something like that, but
there are other ways to do it.

01:45:34.895 --> 01:45:38.398
Sometimes we use
explosives, sledgehammers,

01:45:38.398 --> 01:45:40.767
axes like that to make that hole.

01:45:40.767 --> 01:45:41.935
Real tactics, real everything,

01:45:41.935 --> 01:45:44.371
so I'm going with a
nine on that, actually.

01:45:49.343 --> 01:45:51.244
I mean, this scene seems
very familiar to me

01:45:51.244 --> 01:45:54.815
because I had my own deployments
to Iraq and to Baghdad

01:45:54.815 --> 01:45:56.950
where we were doing raids like these

01:45:56.950 --> 01:45:58.752
where there's only a few terrorists

01:45:58.752 --> 01:46:00.320
in a population of millions.

01:46:00.320 --> 01:46:02.856
For the advantage of your
equipment, you can see at night,

01:46:02.856 --> 01:46:06.360
but also to reduce the
risk of being observed

01:46:06.360 --> 01:46:07.928
and the risk to civilians.

01:46:07.928 --> 01:46:10.030
The fact that their night-vision goggles

01:46:10.030 --> 01:46:12.232
were always on top of their
head is not realistic.

01:46:12.232 --> 01:46:16.002
You'd want to cut the power or
eliminate the light sources,

01:46:16.002 --> 01:46:18.472
so you want to use those
goggles to your advantage.

01:46:24.111 --> 01:46:24.945
Execute.

01:46:24.945 --> 01:46:27.647
It's very common and very smart to have

01:46:27.647 --> 01:46:30.150
a sniper with overwatch who has angles

01:46:30.150 --> 01:46:32.753
that the people moving
into the house can't see.

01:46:32.753 --> 01:46:34.488
It's a really good call for the sniper

01:46:34.488 --> 01:46:36.022
to be the one to execute the first shot,

01:46:36.022 --> 01:46:39.359
because you want to save your
surprise to the last moment,

01:46:39.359 --> 01:46:42.262
and a sniper usually
has a silenced weapon.

01:46:42.262 --> 01:46:43.730
This sniper in particular,

01:46:43.730 --> 01:46:46.767
he'd probably be wearing more equipment,

01:46:46.767 --> 01:46:49.169
and usually it's a sniper and a spotter,

01:46:49.169 --> 01:46:50.971
so there'd be other people with him.

01:46:58.712 --> 01:47:01.081
The way they enter this
house is a very common,

01:47:01.081 --> 01:47:03.817
almost too common, thing
that's portrayed in movies.

01:47:03.817 --> 01:47:05.452
It's called enter and clear a room.

01:47:05.452 --> 01:47:08.722
Throwing a grenade can
be the preferred tactic

01:47:08.722 --> 01:47:12.058
if it's known to be an enemy location

01:47:12.058 --> 01:47:13.760
and you know there's somebody
in there with a weapon.

01:47:13.760 --> 01:47:14.728
Of course you're going to enter it

01:47:14.728 --> 01:47:16.363
with something other than your face.

01:47:16.363 --> 01:47:18.765
So, the way they're stacked on the door

01:47:18.765 --> 01:47:20.367
is about getting in there quickly.

01:47:20.367 --> 01:47:22.436
It actually evolved from SWAT tactics.

01:47:22.436 --> 01:47:24.171
These tactics were actually developed

01:47:24.171 --> 01:47:26.873
to limit that amount of violence

01:47:26.873 --> 01:47:29.342
because there may be
civilians intermixed in there.

01:47:34.881 --> 01:47:37.417
The tactics you see Bradley
Cooper employing there

01:47:37.417 --> 01:47:38.285
is what I would teach.

01:47:38.285 --> 01:47:40.287
You want to get behind
something that's going to stop

01:47:40.287 --> 01:47:41.888
the bullets that people
are shooting at you

01:47:41.888 --> 01:47:44.391
but also be able to return
fire. We call that cover.

01:47:44.391 --> 01:47:47.027
The windows are great to get down behind,

01:47:47.027 --> 01:47:48.962
kneel behind, still shoot from.

01:47:48.962 --> 01:47:50.997
Unlike the enemy in that scene

01:47:50.997 --> 01:47:52.632
running out into the open areas,

01:47:52.632 --> 01:47:54.868
that's actually the opposite
of what actually happens.

01:47:54.868 --> 01:47:55.969
They're going to stay inside the rooms,

01:47:55.969 --> 01:47:56.970
make you come in there.

01:47:56.970 --> 01:47:59.105
You're going to get off
the streets and the alleys.

01:47:59.105 --> 01:48:00.407
Butcher's on the move.

01:48:01.608 --> 01:48:03.577
Repeat. Butcher's on the
move. I'm in pursuit.

01:48:05.745 --> 01:48:07.347
When he goes off by himself and chases,

01:48:07.347 --> 01:48:08.482
that's not realistic.

01:48:08.482 --> 01:48:10.250
We don't move by ourselves anywhere.

01:48:10.250 --> 01:48:11.451
It's almost like a cardinal rule.

01:48:11.451 --> 01:48:13.954
So it would be another team
person right there with him

01:48:13.954 --> 01:48:16.156
and communicating to each other using

01:48:16.156 --> 01:48:18.358
some type of radio or something.

01:48:18.358 --> 01:48:20.527
If he says, "Moving," then somebody else

01:48:20.527 --> 01:48:22.095
would follow him out there.

01:48:22.095 --> 01:48:23.964
I'd give it a rating of around an eight.

01:48:25.665 --> 01:48:26.766
Ambush!

01:48:29.269 --> 01:48:34.207
This happened to me in Iraq,
ambush initiated by an RPG.

01:48:34.207 --> 01:48:36.610
Ambushes are very, very
common in urban warfare.

01:48:36.610 --> 01:48:38.578
It's meant to surprise the people,

01:48:38.578 --> 01:48:41.281
entrap them in a funnel like that.

01:48:41.281 --> 01:48:43.416
Although the bad guys don't usually

01:48:43.416 --> 01:48:44.985
just stand on top of the roof.

01:48:51.157 --> 01:48:52.692
You would not be standing
in the middle of the street

01:48:52.692 --> 01:48:55.095
shooting back. You wouldn't last long.

01:48:55.095 --> 01:48:58.565
We'd seek cover, use those
armored vehicles, which is great.

01:48:58.565 --> 01:49:00.767
Ideally, though, in the whole situation,

01:49:00.767 --> 01:49:02.802
it's called a "kill zone" of the ambush.

01:49:02.802 --> 01:49:03.837
You want to get out of it.

01:49:10.143 --> 01:49:11.845
That concept of having a,

01:49:11.845 --> 01:49:14.848
we call it a quick reaction
force, a backup team.

01:49:14.848 --> 01:49:16.416
Because you've been surprised,

01:49:16.416 --> 01:49:17.751
somebody needs to come to help you,

01:49:17.751 --> 01:49:20.153
and we usually keep forces
in their uniform ready to go

01:49:20.153 --> 01:49:21.288
in an urban setting like this

01:49:21.288 --> 01:49:24.124
to be that quick reaction
force to come to that aid.

01:49:25.325 --> 01:49:26.927
Let's go. Let's go!

01:49:29.963 --> 01:49:32.032
They ram the vehicle,
which is very common.

01:49:32.032 --> 01:49:33.567
Trying to lock them in that kill zone

01:49:33.567 --> 01:49:35.001
so everybody can shoot at you.

01:49:35.001 --> 01:49:36.536
So, I'm actually going to
give it a higher rating

01:49:36.536 --> 01:49:40.707
than I want to because
the ambush is so real

01:49:40.707 --> 01:49:42.909
outside of the tactics, so I'll give it a,

01:49:42.909 --> 01:49:44.311
let's give it a four.

01:49:49.716 --> 01:49:52.185
The drones are probably
the most ubiquitous change

01:49:52.185 --> 01:49:54.354
to urban warfare that's
happened in decades.

01:49:54.354 --> 01:49:57.424
As I go into Ukraine to
research urban warfare right now

01:49:57.424 --> 01:49:59.759
on the major battles,

01:49:59.759 --> 01:50:03.063
there's thousands and
thousands of drones being used.

01:50:03.063 --> 01:50:05.465
It reduces some of the
challenges of urban warfare

01:50:05.465 --> 01:50:08.735
if I can put a drone
in front of the soldier

01:50:08.735 --> 01:50:11.605
to see around that corner,
to see over that building.

01:50:11.605 --> 01:50:14.541
The aspect of X-ray vision,
we're not there yet,

01:50:14.541 --> 01:50:16.810
because we still can't
see through concrete.

01:50:16.810 --> 01:50:19.479
Especially not much can see through steel.

01:50:26.019 --> 01:50:29.456
It gave me a lot of feelings
of real stuff that's happened.

01:50:29.456 --> 01:50:31.157
Especially the use of the dump truck

01:50:31.157 --> 01:50:32.626
reminds me of the Beirut bombings

01:50:32.626 --> 01:50:34.961
where a dump truck was brought
to the front of the base,

01:50:34.961 --> 01:50:36.830
like in that scenario, but then exploded.

01:50:36.830 --> 01:50:38.498
This is why urban warfare is so hard.

01:50:38.498 --> 01:50:40.200
Dump truck could be a threat.

01:50:43.470 --> 01:50:45.372
Although that's probably unlikely

01:50:45.372 --> 01:50:47.807
that you're going to use
your vibranium shield

01:50:47.807 --> 01:50:49.776
to throw it up in the air,
but what it signifies for me

01:50:49.776 --> 01:50:51.544
is what we call a three-block war,

01:50:51.544 --> 01:50:53.213
where, when you're
fighting in urban terrain,

01:50:53.213 --> 01:50:55.982
you are actually very concerned

01:50:55.982 --> 01:50:58.485
about the civilian population
and protecting people

01:50:58.485 --> 01:51:00.086
at the same time as you're fighting,

01:51:00.086 --> 01:51:02.522
and there's restrictions on
what you can or can't do.

01:51:02.522 --> 01:51:04.491
So, no, that's a very plausible scenario

01:51:04.491 --> 01:51:07.227
that any soldier who has values

01:51:07.227 --> 01:51:09.462
is trying to protect
the civilian population

01:51:09.462 --> 01:51:11.631
while still trying to do it, the mission.

01:51:11.631 --> 01:51:13.500
So if the rating has to be for realism,

01:51:13.500 --> 01:51:15.468
I'll give it around a six.

01:51:18.672 --> 01:51:20.240
Eight to 10 Tangos, front gate.

01:51:20.240 --> 01:51:22.475
Left! By the trees.

01:51:22.475 --> 01:51:23.743
A couple little things in there.

01:51:23.743 --> 01:51:26.646
There are nice touches of
adding realism to the situation,

01:51:26.646 --> 01:51:29.683
like the red dot that
you see on your scopes

01:51:29.683 --> 01:51:32.986
on the modern weapons that
will kind of help you aim

01:51:32.986 --> 01:51:35.822
to the green hue that
you see on their eyeballs

01:51:35.822 --> 01:51:37.791
coming off the night-vision goggles.

01:51:37.791 --> 01:51:40.427
So, when you see some version
of night-vision goggles,

01:51:40.427 --> 01:51:42.095
kind of martian type of look.

01:51:42.095 --> 01:51:44.064
When you look around,
everybody's got green eyes.

01:51:44.064 --> 01:51:46.199
Get to the top of this building. Let's go.

01:51:46.199 --> 01:51:49.269
Blocked by buildings.
We got no vantage point.

01:51:49.269 --> 01:51:50.570
This roost is a bust.

01:51:50.570 --> 01:51:53.773
In this scene, they enter
a really strong building

01:51:53.773 --> 01:51:55.709
like that. It's a great idea.

01:51:55.709 --> 01:51:57.744
The strategy was called strongpoints,

01:51:57.744 --> 01:52:00.547
or a building that is really strong,

01:52:00.547 --> 01:52:02.248
it has lines of sight

01:52:02.248 --> 01:52:05.452
or directions you can fire
from in multiple directions,

01:52:05.452 --> 01:52:07.821
and in war it's happened
where a single building

01:52:07.821 --> 01:52:09.222
has been held for months

01:52:09.222 --> 01:52:11.858
just because it was the right strongpoint.

01:52:11.858 --> 01:52:14.461
He says it's a bust because
depending on what type,

01:52:14.461 --> 01:52:17.297
the direction, the building,
and where the enemy is,

01:52:17.297 --> 01:52:19.399
they got up there, thought
they'd have good vantage point,

01:52:19.399 --> 01:52:21.167
but they couldn't see
anything, so it was a bust.

01:52:24.938 --> 01:52:25.905
Take on your right.

01:52:28.408 --> 01:52:30.443
What we usually see in
urban warfare movies

01:52:30.443 --> 01:52:31.978
is everybody trying to get to the roof

01:52:31.978 --> 01:52:34.881
because it kind of is common
sense that I can shoot down,

01:52:34.881 --> 01:52:36.716
I can see people farther out,

01:52:36.716 --> 01:52:38.518
but depending on the context,

01:52:38.518 --> 01:52:40.220
you don't want to be
anywhere near the roof

01:52:40.220 --> 01:52:42.622
because you can also be seen from the air,

01:52:42.622 --> 01:52:46.192
from drones, from mortars, you can be hit.

01:52:46.192 --> 01:52:49.496
It's based on a real situation
that happened in 2012

01:52:49.496 --> 01:52:53.466
at Benghazi where they're
attacking the embassy compound.

01:52:53.466 --> 01:52:55.001
That's a good idea to get up there

01:52:55.001 --> 01:52:57.871
so you can see where they're coming from

01:52:57.871 --> 01:53:00.039
and to take better
shots and not waste ammo

01:53:00.039 --> 01:53:01.141
from the rooftop for sure.

01:53:01.141 --> 01:53:03.543
I'd give that a pretty
high rating in realism,

01:53:03.543 --> 01:53:04.778
about a seven, eight,

01:53:04.778 --> 01:53:08.148
just based on the type
of weapons, the tactics,

01:53:08.148 --> 01:53:11.117
the chaos, the building
construction. It's pretty real.

01:53:19.626 --> 01:53:21.027
I think this is an important scene

01:53:21.027 --> 01:53:23.830
because one of the biggest
myths that TV and movies show

01:53:23.830 --> 01:53:26.332
about urban warfare is
that it's just infantry

01:53:26.332 --> 01:53:28.501
or soldiers shooting their weapons.

01:53:28.501 --> 01:53:31.638
Major urban battles are tanks, artillery,

01:53:31.638 --> 01:53:33.540
engineers, infantry.

01:53:33.540 --> 01:53:36.810
They're full-scale battles in concrete.

01:53:36.810 --> 01:53:39.045
Now, the damage that the
tank does to the building,

01:53:39.045 --> 01:53:42.048
that is unlikely that just

01:53:42.048 --> 01:53:43.917
the whole face of the
building is going to fall off.

01:53:43.917 --> 01:53:44.951
It's going to do a lot of damage,

01:53:44.951 --> 01:53:46.653
and that's why you have a tank,

01:53:46.653 --> 01:53:49.489
because a tank is mobile
protected firepower.

01:53:49.489 --> 01:53:52.959
It has that giant gun that can
punch through that problem,

01:53:52.959 --> 01:53:54.828
which is concrete and cover.

01:53:54.828 --> 01:53:57.230
But in this scene, they're all tracers.

01:53:57.230 --> 01:53:59.766
It's almost like a "Star Wars" ray gun.

01:53:59.766 --> 01:54:02.235
They won't all be like that.

01:54:09.609 --> 01:54:11.411
There's actually a myth that tanks

01:54:11.411 --> 01:54:14.614
are not helpful in urban
warfare, they're too vulnerable.

01:54:14.614 --> 01:54:16.049
But actually, the
history of urban warfare,

01:54:16.049 --> 01:54:18.351
if you go into a major urban fight

01:54:18.351 --> 01:54:20.153
and you don't have a tank, you're wrong.

01:54:20.153 --> 01:54:22.956
But the problem is that
the tank can't go by itself

01:54:22.956 --> 01:54:24.357
and the infantry can't go by itself.

01:54:24.357 --> 01:54:26.092
And as we see in this scene,

01:54:26.092 --> 01:54:29.229
the infantry worked to protect the tank,

01:54:29.229 --> 01:54:31.564
and then the tank protect the infantry.

01:54:31.564 --> 01:54:33.867
But tanks are vulnerable, as you see here.

01:54:33.867 --> 01:54:36.870
The Germans shoot from below
where the tank can shoot,

01:54:36.870 --> 01:54:37.704
in the basement.

01:54:43.042 --> 01:54:45.979
The size needed to take a
town or a village or a city

01:54:45.979 --> 01:54:48.982
all depends on what the
enemy is that they're facing,

01:54:48.982 --> 01:54:51.117
and I think they really didn't know.

01:54:51.117 --> 01:54:52.585
For that street,

01:54:52.585 --> 01:54:54.821
I think that looked
about right, absolutely,

01:54:54.821 --> 01:54:58.024
where you combine a tank
section, always two tanks,

01:54:58.024 --> 01:54:59.926
with an infantry platoon or something

01:54:59.926 --> 01:55:01.761
to clear a street like that

01:55:01.761 --> 01:55:03.596
before you discover where the enemy is.

01:55:03.596 --> 01:55:06.599
Outside of the lasers of the rounds,

01:55:06.599 --> 01:55:10.803
this is a very realistic
scene of high-intensity combat

01:55:10.803 --> 01:55:12.705
and the combined arms that you need,

01:55:12.705 --> 01:55:14.140
so I give it a nine.

01:55:22.215 --> 01:55:23.449
Ugh. Will you pause?

01:55:23.449 --> 01:55:26.586
The one thing that I can't
help myself on this scene

01:55:26.586 --> 01:55:28.521
is that everyone walks up to the wall

01:55:28.521 --> 01:55:30.590
and just sticks their rifle
over the top of the wall.

01:55:30.590 --> 01:55:32.659
That's absolutely what you don't do.

01:55:32.659 --> 01:55:34.727
You're just putting a flag
up saying where you are.

01:55:34.727 --> 01:55:38.164
I don't have any problem with
them moving to the rooftops.

01:55:38.164 --> 01:55:39.432
This is a hasty attack.

01:55:39.432 --> 01:55:40.700
Two different buildings, that's great;

01:55:40.700 --> 01:55:42.335
it gives you converging fire.

01:55:42.335 --> 01:55:44.237
So firing down from two
different directions.

01:55:44.237 --> 01:55:47.040
They can cover more of the courtyard.

01:55:47.040 --> 01:55:49.375
The scene in this movie
is based on the real

01:55:49.375 --> 01:55:51.711
battle of Mosul that happened in 2017,

01:55:51.711 --> 01:55:54.914
one of the biggest battles
really since World War II,

01:55:54.914 --> 01:55:56.816
100,000 security forces

01:55:56.816 --> 01:55:59.819
against a entrenched ISIS enemy force

01:55:59.819 --> 01:56:01.087
that had to be cleared out.

01:56:09.662 --> 01:56:11.264
That's actually pretty realistic.

01:56:11.264 --> 01:56:14.567
You open an attack like that
with your biggest weapon.

01:56:14.567 --> 01:56:16.736
We call it the most
casualty-producing weapons.

01:56:16.736 --> 01:56:18.871
Duds do happen. The way
you usually get around that

01:56:18.871 --> 01:56:21.107
is you have more than a
single point of failure,

01:56:21.107 --> 01:56:24.711
more than one big weapon,
or, like they do here,

01:56:24.711 --> 01:56:27.880
which is actually accurate,
is once that duds, that's it.

01:56:27.880 --> 01:56:29.582
Your surprise is gone. Attack.

01:56:36.956 --> 01:56:38.691
It's actually pretty complex,
what they're trying to do,

01:56:38.691 --> 01:56:40.760
because they have moving forces

01:56:40.760 --> 01:56:42.562
with other elements shooting down

01:56:42.562 --> 01:56:44.664
that they have to know
and watch very closely

01:56:44.664 --> 01:56:47.600
on when to turn their
fire off as you advance.

01:56:47.600 --> 01:56:50.636
That's, in some ways, the
way I would teach it as well,

01:56:50.636 --> 01:56:51.904
that's as they're maneuvering in

01:56:51.904 --> 01:56:53.840
while another person's holding them down.

01:56:58.111 --> 01:57:01.080
Booby traps are very
popular in urban warfare.

01:57:01.080 --> 01:57:03.449
Pictures on the wall. Any and everything

01:57:03.449 --> 01:57:04.917
could be potentially booby traps.

01:57:04.917 --> 01:57:06.619
We see it right now in the Ukraine war

01:57:06.619 --> 01:57:10.356
when Ukraine takes back their
territories from the Russians.

01:57:10.356 --> 01:57:11.624
But you have to be hyperaware

01:57:11.624 --> 01:57:13.426
of all those booby traps, for sure.

01:57:13.426 --> 01:57:15.328
One is just awareness of every soldier

01:57:15.328 --> 01:57:17.730
not to move and displace things,

01:57:17.730 --> 01:57:20.266
and then we have bomb-sniffing
dogs that we'll bring in.

01:57:20.266 --> 01:57:23.036
I'm going to actually give
it around a five, six.

01:57:23.036 --> 01:57:25.238
Just some of it's a little off.

01:57:28.041 --> 01:57:30.810
He'll be on the footwalk over the gutter.

01:57:30.810 --> 01:57:32.612
We'll take him out from behind.

01:57:34.881 --> 01:57:36.816
The underground is a huge component

01:57:36.816 --> 01:57:39.252
of that three-dimensional
aspects of urban warfare.

01:57:39.252 --> 01:57:41.854
Many cities have cities under the cities,

01:57:41.854 --> 01:57:44.357
and in battles like this,
which, this movie is based on

01:57:44.357 --> 01:57:47.193
the battle of Stalingrad,
which was called the "Rat War"

01:57:47.193 --> 01:57:49.929
because there was such
a prevalent underground.

01:57:56.202 --> 01:57:59.906
Yes, those are possible
urban sniper positions.

01:57:59.906 --> 01:58:03.109
The characters on both
sides are loosely tied

01:58:03.109 --> 01:58:05.511
to real snipers that were prevalent

01:58:05.511 --> 01:58:07.280
in the battle of Stalingrad who were known

01:58:07.280 --> 01:58:10.416
to put themselves in the barrels
and the ventilation shafts.

01:58:10.416 --> 01:58:14.120
The cat and the mouse
between the two snipers,

01:58:14.120 --> 01:58:15.388
that didn't happen.

01:58:21.461 --> 01:58:23.763
We do train soldiers
to use the environment,

01:58:23.763 --> 01:58:27.900
and there is some aspect of
manipulating light and smoke.

01:58:27.900 --> 01:58:29.969
That's a real thing, right?
Reflective lights in the mirror,

01:58:29.969 --> 01:58:32.138
but you don't know where the sniper is,

01:58:32.138 --> 01:58:34.974
and unlikely that you're
going to have that ability

01:58:34.974 --> 01:58:38.478
to blind that, so, no, don't do that.

01:58:38.478 --> 01:58:40.947
I want to give it a
higher one because it is

01:58:40.947 --> 01:58:42.915
pointing out the
underground warfare aspect,

01:58:42.915 --> 01:58:46.786
but the realism of the
actual, this happening,

01:58:46.786 --> 01:58:48.521
around a three, I guess.

01:58:54.427 --> 01:58:56.729
That's pretty about as real as it gets.

01:58:56.729 --> 01:58:59.198
The IED, or the improvised
explosive device,

01:58:59.198 --> 01:59:02.702
is the weapon of the last 20-plus years.

01:59:02.702 --> 01:59:06.038
The invisible enemy that you
couldn't fight back against.

01:59:09.442 --> 01:59:11.811
The M2 .50-cal is still used today.

01:59:11.811 --> 01:59:15.148
It's a very effective large machine gun

01:59:15.148 --> 01:59:17.917
that has the size of bullet
and penetration of concrete

01:59:17.917 --> 01:59:20.920
that you'd want, where
other weapons might not.

01:59:20.920 --> 01:59:22.989
The accuracy of what
the bullets were doing

01:59:22.989 --> 01:59:25.558
to the building looked pretty accurate.

01:59:25.558 --> 01:59:27.593
Some of them you can
tell aren't penetrating.

01:59:27.593 --> 01:59:29.529
He didn't shoot that
many bullets at that wall

01:59:29.529 --> 01:59:33.599
to create that level of sprawling

01:59:33.599 --> 01:59:35.034
and honeycombing of that wall.

01:59:39.238 --> 01:59:41.174
The tactics there are pretty sound,

01:59:41.174 --> 01:59:43.409
where you have the machine
gun covering an upper level

01:59:43.409 --> 01:59:45.978
while the infantry are maneuvering lower

01:59:45.978 --> 01:59:48.514
so there's control there
and positioning themselves

01:59:48.514 --> 01:59:50.583
to enter that building where
they know the enemy is.

01:59:53.719 --> 01:59:57.023
I can't say that kicking
of the door is not real.

01:59:57.023 --> 01:59:58.191
It's called a mule kick.

01:59:58.191 --> 01:59:59.892
I wish the movies would stop showing it

01:59:59.892 --> 02:00:02.361
because it's not effective,

02:00:02.361 --> 02:00:05.298
but I have found many soldiers trying it.

02:00:05.298 --> 02:00:07.600
But it's not the most force you can put

02:00:07.600 --> 02:00:10.469
by kicking in that door.
You want to face the door.

02:00:10.469 --> 02:00:13.105
Soldier: On the ground!
[civilian crying out]

02:00:15.041 --> 02:00:18.077
That's an unfortunate
but very realistic aspect

02:00:18.077 --> 02:00:20.046
of that scenario and that chaos

02:00:20.046 --> 02:00:21.247
of when the soldier enters the room,

02:00:21.247 --> 02:00:24.250
but controlled and not
just firing at everybody.

02:00:24.250 --> 02:00:26.352
But then you have scared,

02:00:26.352 --> 02:00:29.422
frantic civilians even approaching you,

02:00:29.422 --> 02:00:31.490
making the soldier feel very unsafe.

02:00:31.490 --> 02:00:35.661
That's a very realistic and
impactful traumatic situation

02:00:35.661 --> 02:00:36.829
that is urban warfare.

02:00:36.829 --> 02:00:39.232
So if we were to take out the mule kick,

02:00:39.232 --> 02:00:41.100
I would give that scene a 10.

02:00:41.100 --> 02:00:42.268
Gareth Timmins here.

02:00:42.268 --> 02:00:43.970
I'm a former Royal Marines commando

02:00:43.970 --> 02:00:46.939
that spent time in
Afghanistan, Egypt, Somalia,

02:00:46.939 --> 02:00:48.874
and just various places
around the Middle East.

02:00:53.412 --> 02:00:54.714
Gas!

02:00:54.714 --> 02:00:56.315
It's CS gas, which has been widely used

02:00:56.315 --> 02:00:58.784
in the Middle East, especially
around the Arab Spring.

02:00:58.784 --> 02:01:01.587
Whether you would fire it
out mortars is another thing.

02:01:01.587 --> 02:01:04.757
Firing the CS gas and
having it airburst like that

02:01:04.757 --> 02:01:06.058
would just be like

02:01:06.058 --> 02:01:08.494
chucking a little cap
of bleach into the sea

02:01:08.494 --> 02:01:11.397
in terms of its effectiveness
on trying to poison swimmers.

02:01:12.898 --> 02:01:14.000
Mine!

02:01:17.470 --> 02:01:18.904
I mean, if you were
breaking contact like that,

02:01:18.904 --> 02:01:20.339
you would absolutely just,

02:01:20.339 --> 02:01:22.141
I would've thought, just
drove in a straight line

02:01:22.141 --> 02:01:24.410
as fast as possible to get out of there.

02:01:24.410 --> 02:01:26.579
But they seemed to veer into it just fine,

02:01:26.579 --> 02:01:27.847
I think for the purposes of the film

02:01:27.847 --> 02:01:28.681
and for the viewers, really.

02:01:28.681 --> 02:01:31.284
The fact that they're just
perfectly placed on the top

02:01:31.284 --> 02:01:32.585
is quite comical, really.

02:01:32.585 --> 02:01:34.787
A minefield would normally be set out

02:01:34.787 --> 02:01:36.756
in a certain way to
either restrict movement

02:01:36.756 --> 02:01:39.358
or to channel the enemy
into a particular point.

02:01:39.358 --> 02:01:42.094
In terms of the spacing
and the mixture of mines,

02:01:42.094 --> 02:01:45.031
it's just been placed
there just for the vehicle,

02:01:45.031 --> 02:01:45.865
just to hit it.

02:01:45.865 --> 02:01:47.166
The anti-personnel mines are actually

02:01:47.166 --> 02:01:49.035
now banned by the Geneva Convention.

02:01:49.035 --> 02:01:50.603
What used to happen with those was

02:01:50.603 --> 02:01:52.672
you'd trigger one of the pines,

02:01:52.672 --> 02:01:54.774
and they'd come out the ground

02:01:54.774 --> 02:01:56.542
at about waist level,
and then they'd detonate.

02:01:56.542 --> 02:01:59.278
But yeah, you wouldn't normally
see them on top like that,

02:01:59.278 --> 02:02:00.446
unless there'd been a sandstorm,

02:02:00.446 --> 02:02:02.048
but even still, they look brand new.

02:02:02.048 --> 02:02:03.983
If you were breaking contact

02:02:03.983 --> 02:02:05.351
or trying to get away from the enemy

02:02:05.351 --> 02:02:07.953
in an open desert like that,
with no kind of life support --

02:02:07.953 --> 02:02:11.190
you've got no urban
environment, no buildings --

02:02:11.190 --> 02:02:13.726
you would absolutely want
to stay in that truck,

02:02:13.726 --> 02:02:15.828
unless it was completely disabled,

02:02:15.828 --> 02:02:17.463
and at that point you had no other option

02:02:17.463 --> 02:02:18.898
but to leave it because it was

02:02:18.898 --> 02:02:20.466
acting as a magnet for the enemy.

02:02:20.466 --> 02:02:22.201
But you certainly wouldn't bail from it

02:02:22.201 --> 02:02:23.803
if it were moving.

02:02:30.509 --> 02:02:32.912
Improvised explosive
devices that were used in

02:02:32.912 --> 02:02:34.580
Iraq and Afghanistan do flip vehicles,

02:02:34.580 --> 02:02:35.981
but whether a mine would be able

02:02:35.981 --> 02:02:40.686
to flip it to that extent is debatable.

02:02:40.686 --> 02:02:42.254
But certainly the people
that were inside it

02:02:42.254 --> 02:02:44.523
would be severely injured, if not killed.

02:02:44.523 --> 02:02:45.825
I'd give it a 3.

02:02:45.825 --> 02:02:48.361
Yeah, they're in the desert,
they're in military vehicles,

02:02:48.361 --> 02:02:49.762
there's military kit getting used,

02:02:49.762 --> 02:02:53.165
but apart from that, it's
very loosely based on reality.

02:02:53.165 --> 02:02:55.000
Radio: I have seven
Mexican police vehicles

02:02:55.000 --> 02:02:57.169
located at the border checkpoint waiting.

02:02:57.169 --> 02:02:59.271
They're moving and forming
up in your configurations.

02:02:59.271 --> 02:03:00.706
They're really, really closed up now.

02:03:00.706 --> 02:03:01.841
Before they got to the border,

02:03:01.841 --> 02:03:05.745
when they were driving in
urban areas or built-up areas,

02:03:05.745 --> 02:03:08.080
that kind of convoy posture
would have been perfect

02:03:08.080 --> 02:03:10.416
if you want to maintain the
posture and the security

02:03:10.416 --> 02:03:11.717
and not have vehicles come in.

02:03:16.355 --> 02:03:18.057
But as soon as they've kind of left that,

02:03:18.057 --> 02:03:18.958
and they're going now into

02:03:18.958 --> 02:03:20.926
the open desert and open ground,

02:03:20.926 --> 02:03:24.063
they would want to really,
really extend the spacing.

02:03:30.202 --> 02:03:32.571
The Humvee, it's an awful vehicle.

02:03:32.571 --> 02:03:34.039
It's not comfortable.

02:03:34.039 --> 02:03:36.942
During this now, you would close up,

02:03:36.942 --> 02:03:38.277
because you've got limited visibility.

02:03:38.277 --> 02:03:40.880
It's exaggerated there, really. Yeah.

02:03:40.880 --> 02:03:42.114
It is a thing.

02:03:42.114 --> 02:03:44.483
It would restrict your view, absolutely.

02:03:44.483 --> 02:03:45.951
Radio: No vehicle, no motion.

02:03:45.951 --> 02:03:47.820
Oscar 1. Incoming on your right.

02:03:47.820 --> 02:03:48.954
Man: Air, I see nothing.

02:03:48.954 --> 02:03:49.789
Radio: Contact right.

02:03:49.789 --> 02:03:50.823
I think it's realistic.

02:03:50.823 --> 02:03:53.592
You would have put out
"contact right, contact right,"

02:03:53.592 --> 02:03:55.294
which is what they do. Again, it's good.

02:03:55.294 --> 02:03:57.897
There's, like, natural,
generalized chat in there

02:03:57.897 --> 02:03:59.765
about the weather and whatnot,

02:03:59.765 --> 02:04:01.667
and that would happen in real life.

02:04:01.667 --> 02:04:04.403
There'd be some jokes,
there'd be some banter,

02:04:04.403 --> 02:04:07.506
but you would be very much
focused on the job at hand.

02:04:07.506 --> 02:04:08.674
So the communication there,

02:04:08.674 --> 02:04:12.278
in terms of communicating
to the whole group, is good.

02:04:12.278 --> 02:04:14.413
It could be a bit better, but it's good.

02:04:14.413 --> 02:04:15.781
Man: I don't see anything.

02:04:15.781 --> 02:04:16.782
Oh, down, down!

02:04:16.782 --> 02:04:18.684
[gunshots]

02:04:20.319 --> 02:04:22.087
You would absolutely, at all cost,

02:04:22.087 --> 02:04:23.355
want to drive through that.

02:04:23.355 --> 02:04:24.757
We call it "driving through it."

02:04:24.757 --> 02:04:26.125
They call it "the killing zone."

02:04:26.125 --> 02:04:28.260
To stop in that or be disabled in that

02:04:28.260 --> 02:04:30.029
would be like your worst nightmare.

02:04:32.164 --> 02:04:34.133
The sound and the impact that

02:04:34.133 --> 02:04:35.835
that round would make on the windscreen,

02:04:35.835 --> 02:04:38.003
I think would naturally,
despite your training,

02:04:38.003 --> 02:04:39.438
just make you do that.

02:04:39.438 --> 02:04:40.973
I think once a few rounds hit it

02:04:40.973 --> 02:04:42.808
and it had massaged your anxiety of

02:04:42.808 --> 02:04:44.376
"nothing's going to come through,"

02:04:44.376 --> 02:04:45.845
you would like to think that you'd be able

02:04:45.845 --> 02:04:48.347
to get back on track
and push through that.

02:04:48.347 --> 02:04:49.548
It's just, it's chaos.

02:04:50.483 --> 02:04:51.550
[gunshots]

02:04:51.550 --> 02:04:53.152
It's what's called a complex attack.

02:04:53.152 --> 02:04:54.687
The first guys go out to the left,

02:04:54.687 --> 02:04:57.723
which is what you would
do on a contact right.

02:04:57.723 --> 02:05:00.326
You would get out on the
left-hand side of the vehicle

02:05:00.326 --> 02:05:01.327
and use it as cover then

02:05:01.327 --> 02:05:03.395
to try and get some
rounds off if you had to.

02:05:03.395 --> 02:05:05.965
You're always told in training
to get out of the side,

02:05:05.965 --> 02:05:08.067
naturally, that's not taking fire.

02:05:08.067 --> 02:05:09.368
So you'd get out to cover.

02:05:09.368 --> 02:05:11.537
And you can see how they're kind of

02:05:11.537 --> 02:05:13.372
getting the weapons into the shoulder

02:05:13.372 --> 02:05:14.874
and how they're manipulating the doors

02:05:14.874 --> 02:05:16.509
and then pushing the door and coming back

02:05:16.509 --> 02:05:18.210
onto the stock of the weapon and stuff.

02:05:18.210 --> 02:05:19.912
It's really high-level stuff

02:05:19.912 --> 02:05:21.847
and weapon manipulation and use of cover.

02:05:21.847 --> 02:05:23.983
It's absolutely superb.

02:05:24.884 --> 02:05:27.820
[gunshots]

02:05:30.823 --> 02:05:33.692
I wouldn't want to be
under there, as a soldier,

02:05:33.692 --> 02:05:36.362
because you'd have no view whatsoever

02:05:36.362 --> 02:05:37.229
of anything that's going off,

02:05:37.229 --> 02:05:38.364
and it would be claustrophobic.

02:05:38.364 --> 02:05:41.166
I can see why she's done
it, un-military-trained,

02:05:41.166 --> 02:05:43.869
but it's not where I'd want to be.

02:05:43.869 --> 02:05:46.338
You've got to try and get
away from that killing zone

02:05:46.338 --> 02:05:48.607
somehow, as soon as possible.

02:05:48.607 --> 02:05:49.742
I'd give it 9.

02:05:49.742 --> 02:05:51.110
The only kind of thing
that lets it down, really,

02:05:51.110 --> 02:05:53.012
is the spacing when they're
going to open ground

02:05:53.012 --> 02:05:55.381
in terms of the vehicles,
but apart from that,

02:05:55.381 --> 02:05:57.683
how they react, the weapon manipulation,

02:05:57.683 --> 02:06:01.253
the communications is really good.

02:06:05.624 --> 02:06:06.525
He's running around often

02:06:06.525 --> 02:06:07.893
with a top off or a vest on.

02:06:07.893 --> 02:06:09.428
The sunburn and the heat stroke

02:06:09.428 --> 02:06:11.263
that he would get from
that would kill him.

02:06:11.263 --> 02:06:14.433
You'd have some kind of
a camouflage shirt on,

02:06:14.433 --> 02:06:17.303
or a tactical jacket, like a thin jacket,

02:06:17.303 --> 02:06:19.538
and you'd almost certainly
have body armor on.

02:06:23.442 --> 02:06:26.078
Depending on the sand,
sometimes it's soft,

02:06:26.078 --> 02:06:28.647
and it would be incredibly
difficult to move.

02:06:28.647 --> 02:06:31.383
I mean, granted, he's got
no kit on, no body armor,

02:06:31.383 --> 02:06:32.818
but it would be incredibly difficult.

02:06:32.818 --> 02:06:33.986
You're running from side to side

02:06:33.986 --> 02:06:36.055
to evade possible enemy fire,

02:06:36.055 --> 02:06:38.223
especially if that's
coming from behind you.

02:06:38.223 --> 02:06:41.393
You'd be running to positions of cover

02:06:41.393 --> 02:06:42.595
rather than in a straight line.

02:06:46.799 --> 02:06:50.436
It's there to look like a Hind.

02:06:50.436 --> 02:06:53.305
It's actually the Pumas
that have been modified.

02:06:53.305 --> 02:06:54.239
They've been marked up to look like

02:06:54.239 --> 02:06:56.675
real Soviet gunships,
yeah. They were introduced,

02:06:56.675 --> 02:06:59.511
I think, by the Russians in 1972.

02:06:59.511 --> 02:07:02.247
So they definitely would have been in

02:07:02.247 --> 02:07:07.019
the actual Afghan and
Russian war in '79 to '89.

02:07:12.324 --> 02:07:13.926
The biggest myth, really, is,

02:07:13.926 --> 02:07:15.494
I think about desert warfare films,

02:07:15.494 --> 02:07:18.163
is, I think, the hero
journey that one takes.

02:07:18.163 --> 02:07:21.000
So it's, if you look at, say, "Rambo"

02:07:21.000 --> 02:07:24.169
or anything like that, it's
kind of a one-man band.

02:07:24.169 --> 02:07:27.306
One person getting
injected into a theater,

02:07:27.306 --> 02:07:29.141
into a war zone,

02:07:29.141 --> 02:07:32.478
and in reality it's just,
it's wholly not possible.

02:07:35.781 --> 02:07:38.283
That weapon system, .50-cal Dushka,

02:07:38.283 --> 02:07:40.986
it would take it down.
It absolutely would.

02:07:40.986 --> 02:07:43.889
And I'm just slightly unconvinced

02:07:43.889 --> 02:07:47.626
that there would be,
A, enough rounds for it

02:07:47.626 --> 02:07:48.994
for that amount of time.

02:07:48.994 --> 02:07:51.397
Sometimes you can get
one or two rounds off,

02:07:51.397 --> 02:07:53.732
then you have to start
doing your stoppage drills.

02:07:53.732 --> 02:07:55.167
One person could operate that, yeah.

02:07:55.167 --> 02:07:57.836
Would be better with two,
but one person could.

02:07:57.836 --> 02:07:59.738
It's a difficult one because
everything that happens

02:07:59.738 --> 02:08:03.042
is in the realms of
possibility. It really is.

02:08:03.042 --> 02:08:05.911
I would probably give it a 5.

02:08:05.911 --> 02:08:08.981
I think the percentage of
success that he would get

02:08:08.981 --> 02:08:11.917
from doing what he does
would be very, very small.

02:08:11.917 --> 02:08:14.687
Call it in. See if there
are friendlies in the area.

02:08:14.687 --> 02:08:16.155
Lima 2 Charlie.

02:08:18.257 --> 02:08:19.091
Lima 2 Charlie.

02:08:19.091 --> 02:08:21.894
The radio operator has
tried to establish comms

02:08:21.894 --> 02:08:22.895
via the sat phone.

02:08:22.895 --> 02:08:25.431
He's not been successful, but he's not

02:08:25.431 --> 02:08:28.167
give the responder opportunity to respond.

02:08:28.167 --> 02:08:30.502
We've used them before in
Somalia and other places.

02:08:30.502 --> 02:08:32.271
It's just a case of sometimes moving

02:08:32.271 --> 02:08:35.441
to an elevated position
or a different location

02:08:35.441 --> 02:08:37.076
to establish communication.

02:08:37.076 --> 02:08:38.377
They are unreliable.

02:08:42.047 --> 02:08:43.716
Who are they, man?

02:08:43.716 --> 02:08:44.550
I don't know.

02:08:44.550 --> 02:08:46.218
You would have automatically hit the floor

02:08:46.218 --> 02:08:47.953
and gone to ground, and
you would have started

02:08:47.953 --> 02:08:51.256
observing from what we
call your belt buckle,

02:08:51.256 --> 02:08:52.758
from a prone position.

02:08:52.758 --> 02:08:55.094
The mirage effect, obviously
it's a thing. It's there.

02:08:55.094 --> 02:08:57.096
But it wouldn't distort
the images like that,

02:08:57.096 --> 02:08:59.465
and you'd still be able
to make people out.

02:08:59.465 --> 02:09:02.101
Their movement towards
you, it's very relaxed.

02:09:02.101 --> 02:09:03.969
They're not running, they're not shouting.

02:09:03.969 --> 02:09:06.538
You'd be looking for
weapons or weapon systems

02:09:06.538 --> 02:09:07.973
as they start to get closer.

02:09:10.743 --> 02:09:12.411
Form a line!

02:09:12.411 --> 02:09:14.046
They do get into extended line,

02:09:14.046 --> 02:09:17.416
which is good tactically
for this scenario,

02:09:17.416 --> 02:09:20.953
but the posture that they get
into, the hostile posture,

02:09:20.953 --> 02:09:22.921
getting weapons and
getting them ready to go

02:09:22.921 --> 02:09:25.090
into the shoulder, is an escalation

02:09:25.090 --> 02:09:26.592
that you would want to try and avoid.

02:09:26.592 --> 02:09:28.727
See, and they're all M16s.
Some of them have got

02:09:28.727 --> 02:09:30.629
underslung grenade-launcher attachments.

02:09:30.629 --> 02:09:34.166
It's an old American-issued weapon.

02:09:34.166 --> 02:09:35.834
They'll be real weapons with probably

02:09:35.834 --> 02:09:38.070
just magazines in, with no ammunition.

02:09:38.070 --> 02:09:39.238
Soldier: What are you doing?

02:09:40.139 --> 02:09:41.774
Swoff!

02:09:41.774 --> 02:09:42.908
Guys, cover him.

02:09:45.944 --> 02:09:47.679
Weapons should have been down, for sure.

02:09:47.679 --> 02:09:49.982
He should have had one of
them just put his hand up,

02:09:49.982 --> 02:09:53.619
but just posing a more
peaceful kind of approach.

02:09:53.619 --> 02:09:54.353
Maybe saying, like,

02:09:54.353 --> 02:09:57.022
the local language
in terms of a welcome

02:09:57.022 --> 02:09:59.591
would have been the right
course of action to take.

02:09:59.591 --> 02:10:01.126
He walked straight into the arc

02:10:01.126 --> 02:10:03.028
of the line of fire of all the lads.

02:10:03.028 --> 02:10:03.829
You would never do that.

02:10:03.829 --> 02:10:05.964
He would have almost
certainly been killed in that

02:10:05.964 --> 02:10:07.966
if it would've gone ugly.

02:10:07.966 --> 02:10:10.469
Realism, I'd probably give it about

02:10:10.469 --> 02:10:11.670
a 5 or a 6.

02:10:11.670 --> 02:10:13.205
It's not uncommon for people to walk

02:10:13.205 --> 02:10:15.874
into your arc of fire or your line of fire

02:10:15.874 --> 02:10:16.942
and just be unaware of it.

02:10:22.347 --> 02:10:24.516
In this instance, you would try

02:10:24.516 --> 02:10:27.719
and scarper away to
cover in a low profile.

02:10:27.719 --> 02:10:30.823
Going to grounding
straightaway is good drills.

02:10:30.823 --> 02:10:31.890
Try and lower your profile.

02:10:31.890 --> 02:10:34.793
But trying to get into some
form of cover would be ideal.

02:10:34.793 --> 02:10:36.395
I know it's difficult in this scene.

02:10:36.395 --> 02:10:38.997
It's very open, and
there's no built-up areas

02:10:38.997 --> 02:10:39.832
or anything like that.

02:10:39.832 --> 02:10:41.800
But your tactics in this would be to,

02:10:41.800 --> 02:10:43.535
if you have to engage, engage,

02:10:43.535 --> 02:10:44.870
but then try and peel away.

02:10:44.870 --> 02:10:46.705
Yeah, it'd be a worst-case scenario.

02:10:48.006 --> 02:10:50.876
It's a Minimi that the guy's got here,

02:10:50.876 --> 02:10:53.478
which is a key tactical team weapon.

02:10:53.478 --> 02:10:56.014
A belt-fed 5.56 ammunition.

02:10:56.014 --> 02:10:57.482
But just how he's holding it,

02:10:57.482 --> 02:10:59.518
it's up, and then he holds the back stock.

02:10:59.518 --> 02:11:02.054
He's definitely been taught that.

02:11:07.159 --> 02:11:09.661
The drills in this as well,
we call it "the rate of fire."

02:11:09.661 --> 02:11:11.330
The people with the rifles, the M16s,

02:11:11.330 --> 02:11:13.932
are firing single shot,
which for accuracy is great.

02:11:13.932 --> 02:11:15.734
A massive thing in Hollywood is

02:11:15.734 --> 02:11:17.936
our weapons are always on
automatic, fully automatic,

02:11:17.936 --> 02:11:19.504
and it's not really correct.

02:11:19.504 --> 02:11:24.076
Although the drills are good and slick,

02:11:24.076 --> 02:11:25.878
in this you can't get away from the fact

02:11:25.878 --> 02:11:28.247
that it looks like actors holding weapons.

02:11:28.247 --> 02:11:29.448
Probably give it a 4.

02:11:29.448 --> 02:11:31.283
I think the SAS, in
reality, would have done

02:11:31.283 --> 02:11:33.018
things differently. A lot differently.

02:11:33.018 --> 02:11:34.253
I don't think you would have engaged in

02:11:34.253 --> 02:11:35.387
preemptive strike like that,

02:11:35.387 --> 02:11:38.190
unless they were literally
coming towards you and upon you.

02:11:42.127 --> 02:11:46.031
The dog is clearly sniffing
for explosives, IEDs.

02:11:46.031 --> 02:11:49.801
Putting them in the ground is
to identify areas to avoid,

02:11:49.801 --> 02:11:51.803
but they might also be put in the ground

02:11:51.803 --> 02:11:55.574
just to show the allied
forces where they can move.

02:11:55.574 --> 02:11:56.875
But they would sit down, yeah,

02:11:56.875 --> 02:11:57.910
when they've come across something

02:11:57.910 --> 02:11:59.311
that they're not that happy with.

02:11:59.311 --> 02:12:00.445
They're expecting a treat.

02:12:00.445 --> 02:12:01.613
What it's kind of all about for them,

02:12:01.613 --> 02:12:02.447
it's just a game.

02:12:06.318 --> 02:12:10.589
[ringing sound]

02:12:10.589 --> 02:12:11.957
In terms of disorientation,

02:12:11.957 --> 02:12:14.593
like the ringing nose alluding to

02:12:14.593 --> 02:12:17.296
how your ears would be, absolutely, yeah.

02:12:17.296 --> 02:12:19.998
You might not hear anything for minutes.

02:12:19.998 --> 02:12:21.166
Might burst your eardrums.

02:12:21.166 --> 02:12:22.768
I mean, it is realistic, but they just

02:12:22.768 --> 02:12:25.070
seem to recover too quickly from it.

02:12:25.070 --> 02:12:26.939
Could be an old shell,

02:12:26.939 --> 02:12:29.308
an old war shell that they've used.

02:12:29.308 --> 02:12:31.777
Pointing really towards a vehicle,

02:12:31.777 --> 02:12:34.479
like a vehicle IED that they've
kind of detonated there.

02:12:34.479 --> 02:12:35.914
The fact that it's actually on

02:12:35.914 --> 02:12:38.650
what looks to be like a common trail

02:12:38.650 --> 02:12:39.985
for vehicles and stuff

02:12:39.985 --> 02:12:42.254
kind of points to the fact
that it would have been that.

02:12:42.254 --> 02:12:45.023
I think it probably might
have been a greater explosion,

02:12:45.023 --> 02:12:47.526
a bigger explosion, but it would

02:12:47.526 --> 02:12:50.629
blow them back like that, if not worse.

02:12:50.629 --> 02:12:52.297
Canine error would be a factor

02:12:52.297 --> 02:12:54.833
that you would factor into
it, especially if you've

02:12:54.833 --> 02:12:57.202
not kind of run the dog
over that area as well.

02:12:59.938 --> 02:13:02.507
Hey! How did your dog miss that?

02:13:02.507 --> 02:13:04.609
You could've gotten us all killed.

02:13:04.609 --> 02:13:06.778
It must have triggered the
bomb from those houses.

02:13:06.778 --> 02:13:08.880
It's all really good
until after the explosion.

02:13:08.880 --> 02:13:10.816
There's no way that you would be kind of

02:13:10.816 --> 02:13:12.985
up on your feet and calling somebody out

02:13:12.985 --> 02:13:14.886
for what they have or haven't missed.

02:13:14.886 --> 02:13:17.155
You'd be glued to the
floor, you'd be shocked,

02:13:17.155 --> 02:13:20.459
you'd be disorientated, and
you'd just be scared to death.

02:13:20.459 --> 02:13:23.195
There's no way that you would kind of

02:13:23.195 --> 02:13:24.696
respond in that kind of manner.

02:13:24.696 --> 02:13:26.865
And another thing that
you've got to worry about is

02:13:26.865 --> 02:13:29.668
that might just be the first detonation.

02:13:29.668 --> 02:13:32.504
There might be a secondary explosion,

02:13:32.504 --> 02:13:35.474
and I think if somebody has detonated that

02:13:35.474 --> 02:13:38.877
with a command wire,
which is what's happened,

02:13:38.877 --> 02:13:40.712
you would absolutely be expecting

02:13:40.712 --> 02:13:42.714
an ambush off the back of that,

02:13:42.714 --> 02:13:44.583
because you've actually
got people watching.

02:13:44.583 --> 02:13:46.518
I think it's really realistic

02:13:46.518 --> 02:13:48.987
up until what happens after the explosion.

02:13:48.987 --> 02:13:51.690
And for that, I'd probably give it a 7.

02:13:59.364 --> 02:14:01.333
So, the red smoke is to indicate

02:14:01.333 --> 02:14:02.934
the LZ, the landing zone,

02:14:02.934 --> 02:14:04.770
where you're going to
extract somebody from.

02:14:04.770 --> 02:14:06.872
Slightly strange in this clip,

02:14:06.872 --> 02:14:09.241
because it's almost self-explanatory,

02:14:09.241 --> 02:14:11.777
given that you've got
helicopters just above it.

02:14:11.777 --> 02:14:13.245
So it's kind of just thrown out there,

02:14:13.245 --> 02:14:15.680
probably visually just for
the viewers of this film.

02:14:15.680 --> 02:14:17.416
It would have been an incredibly

02:14:17.416 --> 02:14:19.117
aggressive kind of posture now

02:14:19.117 --> 02:14:20.752
from the rescuing forces.

02:14:20.752 --> 02:14:23.455
They wouldn't waste any
time trying to decipher

02:14:23.455 --> 02:14:25.023
whether you've got weapons
or whether you're a friendly.

02:14:25.023 --> 02:14:26.058
There's just no time for it.

02:14:26.058 --> 02:14:27.225
It's just quickly in and out,

02:14:27.225 --> 02:14:30.128
so everybody in between them

02:14:30.128 --> 02:14:34.966
and extracting the US personnel

02:14:34.966 --> 02:14:36.868
would have --

02:14:36.868 --> 02:14:38.103
it wouldn't have been a great experience

02:14:38.103 --> 02:14:39.204
for them whatsoever.

02:14:42.407 --> 02:14:43.942
Let me see your hands!

02:14:45.143 --> 02:14:46.545
The Americans, or anybody,

02:14:46.545 --> 02:14:48.213
will always go back for one of their own.

02:14:48.213 --> 02:14:50.949
You would have had guys
on the ground for weeks

02:14:50.949 --> 02:14:54.786
days before, providing
CTRs, close target recces,

02:14:54.786 --> 02:14:58.890
of this and establishing patterns of life.

02:14:58.890 --> 02:14:59.958
What kind of weapons they've got.

02:14:59.958 --> 02:15:01.326
What time they go to bed.

02:15:01.326 --> 02:15:02.461
Where they're keeping the hostage,

02:15:02.461 --> 02:15:03.662
and if they keep moving him.

02:15:03.662 --> 02:15:05.130
And that would have been getting fed back

02:15:05.130 --> 02:15:09.734
in order to create this rescue attempt.

02:15:09.734 --> 02:15:12.137
So the first phase of it
probably would have been

02:15:12.137 --> 02:15:13.572
the Spectre gunships that you see

02:15:13.572 --> 02:15:15.240
and the Apaches coming in,

02:15:15.240 --> 02:15:17.742
followed by the ground
forces following up behind.

02:15:19.978 --> 02:15:22.180
Soldier: Are you Marcus Luttrell?

02:15:22.180 --> 02:15:23.815
You're safe, Marcus. You're going home.

02:15:23.815 --> 02:15:26.751
What they could do is probably
ask him his service number.

02:15:26.751 --> 02:15:28.253
"What's your service
number? What's your name?

02:15:28.253 --> 02:15:30.856
What's your rank?" and stuff like that,

02:15:30.856 --> 02:15:32.457
which would quickly identify him.

02:15:32.457 --> 02:15:35.026
So it's true to life,
right? It actually happened,

02:15:35.026 --> 02:15:38.697
so I've got to give it a 9 or a 10.

02:15:39.931 --> 02:15:42.834
Trey: Got movement. 12
o'clock, about 300 meters,

02:15:42.834 --> 02:15:44.769
two guys on the roof.

02:15:44.769 --> 02:15:46.304
Ortega: Are they armed?
Trey: I don't know.

02:15:46.304 --> 02:15:48.273
So, these are US Marines.

02:15:48.273 --> 02:15:50.775
There's kind of two sides
to the communication.

02:15:50.775 --> 02:15:52.477
The initial start, where they're saying,

02:15:52.477 --> 02:15:54.546
"Have they got weapons?
Have they got weapons?"

02:15:54.546 --> 02:15:58.884
It's to identify them as
potential enemy targets,

02:15:58.884 --> 02:16:01.953
but also it gives them, in a sense,

02:16:01.953 --> 02:16:04.489
a free pass to engage, if they have to.

02:16:04.489 --> 02:16:07.492
Looking for weapons is
the combat indicator.

02:16:07.492 --> 02:16:08.827
It's a simple f---ing question, Trey.

02:16:08.827 --> 02:16:09.895
Do you see a weapon?
Trey: Negative!

02:16:09.895 --> 02:16:10.962
That's not the question you asked.

02:16:10.962 --> 02:16:11.796
You asked if they were armed.

02:16:11.796 --> 02:16:13.131
The answer to that one
is I don't f---ing know.

02:16:13.131 --> 02:16:14.466
